window.PCP_DOCUMENT = {"title":"Proof of the PCP Theorem","sourceHtml":"<h1 id=\"jp-pcp-定理の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0001\">PCP 定理の証明</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0002\">清水 伸高 (東京科学大学)</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-目次\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0003\">目次</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0004\">序文 ... 5</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0005\">第1章 導入 ... 7</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0006\">1.1 計算量理論の復習 ... 7</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0007\">1.2 検証の計算量 ... 11</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0008\">1.3 PCP 定理の背景: ランダムネスを用いる証明の検証 ... 22</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0009\">第2章 弱い PCP 定理 ... 25</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0010\">2.1 局所的な検証はなぜ可能なのか? ... 25</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0011\">2.2 弱い PCP 定理とその証明 ... 36</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0012\">2.3 二入力回路の割り当てに対する PCP 検証者 ... 43</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0013\">第3章 制約充足問題 ... 47</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0014\">3.1 制約充足問題の定義 ... 47</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0015\">3.2 多重グラフの導入とエクスパンダーグラフ ... 55</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0016\">3.3 制約グラフの定数次数エクスパンダー化 ... 60</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0017\">第4章 PCP 定理の証明 ... 69</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0018\">4.1 ギャップ増幅補題 ... 69</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0019\">4.2 制約グラフのべき乗 ... 70</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0020\">4.3 アルファベット削減 ... 79</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0021\">4.4 ギャップ増幅補題と PCP 定理の証明 ... 84</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0022\">付録 A 付録 ... 91</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0023\">A.1 基本的な確率の不等式 ... 91</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"jp-序文\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0001\">序文</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0002\">このノートは、京都大学大学院理学研究科数学・数理解析専攻数理解析系で開催される集中講義「PCP定理の証明と応用」の講義ノートである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0003\">計算量 (computational complexity) とは、問題を解くために必要な計算リソースの量 (例えば計算時間、記憶領域のサイズ、乱択や量子性の有無や量) を意味し、計算量理論 (computational complexity theory) とはそれぞれの問題の計算量を明らかにするための理論である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0004\">PCP定理とは、判定問題 (YesかNoで答える問題) の検証に要する計算量に関する結果であり、端的に言うと、ある命題が真であると主張する証明が文字列として与えられたとき、その証明を検証するためには、通常、全ての文字を見て確認する必要があるが、PCP定理によれば、その証明の一部だけを見ることで、その命題が真であるか否かを確率的に検証することができるという驚くべき結果である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0005\">例えば、ある実行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>と実ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>に対して線形方程式系<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Ax=b\\)</span>は解を持つ、という命題を考えてみよう。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0006\">この命題が真であるならば実際に解の一つ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>を証明として提示することができるが、その証明が正しいかどうかを検証するためには検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Ax\\)</span>を実際に計算し、その各成分が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>と一致するかを確認する必要がある。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0007\">ところがPCP定理によれば、巧妙に構成された証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を提示することにより、その証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>全ての文字を見ることなく、99%の確率で正しく検証できるのである (ここでは確率的な検証、つまり検証者はランダムネスを用いた検証を行う設定を考える)。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0008\">このように、局所的な情報だけを使って全体の構造を推測できるというPCP定理の性質は、単に理論的に興味深いだけでなく、誤り訂正符号の構成、確率論的手法の脱乱択化、最適化問題の近似率限界の導出など、理論計算機科学において広大な応用を持ち、現在でも計算量理論の中心的な研究対象の一つである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0009\">このような重要性を持つにもかかわらず、PCP定理の証明に包括的に日本語でアクセスできる文献は (筆者が知る限り本資料の執筆時点では) 存在しない。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0010\">本講義ではPCP定理の証明を与えるとともに、その証明に用いられた計算量理論の基本的な概念や技法を解説し、PCP定理の応用についても触れる。</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"jp-第1章-導入\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0011\">第1章 導入</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0012\">この集中講義ではPCP定理と呼ばれる計算量理論の基本的な結果について解説し、その証明を与える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0013\">PCP定理は1998年にAroraとSafra、およびArora、Lund、Motwani、Sudan、Szegedy [AS98, ALMSS98] によって証明された。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0014\">この証明は代数的な手法に基づく誤り訂正符号を技巧的に組合せたものであり、難解なものであったが、その後Dinur [Din07] によってより簡潔な証明が与えられた。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0015\">この講義ではDinur [Din07] による比較的簡単な証明を紹介する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0016\">ちなみにPCP定理の最初の証明やその応用に多大な貢献を果たしたArora, Feige, Goldwasser, Lund, Lovász, Motwani, Safra, Sudan, Szegedyは2001年にゲーデル賞を受賞し、証明を大幅に単純化したDinurは2019年にゲーデル賞を受賞している。</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-11-計算量理論の復習\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0017\">1.1 計算量理論の復習</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0018\">まずは計算量理論のどの教科書にも載っているような基礎的な用語の定義を与える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0019\">これらの用語に明るい読者は1.2.2節から読み始めても良い。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0020\">なお、このノートではアルゴリズムの定義 (チューリング機械の定義) は省略し、アルゴリズムについて述べる際は具体的な計算の手続きを述べる<a href=\"#jp-fn1\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref1\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>1</sup></a>が、主張1.2.12において簡単に触れる。</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0022\">ただし、計算機内では全ての数値は有限桁の二進数で表記されており、その読み書きや演算には少なくとも桁数に比例した計算時間がかかる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0023\">なお、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sqrt{2}\\)</span>といった無理数は本来は有限桁で打ち切った近似値を扱うが、そのような小数はこの講義では扱わず、特に断りのない限りは整数値のみを考える。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0024\">まずは基本的な記号の定義を与える:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0025\">オーダー記法: 二つの関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f,g\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=O(g(n))\\)</span>であるとは、ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して、十分大きな全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)\\le cg(n)\\)</span>が成り立つことをいう。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0026\">また、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=\\Omega(g(n)), f(n)=o(g(n)), f(n)=\\omega(g(n))\\)</span>なども同様に (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)</span>として) 定義する。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0027\">自然数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\([n]=\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\)</span>とする。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0028\">有限集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\sim S\\)</span>と書いたとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span>から一様ランダムに選ばれた元であることを意味する。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0029\">本資料で登場する全ての有限集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_m\\}\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s_1&lt;s_2&lt;\\dots&lt;s_m\\)</span>という順序が暗に定まっているとする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0030\">非空な部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T=\\{t_1,\\dots,t_k\\}\\subseteq S\\)</span>を考える際は常にこの順序に従って<span class=\"math inline\">\\(t_1&lt;\\dots&lt;t_k\\)</span>を仮定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0031\">例えばグラフの頂点集合にも順序が一つ決まっており、無向辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\)</span>を考える際も<em>常に</em><span class=\"math inline\">\\(u&lt;v\\)</span>を仮定する。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0032\">集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span>上のベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in S^n\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(I=\\{i_1,\\dots,i_\\ell\\}\\subseteq[n]\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_I=(x_{i_1},\\dots,x_{i_\\ell}) \\in S^{\\ell}\\)</span>を、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\)</span>への制限とする。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0033\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^*=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>を有限長の二進文字列全体とする。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0034\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>の文字数とする。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0035\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\)</span>を入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x \\in \\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対するアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>の出力とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0036\">ここで、単に「アルゴリズム」と言った場合は決定的アルゴリズムを指し、乱択アルゴリズムについては明示的に言及する。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0037\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>を考える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0038\">十分大きな全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>に対して、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\)</span>を出力するまでにかかる計算ステップ数の最大値が高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>であるとき、アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>の計算量は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>であるという。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0039\">特に、ある (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>に依らない) 定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して計算量が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n^c)\\)</span>で抑えられるアルゴリズムを<em>多項式時間アルゴリズム</em>という。<a href=\"#jp-fn2\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref2\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>2</sup></a></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0040\">計算量理論において慣例的に用いられる記法だが、関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>に関する多項式である、すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>に依存しないある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=O(n^c)\\)</span>が成り立つことを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=n^{O(1)}\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span>と表す。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0041\">例えば多項式時間アルゴリズムとは、十分大きな全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対して、長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>の任意の文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>を受け取ったときの時間計算量が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>で抑えられるアルゴリズムである。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0042\">二つの文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>を入力として受け取るアルゴリズムは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x,y)\\)</span>と表す。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0043\">三つ以上の場合も<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x,y,z)\\)</span>などと表す。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0045\">多項式時間で動くかどうかの議論であれば、多くの古典的な計算モデルは等価である。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0046\">計算量理論で最も基本的な問題群として判定問題と呼ばれる問題群がある。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-111判定問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0047\">定義 1.1.1（判定問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0048\">部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>を<em>判定問題 (または言語)<em>といい、アルゴリズムに与える入力を問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>の</em>インスタンス</em>という。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0049\">また、インスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>であるとき、判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>の<em>Yesインスタンス</em>といい、そうでない場合は<em>Noインスタンス</em>という。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0050\">文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>を、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかの指示関数、すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)=1\\)</span>、そうでなければ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)=0\\)</span>と定義する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0051\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は、任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)=L(x)\\)</span>が成り立つとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>を解くという。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-例-112グラフ連結性判定問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0052\">例 1.1.2（グラフ連結性判定問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0053\">グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>の隣接行列を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A \\in \\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert V\\rvert\\times\\lvert V\\rvert}\\)</span>とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0054\">この行列を長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert^2\\)</span>の二進文字列として表現したものを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{str}(A)\\)</span>とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0055\">すなわち、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{str}(A)\\)</span>の第<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>ビットは、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i=\\lceil k/\\lvert V\\rvert\\rceil+1, j=k\\bmod \\lvert V\\rvert+1\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(i,j)\\)</span>の値として与えられる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0056\">このとき、</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nL = \\{ \\mathsf{str}(A)\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert V\\rvert^2} \\colon \\text{$A$は連結グラフ$G$の隣接行列} \\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0057\">は与えられたグラフが連結であるかどうかを判定する判定問題である。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0058\">この講義では「効率的に解ける」といった場合、多項式時間アルゴリズムによって解けることを指す。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0059\">そのような判定問題の集合をクラスPという。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-113クラスp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0060\">定義 1.1.3（クラスP）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0061\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>は、それを解く多項式時間アルゴリズムが存在するとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>に属するといい、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L \\in \\mathsf{P}\\)</span>と表す。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-114入力のフォーマット\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0062\">注意 1.1.4（入力のフォーマット）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0063\">例1.1.2では入力としてグラフの隣接行列を二進文字列として表現したものを考えたが、一般にグラフの表現方法は他にも隣接リストなどが考えられる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0064\">しかし、例えばグラフを隣接行列で表現するか隣接リストで表現するかは、それぞれのフォーマット間の変換が多項式時間で行えるため、<em>多項式時間で解けるかどうか</em>という点では等価である。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0065\">そのため、厳密には問題を定義する際はその入力のフォーマット (グラフを隣接行列で表現するか、隣接リストで表現するか、など) も指定する必要があるが、フォーマット間の変換が自明に多項式時間で行える場合に限ってはこの講義ではそのようなフォーマットの指定を省略し、例えばグラフ連結性判定問題を「グラフが与えられたときにそれが連結であるかどうかを判定する問題」と表現する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0066\">次に乱択アルゴリズムについて定義する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0067\">端的に言えばアルゴリズムの内部でコイントスを行うものを乱択アルゴリズムという。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0068\">ここでは明示的にランダムシードを受け取るアルゴリズムを乱択アルゴリズムと呼ぶことにする。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-115乱択アルゴリズム\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0069\">定義 1.1.5（乱択アルゴリズム）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0070\">入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>とは別にランダムシード (乱数表) と呼ばれる別の文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>を受け取るアルゴリズムを<em>乱択アルゴリズム</em>といい、ランダムシードであることを強調するために<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x;s)\\)</span>などと表す。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0071\">なお、任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,s\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x;s)\\)</span>は有限時間で停止するとし、その計算量は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span>のみに依存する関数で表せるとする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0072\">このとき、ランダムシード<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>の長さを常に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>の計算量で上から抑える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0073\">すなわち、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>の計算量が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>であるとき、十分大きな全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が読み込む<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>の文字数は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>であるため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in \\{0,1\\}^{T(n)}\\)</span>であると仮定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0074\">しばし、ランダムシード<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>を明記する必要が特にない場合は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\)</span>と表す。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0075\">乱択アルゴリズムのランダムシードに関する確率、期待値、分散を議論する際は記号として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_{A}[\\cdot],\\mathbb{E}_A[\\cdot],\\operatorname{Var}_A[\\cdot]\\)</span>を用いる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0076\">乱択アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>を解くとは、</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_A[A(x)=L(x)]\\geq 2/3\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0077\">が成り立つことをいう。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0078\">また、入力とは別に文字列へのオラクルアクセスを受け取るアルゴリズムを考える。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-116オラクルアルゴリズム\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0079\">定義 1.1.6（オラクルアルゴリズム）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0080\">文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>への<em>オラクルアクセス</em>を持つアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span>とは、計算途中で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の指定された位置の文字を読むことができるアルゴリズムである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0081\">すなわち、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>番目の文字を読む操作を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span>の計算過程中に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log \\lvert\\pi\\rvert)\\)</span>時間で行うことができるアルゴリズムである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0082\">このときのインデックス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>を<em>クエリ</em>と呼ぶ。<a href=\"#jp-fn3\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref3\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>3</sup></a></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0083\">同様に乱択オラクルアルゴリズムについても定義できる。</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0085\">オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span>の各オラクルアクセスのクエリ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>が、それ以前のオラクルアクセスの内容に依存するとき、そのオラクルアルゴリズムを<em>適応的</em>といい、そうでない場合は<em>非適応的</em>という。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0086\">本講義では特に常に非適応的なオラクルアルゴリズムのみを扱う。</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-12-検証の計算量\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0087\">1.2 検証の計算量</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0088\">数学全般における検証とは、ある命題が真であると主張する証明が与えられたとき、その証明が実際にその命題を正しく証明しているかどうかを確認することを意味する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0089\">論文や記述試験の証明の査読や採点をイメージしてもらうとわかりやすいだろう。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0090\">計算量理論では検証やその計算量の議論は重要な研究テーマであり、その検証に要する計算量が議論される。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0091\">より詳細な背景は1.3節を参照されたい。</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-121-効率的な検証とクラスnp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0092\">1.2.1 効率的な検証とクラスNP</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0093\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>と入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>を与えられたとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかを審議したい。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0094\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>を主張する証明が文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>で与えられたとする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0095\">このとき、<em>検証者</em>と呼ばれるアルゴリズムは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を読み込んで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかを判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0096\">この判定を多項式時間で行えるとき、その判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>の集合を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>という。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-121クラスnp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0097\">定義 1.2.1（クラスNP）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0098\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>は、以下を満たす多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>と多項式<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>が存在するとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に属するという:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0099\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>は入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>を受け取り、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>を出力する。</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0100\">もし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>ならば、ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^{p(\\lvert x\\rvert)}\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=1\\)</span>となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0101\">このとき<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>は入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,\\pi)\\)</span>を<em>受理する</em>という。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0102\">もし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\notin L\\)</span>ならば、全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^{p(\\lvert x\\rvert)}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=0\\)</span>となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0103\">このとき<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>は入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,\\pi)\\)</span>を<em>拒否する</em>という。</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0104\">また、このようなアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>を<em>NP検証者</em>といい、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を<em>NP証明</em>という。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-122クラスpとnpの関係\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0105\">注意 1.2.2（クラスPとNPの関係）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0106\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>に属するならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0107\">実際、受け取った<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)\\)</span>を計算してそれを出力する検証者を考えばよい。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0108\">すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\subseteq\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0109\">一方、逆側の包含関係<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\subseteq\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>が成り立つかどうかはP vs NP問題と呼ばれる計算量理論における最も重要な未解決問題であり、多くの研究者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\subseteq\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>が成り立たないと信じている。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-例-123合成数判定問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0110\">例 1.2.3（合成数判定問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0111\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L=\\{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\colon x\\text{は合成数}\\}\\)</span>を考える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0112\">このとき、検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を読み込んで、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\notin\\{1,x\\}\\)</span>かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>を割り切るかどうかを判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0113\">もしも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>である場合、合成数なので非自明な約数を証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>として与えれば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=1\\)</span>となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0114\">そうでない場合、非自明な約数は存在しないため必ず<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=0\\)</span>となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0115\">このアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>は入力長 (つまり数値の二進表現したときのビット長) に関する多項式時間で動作するため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>はNPに属する。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-例-124グラフ彩色問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0116\">例 1.2.4（グラフ彩色問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0117\">自然数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\ge 2\\)</span>とグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>に対し、関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\colon V\\to[k]\\)</span>が全ての辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c(u)\\neq c(v)\\)</span>を満たすとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色といい、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色が存在するようなグラフは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色可能であるという。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0118\">任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\ge 2\\)</span>に対し、判定問題</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nL=\\{G\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\colon G\\text{は}k\\text{-彩色可能}\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0119\">はNPに属する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0120\">検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を読み込んで、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色であるかどうかを判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0121\">もしも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\in L\\)</span>である場合、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色可能であるため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色の証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を与えれば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(G,\\pi)=1\\)</span>となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0122\">そうでない場合、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色可能でないため必ず<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(G,\\pi)=0\\)</span>となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0123\">このアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>は多項式時間で動作するため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>はNPに属する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0124\">例1.2.3では、与えられた数字が合成数であることの証拠として非自明な約数を与えることで、検証者が多項式時間で検証できることを確認した。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0125\">では、与えられた数が素数であることの証拠は提示できるだろうか？</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0126\">興味深いことに、実はこれは可能である。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習-1素数判定問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0127\">演習 1（素数判定問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0128\">自然数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対しその二進表記を<span class=\"math inline\">\\((n)_2\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>と表す。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0129\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PRIMES}=\\{(a)_2\\in\\mathbb{N}\\colon a\\text{は素数}\\}\\)</span>を考える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0130\">この問題は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>に属することが知られている [AKS04] が、その複雑なアルゴリズムを用いずに初等的に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PRIMES}\\in \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>を示したい。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0131\">そのために、以下の事実を用いる:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0132\">任意の自然数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots \\pmod a\\)</span>は周期的である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0133\">さらに、以下が成り立つ:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0134\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>が素数であるならば、ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots \\pmod a\\)</span>の周期が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span>である。<a href=\"#jp-fn4\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref4\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>4</sup></a></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0135\">一方、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>が素数でないならば、全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots \\pmod a\\)</span>の周期は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span>未満である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0136\">特に、その周期<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span>を割り切る。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0138\">これらの事実を用いて、以下の小問に答えよ。</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0139\">次の検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_1\\)</span>を考える: 入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots,\\gamma^{a-2} \\pmod a\\)</span>を全て検証し、これら全て相異なるかどうかを判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0140\">この検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_1\\)</span>が多項式時間アルゴリズム<em>でない</em>理由を簡潔に説明せよ。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0141\">入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>が素数であることの証明として、原始元<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span>の素因数分解<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1=p_1^{\\alpha_1}\\cdots p_k^{\\alpha_k}\\)</span>および各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_i\\)</span>が素数であることの証明を再帰的に与える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0142\">この証明を用いて、検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_2\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>が素数であるかどうかを多項式時間で判定できることを示せ。</span></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"jp-122-局所的な検証とクラスpcp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0143\">1.2.2 局所的な検証とクラスPCP</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0144\">一般に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかの検証では、証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の全ての文字を読む必要がある。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0145\">しかし、証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>のうちの一部の文字を読むだけで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかを<em>確率的に</em>判定できる場合がある。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0146\">そのような性質を持つ証明を<em>確率的検証可能な証明</em> (Probabilistically Checkable Proof, PCP) という。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-125確率的検証可能な証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0147\">定義 1.2.5（確率的検証可能な証明）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0148\">二つの関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r,q\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span>を以下の性質を持つ判定集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>の集合とする: ある多項式時間オラクル乱択アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>が存在して、任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対し、</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0149\">もし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>ならば、ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>が存在して、(<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>の乱択に関して) 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=1\\)</span>となる (<em>完全性</em>)。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0150\">もし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\notin L\\)</span>ならば、全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対して、(<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>の乱択に関して) 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span>以上で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=0\\)</span>となる (<em>健全性</em>)。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0151\">さらに、入力長が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n=\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span>のとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)\\)</span>はオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>のうち高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(q(n))\\)</span>個の文字を読み、そのランダムシード長は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(r(n))\\)</span>で抑えられる。</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0152\">このようなオラクル乱択アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>を<em>PCP検証者</em>といい、証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を<em>PCP</em>という。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0153\">学術雑誌の査読を例に考えてみよう。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0154\">入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>は査読している論文の主張であり、その証明を表す文字列が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>で与えられている。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0155\">PCP検証者とは、主張が正しいかどうかを証明の中の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>個の文字を読むだけで確率的に判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0156\">一般的な感覚で考えると<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\ll \\lvert\\pi\\rvert\\)</span>ならば、そのようなことは不可能であるように思える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0157\">ところが、例えば手料理における味見では、調理中の料理の味を、そのランダムな部分を少し食べることで判定するという行為であり、これはある意味でPCP検証者のようなものである。</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/PCPverifier.png\" alt=\"PCP検証者のイメージ図\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0158\">図 1.1: PCP検証者のイメージ図。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-126pcpの長さ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0159\">注意 1.2.6（PCPの長さ）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0160\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span>の証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の長さは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)2^{r(n)}\\)</span>で抑えられる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0161\">各ランダムシード<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s \\in \\{0,1\\}^{r(n)}\\)</span>に対して検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>のうち高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)\\)</span>個の文字を読むため、全てのランダムシードを列挙すると、アクセスされる可能性のある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の文字数は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)2^{r(n)}\\)</span>で抑えられる。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0162\">一般にランダムシード長<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)\\)</span>と読み込む文字数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)\\)</span>が小さいほど良いPCP検証者であると考えられる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0163\">PCP検証者の構成は非常に難しい。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0164\">例えば例1.2.4のグラフ彩色問題に対する次の検証者を考えてみよう:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0165\">入力としてグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>と証明として関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon V\\to[k]\\)</span>を受け取り、この関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が実際に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色であるかどうかを判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0166\">検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>をランダムに一つ選び、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(u)\\neq \\pi(v)\\)</span>であるかどうかによって判定する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0167\">この検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>のうち高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)=O(1)\\)</span>個の文字を読み、そのランダムシード長は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)=O(\\log n)\\)</span>で抑えられる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0168\">しかし、この検証者は健全性の条件を満たさない。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0169\">実際、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色可能でない場合、どのような関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon V \\to [k]\\)</span>を与えても、少なくとも一つの辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(u)=\\pi(v)\\)</span>となるが、検証者がこのような辺を引き当てる確率は最悪の場合、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>となるからである。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0170\">PCP定理とは、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span>ビットのランダムネスと<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスによって、任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に属する判定問題のPCP検証が可能であることを保証する定理である。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-127pcp定理\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0171\">定理 1.2.7（PCP定理）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0172\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)=\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0173\">例えばグラフ彩色問題は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に属する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0174\">PCP定理によると、証明のうち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>個の文字を読むだけで確率的に検証できることがわかる。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-128片側の包含関係\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0175\">注意 1.2.8（片側の包含関係）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0176\">PCP定理において、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)\\subseteq \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>は容易に示すことができる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0177\">実際、注意1.2.6により、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(r(n))\\)</span>ビットのランダムネスと<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(q(n))\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行うPCP検証者の証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の長さは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(q(n))2^{O(r(n))} = n^{O(1)}\\)</span>で抑えられる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0178\">また、PCP検証者のランダムビットの長さが<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span>なので、全てのありうる<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{O(r(n))}=n^{O(1)}\\)</span>個のランダムシードを列挙し、それら全てに対してPCP検証者を適用し、その出力値の多数決をとることで、入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかを多項式時間でNP検証できる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0179\">PCP定理の証明の本質的な難しさは逆側の包含関係<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\subseteq \\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)\\)</span>の証明にある。</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-12-np完全性とcook-levinの定理\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0001\">1.2 NP完全性とCook-Levinの定理</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0002\">クラス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に属する全ての問題に対しそれ以上に難しいという性質を<strong>NP困難性</strong>という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0003\">そしてNPに属する問題がNP困難であるとき, その問題は<strong>NP完全</strong>であるという.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0004\">ここでは判定問題<a href=\"#jp-fn5\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref5\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>5</sup></a>に対してNP困難性とNP完全性の定義を与える.</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0006\">その際は最適化問題Xを解く多項式時間アルゴリズムを用いると全てのNPに属する問題が多項式時間で解けるということを意味する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-129np完全性\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0007\">定義 1.2.9（NP完全性）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0008\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>は, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に対して以下を満たす決定的多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が存在するとき, <strong>NP完全</strong>であるという (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>に依存してよい):</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0009\">文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>を入力として受け取ったアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>の出力を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0010\">このとき, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L&#39;\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\in L\\)</span>は同値である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0011\">また, このようなアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>への<strong>カープ帰着</strong>という.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注釈-1210np以上に難しいの意味\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0012\">注釈 1.2.10（「NP以上に難しい」の意味）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0013\">NP完全な判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>を解く多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0\\)</span>が存在するならば, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\in \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>を解く多項式時間アルゴリズムが存在する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0014\">実際, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>へのカープ帰着を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>とすると, 与えられた<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>かどうかはまず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y=A(x)\\)</span>を計算し, その後<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0(y)\\)</span>を計算することで判定できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0015\">この意味で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>以上に難しいといえる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0016\">しかし, 二つの問題の困難性を比較する際は必ずしもカープ帰着に拘る必要はない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0017\">実際, 上述のアルゴリズムは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>を解くアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0\\)</span>を一度だけ用いてしかもその出力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0(y)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>がそのまま<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L&#39;\\)</span>かどうかの答えと一致しているが, 「<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>が解けたら<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>も解ける」ということを示すのであれば複数の入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_1,\\dots,y_m\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0(y_1),\\dots,A_0(y_m)\\)</span>を計算してもよいし, それらの出力を組み合わせて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L&#39;\\)</span>かどうかを判定してもよい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0018\">このような自由度の高い操作を許しつつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0\\)</span>が多項式時間で動くならば全体も多項式時間で動くことが保証されるような帰着を<strong>チューリング帰着</strong>という.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0019\">具体的にカープ帰着含めNP完全性の枠組みが確立されたのは[Kar72]であるが, 実はそれ以前の[Coo71]は, カープ帰着とは少し異なる帰着を用いて<strong>回路充足可能性判定問題</strong>と呼ばれる問題がNP完全であることを示していた.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0020\">回路充足可能性判定問題は論理回路に関する問題であり一見するとグラフなどとは関係なさそうに見えるが, [Kar72]は, カープ帰着の下でも[Coo71]の証明は成立することを利用して最大クリーク問題, ハミルトン閉路問題, 彩色数の計算, ナップザック問題といった様々な自然な組合せ最適化問題のNP困難性を証明していった.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0021\">[Kar72]がNP完全性を示したこれらの問題は現在では<strong>Karpの21のNP完全問題</strong> (Karp's 21 NP-complete problems)と呼ばれており, この成果によってCookは1982年とKarpは1985年にそれぞれチューリング賞を受賞している.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0022\">また, 実は[Lev73]も同様の結果を独立に示していたことが判明し(論文の発行当時は冷戦下であったことが災いして違いの認識が遅れてしまった), Levinは2012年にクヌース賞を受賞している.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0023\">このことから以下に定める回路充足可能性判定問題のNP完全性を示す定理を<strong>Cook-Levinの定理</strong>といい, この定理は全てのNP完全性の理論の基礎となる計算量理論における最も重要な定理の一つである.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-1211cook-levinの定理\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0024\">定理 1.2.11（Cook-Levinの定理）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0025\">論理回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>が存在するかどうかを判定する問題を<strong>回路充足可能性判定問題</strong>(Circuit SAT)という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0026\">回路充足可能性判定問題はNP完全である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0027\">Cook-Levinの定理の証明はクラスNPの検証者を非決定的チューリング機械で模倣し, その機械を回路として表現することによって与えられる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0028\">すなわち, 回路による計算はチューリング機械を模倣できるという性質が完全性において肝要な性質であり, 以下のように述べられる:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-1212チューリング機械を模倣する回路\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0029\">主張 1.2.12（チューリング機械を模倣する回路）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0030\">任意の多項式時間チューリング機械<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span>に対して, ある回路族<span class=\"math inline\">\\((C_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\)</span>が存在して以下が成り立つ:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0031\">全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_n\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>個のゲート(AND,OR,NOT)を含む.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0032\">なお, ここでは全ての素子は二つの入力を受け取り一つの出力を持つ.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0033\">任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>を受理することと<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_n(x)=1\\)</span>は同値.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0034\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_n\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>時間で構成できる.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0035\">主張 1.2.12を証明するにはチューリング機械の厳密な定義なども必要であり講義では詳しくは扱わないが, その概要を以下に説明する.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/Turing.png\" alt=\"チューリング機械の一例。各状態において、ヘッドの指し示す記憶テープの値のラベルがついた有効辺に沿って遷移し、そのヘッドの位置にある記憶テープの値を必要があれば書き換え、最後にヘッドの位置を一つ右、一つ左のいずれかに移動するかそのままにする。\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0036\">図 1.2: チューリング機械の一例.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0037\">各状態において, ヘッドの指し示す記憶テープの値のラベルがついた有効辺に沿って遷移し, そのヘッドの位置にある記憶テープの値を必要があれば書き換え, 最後にヘッドの位置を一つ右, 一つ左のいずれかに移動するかそのままにする.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明の概要\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0038\">証明の概要.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0039\">チューリング機械とは, 無限の長さの<strong>記憶テープ</strong>を備えた有限状態機械である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0040\"><strong>記憶テープ</strong>とは無限の長さの記号列であり, 入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>に対してまずは最初の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span>文字は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>が記載されており, それ以外の文字は全て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bot\\)</span>(空白であることを表す記号)である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0041\">また, <strong>ヘッド</strong>と呼ばれるポインタがあり, これは現在は記憶テープのどの位置の文字を読み書きするかを表す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0042\">各ステップにおいて, 現在の状態とヘッドが指し示す文字を参照し, 次の状態, 現在のヘッドに書き込む内容, ヘッドの移動方向(左右もしくはそのまま)を決める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0043\">さらに機械には二つの相異なる二つの特別な状態<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{accept}}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{reject}}\\)</span>があり, これらに遷移すると計算が終了する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0044\">終了時の状態に応じてチューリング機械の出力(acceptまたはreject)が決まる (図 1.2を参照).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0045\">計算が終了するまでに要したステップ数をこのチューリング機械の**(時間)計算量**という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0046\">チューリング機械<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span>が多項式時間であったとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0047\">このとき, 任意の十分大きな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span>の計算の任意の時点での記憶テープの内容(<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bot\\)</span>以外の文字の個数)は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>文字で表現できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0048\">また, ヘッドの指し示す位置は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span>ビットで表現でき, 状態の個数は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0049\">従って, 任意の時点でのチューリング機械の, 記憶テープの内容も含めた状態(便宜上これを計算状態と呼ぶことにする)は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>ビットで表現できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0050\">また, 記憶テープの書き換えは高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>ビットの書き換えであり, ヘッド位置の変更も加算と減算で計算できるため, チューリング機械の計算の各ステップもまた小さいサイズの回路によって模倣できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0051\">すなわち, 現在の計算状態を入力として受け取り, 計算状態の第<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>番目のビットを出力する回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_i\\)</span>を用意してこれを全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[n^{O(1)}]\\)</span>に対して並べることによって, 回路として表現できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0052\">これを全てのステップについて並べれば, チューリング機械<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span>を模倣する多項式サイズの回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> (すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M(x)\\)</span>の最終状態が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{accept}}\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{reject}}\\)</span>であるかどうかを判定する回路)が得られる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0053\">これを用いてCook-Levinの定理の証明を与える.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-1211の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0054\">定理 1.2.11の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0055\">NPの定義より, 任意の判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に対して, 多項式時間のNP検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x;\\pi)\\)</span>が存在する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0056\">主張 1.2.12より, このNP検証者を模倣する回路族<span class=\"math inline\">\\((C_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\)</span>が存在する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0057\">特に, 入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>が与えられたとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x;\\cdot)\\)</span>というチューリング機械を模倣する回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_x\\colon\\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert\\pi\\rvert}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>が多項式時間で構成できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0058\">さらにこれが<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>から回路充足可能性判定問題へのカープ帰着になっていることを確認すれば証明は完了する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0059\">実際, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>がYesインスタンスであるならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x;\\pi)=1\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が存在することから, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_x\\)</span>は充足可能である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0060\">一方で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>がNoインスタンスであるならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_x\\)</span>は充足可能でない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0061\">従って<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\mapsto C_x\\)</span>は確かに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>から回路充足可能性判定問題へのカープ帰着になっている.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0062\">Cook-Levinの定理を用いると, 以下に示す二次方程式系の充足可能性判定問題がNP完全であることが示せる:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-1213二次方程式系の充足可能性判定問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0063\">定理 1.2.13（二次方程式系の充足可能性判定問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0064\">判定問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{QuadEq}\\)</span>を以下のように定義する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0065\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span>個の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>変数二次関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1,\\dots,f_m\\colon \\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>が与えられる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0066\">このとき, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1(x)=\\dots=f_m(x)=0\\)</span>を満たすかどうかを判定せよ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0067\">特に, 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[m]\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_i(x_1,\\dots,x_n)\\)</span>の値が高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>個の変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{i_1},\\dots,x_{i_k}\\)</span>の値のみに依存するようなとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{QuadEq}\\)</span>と呼ぶ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0068\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>はNP完全である.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0069\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0070\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>はNPに属する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0071\">実際, NP証拠として連立方程式の解<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を与え, それが実際に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1(x)=\\dots=f_m(x)=0\\)</span>を満たすかどうかは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>時間で判定できる (入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1,\\dots,f_m\\)</span>が与えているため, これを二進文字列として表現したときの長さは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>以上である).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0072\">次に, 回路充足可能性判定問題から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>へのカープ帰着を構成する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0073\">回路充足可能性判定問題の入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>が与えられたとき, これらの変数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0074\">各ゲートに対し, そのゲートに対応する変数を新たに用意し, これらを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{n+1},\\dots,x_{n+s}\\)</span>とする (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>は回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>に含まれる入力以外のゲートの個数).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0075\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[s]\\)</span>に対し, 変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{n+k}\\)</span>に対応するゲート<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span>の入力に対応する変数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i,x_j\\)</span>とし (もしもゲートがnotゲートであったならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span>のみを考える), さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j &lt; n+k\\)</span>を満たすとする (こうなるように, 入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>に近い順にゲートの番号づけを行う).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0076\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\colon \\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>を以下のように定義する: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[s]\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_k(x) = \\begin{cases}\nx_k - x_i x_j &amp; \\text{if ゲート$c$がANDゲート}, \\\\\nx_k - (x_i + x_j - x_i x_j) &amp; \\text{if ゲート$c$がORゲート}, \\\\\nx_k - (1-x_i) &amp; \\text{if ゲート$c$がNOTゲート}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0077\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(k=s+1\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_{s+1}(x) = 1 - x_{n+s}\\)</span>と定義する (図 1.3を参照).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0078\">このとき, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\)</span>は高々三つの変数にのみ依存するため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in[s+1]}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンスとなり, 回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>が入力として与えられたとき多項式時間で構成できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0079\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(x)=0\\)</span>であるという制約は, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\le s\\)</span>においては<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>番目のゲートの出力が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_k\\)</span>であることを強制する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0080\">従って全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[s]\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(x)=0\\)</span>であるならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{n+s}\\)</span>は入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)</span>に対する回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>の出力に一致する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0081\">最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_{s+1}(x)=0\\iff x_{n+s}=1\\)</span>であるとき, その入力が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x_1,\\dots,x_n)=1\\)</span>であることを意味する.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/circuit_to_3-QUADEQ.png\" alt=\"回路充足可能性判定問題から3-QuadEqへのカープ帰着の例。左辺と右辺の差が各f_kとなる。\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0082\">図 1.3: 回路充足可能性判定問題から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>へのカープ帰着の例.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0083\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\text{左辺})-(\\text{右辺})\\)</span>が各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\)</span>となる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0084\">最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>が回路充足可能性判定問題のYesインスタンスであることと<span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in[s+1]}\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>のYesインスタンスであることが同値であることを示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0085\">識別のため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>の入力は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>とし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\)</span>の入力は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n+s}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0086\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>がYesインスタンスであるとき, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0087\">出力値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)\\)</span>を計算するときの途中の各ゲートの出力値をそのまま対応する変数に割り当てることによって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>を構成すると, 確かに全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(\\overline{x})=0\\)</span>を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0088\">一方, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)\\)</span>がYesインスタンスであるとき, 最初の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x=(\\overline{x}_1,\\dots,\\overline{x}_{n})\\)</span>とすれば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0089\">従って, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\mapsto (f_k)_{k\\in[s+1]}\\)</span>は確かに回路充足可能性判定問題から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>へのカープ帰着になっている.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0090\">Cook-Levinの定理を用いると[Kar72]のように, 一見すると回路と全く関係がない様々な問題に対してNP完全性を示すことができる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0091\">例えば, 3彩色問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3COL}\\)</span>はNP完全である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0092\">PCP定理を証明するには, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>に対して所望のPCP検証者を構成すれば良いが, 実際には一つのNP完全問題(例えば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{QuadEq}\\)</span>や<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3COL}\\)</span>)を固定してそれに対するPCP検証者を構成すれば良いことがわかる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-1214二次方程式系に対するpcp検証者\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0093\">定理 1.2.14（二次方程式系に対するPCP検証者）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0094\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\in \\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習問題-2\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0095\">演習問題 2</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0096\">定理 1.2.14, 定理 1.2.13を仮定して, PCP定理(定理 1.2.7)を証明せよ.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-13-pcp定理の背景-ランダムネスを用いる証明の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0097\">1.3 PCP定理の背景: ランダムネスを用いる証明の検証</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0098\">PCP定理は近似アルゴリズム設計の困難性という, 組合せ最適化の分野への著しい応用を持つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0099\">しかしながら元々は計算量理論における「証明の検証」の概念の捉え方の変容が背景にある.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0100\">この節の内容のほとんどは[ODo]によるものであるため, 詳細はそちらを参照されたい.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0101\">定義 1.2.1は, よく知られるように正しいとされる主張に対してはそれを確定するに足る証拠が存在し, さらにその検証もまた効率的(多項式時間)に行えるという性質を捉えたものである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0102\">例えば「グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は3彩色可能である」という主張は実際に3彩色を証明として提示できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0103\">一方で「グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は3彩色可能でない」という主張はそのような証明を提示することができるだろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0104\">計算量理論の重要予想<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\ne\\mathsf{coNP}\\)</span>によれば, このような証明を提示することができないとされているがその証明は与えられていない.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0105\">そのような中, <strong>対話型証明</strong>, <strong>ゼロ知識証明</strong>, <strong>Arthur-Merlin証明</strong>といった新しい証明の概念が独立同時期に複数の研究グループによって提案された [GMR85, Bab85].</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0106\">対話型証明は証明者が証明を提示して検証者がそれを読み取るという1ラウンドのプロセスを多項式個のラウンドに拡張し, さらに乱択を用いて確率的な証明の検証を行うというものである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0107\">直感的には学術雑誌の査読と同様の形態で, 論文の著者(証明者)と査読者(検証者)の間で質問とその返答を繰り返して最終的に論文の正しさを確認するというものであるが, 検証者はコイントスを行うことが許されており, 実際に証明が正しい場合は高確率で受理され, 間違っている場合は高確率で拒否される.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0108\">多項式時間アルゴリズムの検証者が無限の計算能力を持つ証明者の多項式ラウンドの会話によって確率的に検証できる判定問題のクラスを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{IP}\\)</span>という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0109\">ここで証明者とは関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\colon \\{0,1\\}^*\\to\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>であり, 検証者は質問<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\operatorname{poly}(n)}\\)</span>を証明者に送り, 証明者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P(q)\\)</span>を返す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0110\">ただし関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\mapsto P(q)\\)</span>の計算時間は多項式時間でなくてもよい.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0111\">なお, コイントスを行わない決定的な検証を考える場合はNP検証と同等であることが知られているため, 対話型証明とNP証明のギャップは本質的にはランダムネスに依拠することになる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0112\">従って対話型証明は当初, 証明能力をそれほど大きくしないと考えられており, 前述の論文は[ODo]によれば, 理論計算機科学ではなかなか受け入れられなかったようである.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0113\">この状況の変化の兆しが見えたのは[GMW86]による<strong>グラフ非同型性判定問題(GNI)<strong>に対するゼロ知識証明の構築である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0114\">この問題は, 二つのグラフの組<span class=\"math inline\">\\((G_1,G_2)\\)</span>が</strong>非同型</strong>ならばYesインスタンスであり, 同型ならばNoインスタンスであるとする問題である<a href=\"#jp-fn6\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref6\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>6</sup></a>.</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0116\">「二つのグラフは同型である」という主張は実際に同型写像を持ってくれば検証できるが, 「二つのグラフは非同型である」という不可能性の証明はどのようにすれば良いのだろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0117\">以下に証明の直感的な説明を与える:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0118\">盲目の人に色の概念の証明をすることを考える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0119\">具体的には二つのくつ下1, くつ下2があり, これらの色が異なることを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>さんという盲目の人に納得してもらうためにはどのようにすれば良いか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0120\">まず, 両方のくつ下の色を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>さんに渡す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0121\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>さんは証明者に見えないところでコイントスを行い, どちらか一方のくつ下をランダムに選び, そのくつ下を証明者に見せてそのくつ下の番号を質問する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0122\">証明者はそのくつ下の番号を答え, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>さんは事前に聞いた説明と合致するかどうかを検証する.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0123\">もしも両方のくつ下が同じ色だとすれば, 証明者は区別できないので当てずっぽうをするしかない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0124\">この場合の正解確率は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/2\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0125\">もしも両方のくつ下が異なる色だとすれば, 証明者は色によってくつ下の番号を当てることができる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0126\">この場合の正解確率は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0127\">この検証を複数回繰り返せば, 同じ色であった場合の正解確率を限りなく<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>まで近づけることができる.<a href=\"#jp-fn7\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref7\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>7</sup></a></span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0129\">このような証明を<strong>ゼロ知識証明</strong>という.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0130\">このことは対話によって証明の検証能力が非自明に向上することを示唆するが, 実はさらなる向上が可能であり, 実は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PSPACE}\\)</span>と呼ばれる問題クラス(多項式ビットの記憶領域を用いて解ける判定問題のクラス)は全て対話証明可能であることが[Sha02, LFKN02]によって示され, 対話型証明が可能な判定問題の特徴づけが与えられた (理論計算機科学では通常, 論文の著者はアルファベット順に名前を並べるが, Lundらの論文に関してはその慣例を破っている点が特徴的である).</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-131\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0131\">定理 1.3.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0132\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{IP}=\\mathsf{PSPACE}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0133\">上記の定理は対話型証明の特徴づけを与えており, 特に検証にランダムネスを付与するとその検証能力が飛躍的に高まることを示唆している.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0134\">そこで, この定理を「スケールダウン」することによって, クラス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>を特徴づけられないだろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0135\">具体的には, ランダムネスを持つものの能力が制限された検証者によって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>を特徴づけることは可能であろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0136\">(能力が制限されていない時はクラス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{IP}\\)</span>と一致する).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0137\">クラス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}\\)</span>はこのような背景の下で考えられており, 具体的には検証者がアクセスできる証明の文字数を制限することによって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>が特徴づけられる, という結果がPCP定理である.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"jp-第2章-弱いpcp定理\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0001\">第2章 弱いPCP定理</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0002\">この章はPCP検証者における「少ないクエリ回数」という要件がなぜ達成できるかについて説明することを目的とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0003\">そのため, まずはPCP定理の要件であるランダムビットの短さはひとまず無視した以下の形の「弱いPCP定理」を証明する: 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{NP}\\)</span>に属する判定問題が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>クエリかつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span>ビットのランダムネスを用いて確率的に検証可能である.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-21-局所的な検証はなぜ可能なのか\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0004\">2.1 局所的な検証はなぜ可能なのか?</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0005\">PCP検証者は, オラクルアクセスとして与えられた証明のうち, 定数文字のみを見てその証明がちゃんと証明たりうるかを判定しなければならない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0006\">ここでは, 定数個の文字のみを見るという性質を検証者の局所性と呼ぶことにする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0007\">一般的な感覚として, ある主張が成り立つことを示す証明を検証する際, <em>冗長に表現で記述しない限り</em>その証明を全て読み込まなければ検証できないと思われる (全ての文字に本質的な意味があるならば全てを確認しなければならないはずであろう).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0008\">では逆に, 証明を冗長に表現することで局所的な検証を可能にすることはできるだろうか?</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-211-線形方程式に対する局所的な検証12\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0009\">2.1.1 線形方程式に対する局所的な検証(1/2)</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0010\">興味深いことに, 非常に冗長性が大きい記述で証明が与えられたならば局所検証が可能であることを, 線形方程式の検証を例に説明する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-211線形方程式\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0011\">定義 2.1.1（線形方程式）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0012\">有限体<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>上の行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n}\\)</span>とベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nMy=z\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0013\">を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在するかどうかを判定する問題を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0014\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathbb{F}\\rvert=q\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(m,n\\)</span>とは独立な定数であるとする (従って有限体上の演算は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>時間で計算できると仮定する).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0015\">愚直な検証者として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を証拠として受け取り, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を確認することで検証を行うことを考える (実際の計算機では全てを二進文字列で表現するため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>の元は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lceil\\log_2\\lvert\\mathbb{F}\\rvert\\rceil=O(1)\\)</span>ビットで表現されている).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0016\">明らかにこの検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\)</span>を計算するために<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>の全ての成分 (すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span>ビット) を読み込む必要があるため, オラクルアクセスの回数が大きくなり局所性を持たない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0017\">もちろん, この判定問題は標準的な線型方程式の解の存在性判定であるため, 掃き出し法を用いれば証拠へのオラクルアクセスなしでも多項式時間で解ける.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0018\">しかしここではあえて, 局所的な検証の構成例を与えるという目的で取り扱う.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0019\">重要な性質として, 二つのベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a,b\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対する次の性質を考える (証明は演習3):</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-linear-equation-property\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n}\\left[r^\\top a\\ne r^\\top b\\right]=\\begin{cases}0&amp;\\text{if }a=b,\\\\1-\\frac1q&amp;\\text{if }a\\ne b.\\end{cases}\\tag{2.1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0020\">さて, 式 (2.1) の性質を用いると一様ランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-random-inner-product\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nr^\\top My=r^\\top z\\tag{2.2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0021\">かどうかを確認することで, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>かどうかを確率的に検証できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0022\">実際, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>ならば確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で検証者は受理し,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0023\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\ne z\\)</span>ならば確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/q\\)</span>で検証者は拒否する (何度も繰り返せばこの拒否率を任意に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>に近い定数まで近づけることができる).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0024\">ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span>は入力として与えられているため式 (2.2) の右辺は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span>時間で計算できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0025\">一方で, ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>は証拠として与えられるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top My\\)</span>の計算は愚直に考えると<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(mn)\\)</span>時間かかる上に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>の全ての成分を読み込む必要がある.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0026\">ところが, 全てのベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y^\\top w\\in\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>を連結して得られる長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>の文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\((y^\\top w)_{w\\in\\mathbb{F}^n}\\)</span>を証拠<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>として与えることにより,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0027\">自身で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top M\\)</span>を計算した後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top My=y^\\top(M^\\top r)\\)</span>の値を<em>一文字の証拠の読み込み</em>で計算できるのである.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習3ランダムなベクトルとの内積\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0028\">演習3（ランダムなベクトルとの内積）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0029\">式 (2.1) を証明せよ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0030\">このように, ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を, それを係数とする線形関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\mapsto y^\\top w\\)</span>の<em>真理値表(全ての点での評価値を並べた文字列)として冗長に表現する</em>ことで, 証拠の読み込み回数を定数に抑えるというのが基本的なアイデアである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0031\">このように文字列を冗長に表現する手法を総称して<em>誤り訂正符号</em>という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0032\">本講義では, <em>アダマール符号</em>と呼ばれる以下の誤り訂正符号を用いる:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-212アダマール符号\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0033\">定義 2.1.2（アダマール符号）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0034\">有限体<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>上のベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して, 長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>の文字列</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)=(y^\\top w)_{w\\in\\mathbb{F}^n}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0035\">を考える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0036\">集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n=\\{\\operatorname{Had}_n(y):y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\}\\)</span>を<em>アダマール符号</em>といい,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0037\">その元を<em>アダマール符号の符号語</em>という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0038\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>を*<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>をアダマール符号で符号化した文字列*という.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0039\">なお, 次元<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>が明らかなときは省略して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>などと表す.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-213\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0040\">注意 2.1.3</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0041\">本講義では文字列, ベクトル, 関数をしばし同一視する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0042\">すなわち, ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を単に文字列と呼ぶこともあり,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0043\">または関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\colon[n]\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>とみなすこともある.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0044\">例えば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>をアダマール符号で符号化した文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>は, 長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>の文字列とみなすこともあれば,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0045\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>とみなすこともある.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0046\">これは, アダマール符号の解析の過程では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>を関数とみなして扱う方が都合が良いものの,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0047\">PCP定理の証明の過程では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>を文字列として扱い, 証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>として与えることになるからである.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0048\">素朴な証拠<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>をアダマール符号で符号化したものを証明として扱うというアイデアを素朴に実装すると, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>に対する局所的な検証(の候補)として以下の検証者を考えることができる:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-214\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0049\">アルゴリズム 2.1.4</span></h4>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0050\">入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M,z\\)</span>を受け取り, 証拠として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取る.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0051\">一様ランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>を選択し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top M\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top z\\)</span>を計算する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0052\">証拠<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>を関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>として解釈し, オラクルアクセスを用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a=\\pi(M^\\top r)\\)</span>を求める.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0053\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(a=r^\\top z\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>を出力し, そうでなければ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>を出力する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0054\">上記の検証者はPCP検証者の性質を持つだろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0055\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span>がYesインスタンスであるとき, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0056\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>とすると, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(M^\\top r)=y^\\top(M^\\top r)=r^\\top(My)=r^\\top z\\)</span>となるため, 検証者は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0057\">一方で<span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span>がNoインスタンスであるときに検証者が拒否する確率はどうなるだろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0058\">PCP検証者であることを示す(cf. 定義1.2.1)には, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>に対して拒否確率は少なくとも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span>以上でなければならない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0059\">これは示せるのであろうか?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0060\">証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>がアダマール符号の符号語である(すなわち, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>と表せる)場合は, 式 (2.2) の性質および<span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span>がNoインスタンスであることから<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\ne z\\)</span>より, 少なくとも確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/q\\)</span>で検証者は拒否する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0061\">しかしながら, 一般の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>はこのような線形関数として表現できるとは限らず, そのような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に対しても拒否しなければならない.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-212-線形性テスト\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0062\">2.1.2 線形性テスト</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0063\">与えられた<span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span>がNoインスタンスであるときに任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>を定数確率で拒否するために,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0064\">アルゴリズム2.1.4を以下のように修正する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0065\">証拠<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>がアダマール符号の符号語ならば, アルゴリズム2.1.4のステップ2-4を実行し, そうでないならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>を出力する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0066\">ここで新たに一つの問題が生じる: どのようにして<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の線形性を局所検証すればよいだろうか?</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0067\">一般にオラクルアクセスで与えられた関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>が線形関数かどうかを厳密に確認するには,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-linearity-test-all-points\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\forall x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,\\quad\\pi(x+y)=\\pi(x)+\\pi(y)\\tag{2.3}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0068\">が成り立つかどうかを確認する必要があり, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスが必要となってしまう.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0069\">そこで, 「線形関数であるかどうか」ではなく「線形関数に近いかどうか」を局所検証することを考える.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-215関数同士の近さ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0070\">定義 2.1.5（関数同士の近さ）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0071\">二つの関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f,g\\colon A\\to B\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)=\\Pr_{a\\sim A}[f(a)\\ne g(a)]\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0072\">と定義し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\\le\\delta\\)</span>を満たすとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(g\\)</span>に*<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta\\)</span>-近い*(<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta\\)</span>-close)という.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0073\">また, 関数クラス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq\\{g\\colon A\\to B\\}\\)</span>および関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon A\\to B\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{F})=\\min_{g\\in\\mathcal{F}}\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0074\">と定義し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta(f,\\mathcal{F})\\le\\delta\\)</span>であるとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{F}\\)</span>に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta\\)</span>-近いという.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0075\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon A\\to B\\)</span>をベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in B^A\\)</span>と同一視すると, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\\)</span>はベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f,g\\)</span>間の正規化されたハミング距離(<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell_0\\)</span>ノルム)に一致する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0076\">線形関数全体<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\subseteq\\{\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\}\\)</span>を考える (定義2.1.2).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0077\">オラクルアクセスとして与えられた関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>に近いかどうかを検証する局所検証者が構成できる [BLR93].</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-216線形性テスト\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0078\">定理 2.1.6（線形性テスト）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0079\">以下を満たす乱択多項式時間オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span>が存在する<a href=\"#jp-fn8\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref8\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>8</sup></a>:</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0080\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>がオラクルアクセスとして与えられたとき,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0081\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0082\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge0.01\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-217符号の局所検査性\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0083\">注意 2.1.7（符号の局所検査性）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0084\">定理2.1.6はアダマール符号<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>は, オラクルアクセスとして与えられた文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>がその符号語か, もしくは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>から遠いかどうかを, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスで確立的に判定できることを意味する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0085\">このような性質を持つ誤り訂正符号を<em>局所検査可能符号</em> (locally testable code)という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0086\">この概念は元々は証明長の短いPCP定理の証明の文脈において, [GS06]によって提案された概念である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0087\">局所検査可能符号の多くの構成はPCP定理の証明とほぼ同様の手法に基づいて構成されている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0088\">詳細はGoldreichによるサーベイ論文[Gol10]を参照されたい.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-1\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0089\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0090\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi\\)</span>は非常に単純で, 線形関数であることの特徴づけ式 (2.3) をランダムな点で確認するというものである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0091\">具体的には以下で与えられる:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-218\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0092\">アルゴリズム 2.1.8</span></h4>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0093\">オラクルアクセスとして関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>を受け取り, 入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n\\)</span>を受け取る.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0094\">一様ランダムに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を選び, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x+y)\\ne\\pi(x)+\\pi(y)\\)</span>が成り立つならば拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0095\">十分大きな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対し, ステップ2を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\)</span>回繰り返す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0096\">この繰り返しの中で一度も拒否しなければ, 受理する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0097\">実際, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>ならば式 (2.3) が成り立つため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0098\">一方で, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge0.01\\)</span>であるときに拒否確率を下から抑えたい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0099\">これは以下の主張から従う:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-219線形性テストの拒否確率\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0100\">主張 2.1.9（線形性テストの拒否確率）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0101\">任意の関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{x,y\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n}[\\pi(x+y)\\ne\\pi(x)+\\pi(y)]\\ge\\frac16\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had}).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0102\">主張2.1.9は後で証明する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0103\">仮定より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge\\delta:=0.01\\)</span>なので,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0104\">ステップ3の定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\)</span>を十分大きくすることによって, アルゴリズム2.1.8の拒否確率を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>より大きくすることができる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0105\">次に主張2.1.9を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0106\">実際にはより一般的な次の補題を証明する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-2110準同型性テスト\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0107\">補題 2.1.10（準同型性テスト）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0108\">有限アーベル群<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G,H\\)</span>に対し, 写像<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon G\\to H\\)</span>が準同型であるとは, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)\\)</span>が成り立つことをいい, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(H\\)</span>への準同型な写像の全体を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{H}\\)</span>と表す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0109\">このとき, 任意の関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon G\\to H\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{x,y\\sim G}[f(x+y)\\ne f(x)+f(y)]\\ge\\frac1{12}\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H}).\n\\]</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-2\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0110\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0111\">記号の簡単のため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f:=\\Pr_{x,y\\sim G}[f(x+y)\\ne f(x)+f(y)]\\)</span>と表す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0112\">また, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in G\\)</span>に対し, 写像<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_y\\colon G\\to H\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_y(x)=f(x+y)-f(y)\\)</span>とし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\colon G\\to H\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nv(x)=\\operatorname*{argmax}_{a\\in H}\\{\\Pr_{y\\sim G}[v_y(x)=a]\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0113\">で定める (タイが存在する場合は任意).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0114\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x)\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\((v_y(x))_{y\\in G}\\)</span>の中での多数決, すなわち最も出現頻度の高い値として定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0115\">証明は以下の三つの主張を組み合わせることによって得られる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-2111\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0116\">主張 2.1.11</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0117\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,v)\\le2\\rho_f\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-3\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0118\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0119\">定義より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f=\\Pr_{x,y}[f(x)+f(y)\\ne f(x+y)]=\\Pr_{x,y}[f(x)\\ne v_y(x)]\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0120\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x)\\ne h\\Rightarrow\\Pr_y[v_y(x)=h]\\le1/2\\iff\\Pr_y[v_y(x)\\ne h]\\ge1/2\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0121\">実際, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((v_y(x))_{y\\in G}\\)</span>の中で多数決をとったときに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\)</span>が選ばれなかったということは, 過半数に至らなかったことを意味するからである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0122\">特に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h=f(x)\\)</span>とすると, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_{v(x)\\ne f(x)}\\le2\\Pr_y[v_y(x)\\ne f(x)]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0123\">両辺の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\sim G\\)</span>に関する期待値をとると</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(f,v)=\\Pr_x[v(x)\\ne f(x)]\\le2\\Pr_{x,y}[v_y(x)\\ne f(x)]=2\\rho_f.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0124\">となり主張を得る.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-2112\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0125\">主張 2.1.12</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0126\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f&lt;1/6\\)</span>ならば, 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in G\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_y[v_y(x)=v(x)]&gt;2/3\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-4\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0127\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0128\">任意に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in G\\)</span>を固定し, 確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi\\)</span>を, 一様ランダムに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\sim G\\)</span>を選び<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi=v_y(x)\\)</span>として定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0129\">我々の目標は, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in H\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[\\Phi=a]&gt;2/3\\)</span>が成り立つことを示すことである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0130\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi\\)</span>の独立なコピーとする (固定する<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>は同一).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0131\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi\\)</span>がある値をとる傾向にあることを示すために, まずは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi_1=\\Phi_2\\)</span>が成り立つ確率が大きいことを示す.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[\\Phi_1=\\Phi_2]&amp;=\\Pr_{y_1,y_2\\sim G}[v_{y_1}(x)=v_{y_2}(x)]\\\\\n&amp;=\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(x+y_1)-f(y_1)=f(x+y_2)-f(y_2)]\\\\\n&amp;=\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(x+y_1)-f(x+y_2)=f(y_1)-f(y_2)]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge1-2\\rho_f\\\\\n&amp;&gt;2/3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0132\">最初の不等式では, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(x+y_1)-f(x+y_2)\\ne f(y_1-y_2)]\\le\\rho_f\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(y_1)-f(y_2)\\ne f(y_1-y_2)]\\le\\rho_f\\)</span>を用いた.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0133\">ここで, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2/3&lt;\\Pr[\\Phi_1=\\Phi_2]=\\sum_{a\\in H}\\Pr[\\Phi=a]^2\\le\\max_a\\Pr[\\Phi=a]\\cdot\\sum_{a\\in H}\\Pr[\\Phi=a]=\\max_a\\Pr[\\Phi=a]\\)</span>より主張を得る.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-2113\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0134\">主張 2.1.13</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0135\">全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in G\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_y[v_y(x)=v(x)]&gt;2/3\\)</span>が成り立つならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in\\mathcal{H}\\)</span>, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\)</span>は準同型である.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-5\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0136\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0137\">全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)+f(y)=f(x+y)\\)</span>が成り立つことを示せばよい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0138\">任意に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>を固定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0139\">一様ランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\sim G\\)</span>を選ぶ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0140\">このとき, 仮定より</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0141\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_z[v_z(x)\\ne v(x)]&lt;1/3\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0142\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_z[v_{z-y}(y)\\ne v(y)]&lt;1/3\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0143\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_z[v_{z-y}(x+y)\\ne v(x+y)]&lt;1/3\\)</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0144\">従って, ユニオンバウンドより, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>に対してある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in G\\)</span>が存在して次の三つが同時に成り立つ:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0145\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x)=v_z(x)=f(x+z)-f(z)\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0146\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(y)=v_{z-y}(y)=f(z)-f(z-y)\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0147\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x+y)=v_{z-y}(x+y)=f(x+z)-f(z-y)\\)</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0148\">これらの等式から</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nv(x)+v(y)-v(x+y)=f(x+z)-f(z-y)-(f(x+z)-f(z-y))=0.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0149\">より主張を得る.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0150\">最後に補題2.1.10の証明を完成させる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0151\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f&lt;1/6\\)</span>ならば, 主張2.1.12および主張2.1.13より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in\\mathcal{H}\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0152\">さらに主張2.1.11より,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\rho_f\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(f,v)}2\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H})}2\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0153\">である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0154\">一方, そうでなければ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f\\ge1/6\\ge\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H})/6\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0155\">いずれにせよ, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f\\ge\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H})/6\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0156\">以上より, 与えられた<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>に近いかどうかを局所検証できることを示した.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0157\">では, 仮に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>に近いとし,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0158\">最も近い線形関数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>としよう.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0159\">このような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>は一意である (演習4).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0160\">このとき, 99%の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x)=f(x)\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0161\">一方でアルゴリズム2.1.4では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(M^\\top r)\\)</span>を計算する必要があり,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0162\">一般に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M^\\top r\\)</span>の分布は一様ランダムとは限らない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0163\">そこで, 任意の点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)\\)</span>を計算することが必要であり,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0164\">次の補題はこれが可能であることを示している.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-2114最も近い線形関数の任意点での評価\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0165\">補題 2.1.14（最も近い線形関数の任意点での評価）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0166\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\le\\epsilon\\)</span>を満たすとし,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0167\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に最も近い線形関数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0168\">このとき, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>への2回のオラクルアクセスを用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span>時間で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)\\)</span>を確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-2\\epsilon\\)</span>で出力する乱択オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span>が存在する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-6\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0169\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0170\">入力として与えられた<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span>はランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を選び, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x+r)-\\pi(r)\\)</span>を出力する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0171\">明らかに時間計算量は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span>であり, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_r[\\pi(x+r)\\ne f(x+r)],\\Pr_r[\\pi(r)\\ne f(r)]\\)</span>はそれぞれ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\epsilon\\)</span>以下である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0172\">従って, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-2\\epsilon\\)</span>で出力値は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x+r)-\\pi(r)=f(x+r)-f(r)=f(x)\\)</span>となる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習4最も近い線形関数の唯一性\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0173\">演習4（最も近い線形関数の唯一性）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0174\">関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\le0.01\\)</span>を満たすとき,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0175\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,f)\\le0.01\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>は唯一存在することを示せ.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-213-線形方程式の局所的な検証22\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0176\">2.1.3 線形方程式の局所的な検証(2/2)</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0177\">ここまでの議論を用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>に対する局所的な検証者を構成する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-2115\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0178\">アルゴリズム 2.1.15</span></h4>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0179\">入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M,z\\)</span>を受け取り, 証拠として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取る.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0180\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>をオラクルとして定理2.1.6のアルゴリズムを実行し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge0.01\\)</span>ならば拒否して終了する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0181\">一様ランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>を選択し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top M\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top z\\)</span>を計算する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0182\">補題2.1.14のアルゴリズムを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M^\\top r\\)</span>を入力, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>をオラクルとして実行し, その出力を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>とする.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0183\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\ne r^\\top z\\)</span>ならば拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0184\">ステップ3-5を3回繰り返し, 一度も拒否しなければ受理して終了する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0185\">アルゴリズム2.1.4の検証者と比較すると,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0186\">まず, 証拠として与えられた関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が線形関数であることを検証するためのステップが追加されている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0187\">また, ステップ4では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(M^\\top r)\\)</span>の代わりに補題2.1.14のアルゴリズムが用いられている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0188\">これまでの議論から, アルゴリズム2.1.15は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>に対する局所的な検証者であることがわかる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0189\">ステップ6は単に成功確率を増幅させるための繰り返しである.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-2116線形方程式の局所的な検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0190\">定理 2.1.16（線形方程式の局所的な検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0191\">アルゴリズム2.1.15の検証者を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0192\">このとき, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n},z\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>に対して以下が成り立つ:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0193\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスの回数は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0194\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在するならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>としたとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0195\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在しないならば, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2/3\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-7\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0196\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0197\">オラクルアクセスの回数は明らかに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0198\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在するならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>としたとき, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0199\">一方, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>が存在しないときに検証者が拒否する確率を考える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0200\">オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に関して以下の二つのケースを考える:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-ケース1-piが線形関数に001-近いとき\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0201\">ケース1: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が線形関数に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-近いとき.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0202\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>がステップ5で拒否する確率を評価する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0203\">オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に最も近い線形関数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0204\">このとき, 補題2.1.14 (入力を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M^\\top r\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\epsilon=0.01\\)</span>とする)より, ステップ4で計算される<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>は, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-2\\epsilon=0.98\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(M^\\top r)\\)</span>と等しい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0205\">ここで, 線形関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)=x^\\top y\\)</span>と表せるとすると, 拒否確率は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[a\\ne r^\\top z]&amp;\\ge\\Pr_r[a\\ne r^\\top z\\mid a=f(M^\\top r)]\\cdot\\Pr_r[a=f(M^\\top r)]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\Pr_r[r^\\top My\\ne r^\\top z]\\cdot0.98\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\left(1-\\frac1q\\right)\\cdot0.98\\\\\n&amp;\\ge0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0206\">を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0207\">三つ目の不等号では式 (2.2) および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\ne z\\)</span>を用いている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0208\">四つ目の不等式では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\ge2\\)</span>を用いている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0209\">これを4回繰り返すため, 拒否確率は少なくとも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-(1-0.4)^3\\ge\\frac23\\)</span>を満たす.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-ケース2-piが線形関数に001-遠いとき\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0210\">ケース2: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が線形関数に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-遠いとき.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0211\">このとき, 定理2.1.6より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>はステップ2において少なくとも確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0212\">従って, どちらのケースでも, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>は少なくとも確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2/3\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0213\">ここまでの議論を用いると以下のように, オラクルを用いて線形方程式の解を効率的に検証する検証者が構成できる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-2117線型方程式の解の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0214\">補題 2.1.17（線型方程式の解の検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0215\">以下を満たす定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span>と乱択<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(mn)\\)</span>時間オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>が存在する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0216\">入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n},z\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>を受け取り, オラクルとして<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取り, これらのオラクルに対して合計で高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行う.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0217\">任意のオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に対し以下が成り立つ:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0218\">あるベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>であるならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0219\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を満たす全てのベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had}_n(y))\\ge0.01\\)</span>ならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/linear_verification.png\" alt=\"補題2.1.17の検証者のイメージ\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0220\">補題2.1.17の検証者のイメージ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0221\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>がオラクルとして与えられたときは常に受理する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0222\">一方, そのような全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>から離れた<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>は高確率で拒否する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習5線型方程式の解の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0223\">演習5（線型方程式の解の検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0224\">補題2.1.17を証明せよ (ヒント: 式 (2.2) および補題2.1.14を用いる).</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-22-弱いpcp定理とその証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0001\">2.2 弱いPCP定理とその証明</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0002\">定理2.1.16のアイデアを拡張してPCP定理を証明するには何が必要だろうか?</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0003\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{LinEQ}\\)</span>は実際には多項式時間で解ける判定問題であるが, 実際には何かしらのNP完全な判定問題に対して定理2.1.16のような検証者を構成しなければならない.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0004\">次に, 定理2.1.16の検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>は, ランダムに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r,r&#39;\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を選ぶ時点で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Omega(n)\\)</span>ビットのランダムネスを必要とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0005\">実際, 証拠として与えられた関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を文字列として表現するとその長さは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>に比例するため, その文字列のランダムなインデックスを指定しようとすると必然的に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Omega(n)\\)</span>ビットのランダムネスが必要である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0006\">そこで証明<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>の長さを指数的な長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{\\Theta(n)}\\)</span>から多項式<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>に抑える必要がある (このとき, インデックスの指定に必要なビット数は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span>で抑えられる).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0007\">本節では前者の問題を解決し, 以下の弱いPCP定理を証明する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-221弱いpcp定理\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0008\">定理 2.2.1（弱いPCP定理）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0009\">ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)=\\operatorname{poly}(n),q(n)=O(1)\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{NP} \\subseteq \\mathrm{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0010\">我々の最終的な目標であるPCP定理(定理1.2.7)では, PCP検証者のランダムビットの長さが<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)=O(\\log n)\\)</span>であることを要求しているため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)\\)</span>に関しては定理2.2.1は指数的に大きい値になってしまっているが, 読み込む証明の文字数は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>に依存しない定数で抑えられるという点では同一である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0011\">なお, 定理2.2.1の証明のアイデアは後に定理1.2.7の証明でも用いられる.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-221-方針\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0012\">2.2.1 方針</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0013\">定理1.2.13で定義した問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span>に対してPCP検証者を構成すればよい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0014\">ここではより一般的に, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=O(1),q=\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\in\\mathrm{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span>を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0015\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンスは</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;f_1(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = 0,\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\vdots\\\\\n&amp;f_m(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0016\">という<span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span>個の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>上の二次連立方程式からなる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0017\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_ix_j\\)</span>としてこれらを並べたベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\)</span>を考える (ここでは<span class=\"math inline\">\\((i,j)\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\([n^2]\\)</span>の元として扱う).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0018\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[m]\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in[n]\\)</span>に対し,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-M-matrix\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nM_{k,(i,j)} = \\begin{cases}\n1 &amp; f_k(x_1,\\dots,x_n)\\text{ が項 }x_i x_j\\text{ を含む場合},\\\\\n0 &amp; \\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\\tag{2.4}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-b-vector\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nb_k = f_k(0)\\tag{2.5}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0019\">によって行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{m\\times n^2}\\)</span>とベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^m\\)</span>を定義すると, 連立方程式は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>と表現できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0020\">従って, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_i)_{i\\in[m]}\\)</span>がYesインスタンスであることと, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>が存在して</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0021\"><strong>条件1</strong> 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0022\"><strong>条件2</strong> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0023\">を満たすことは同値である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0024\">方針としては, この二つの条件を検証するPCP検証者を構成して組み合わせることによって, 上記の問題に対する検証者を構成する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0025\">条件2に関しては, 定理2.1.16の検証者を用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,b)\\)</span>を実行すればよい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0026\">この検証者はオラクルアクセスの回数が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>でランダムビット長が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n^2)\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-222-証明の構成\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0027\">2.2.2 証明の構成</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0028\">有限体<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>の要素数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0029\">PCP検証者が受け取る証明は, 二つの関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>からなる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0030\">具体的には, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>を表すそれぞれ長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^{n^2}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>の文字列を結合して得られる文字列が証明となる (従って全体の長さは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^{n^2}+q^n\\)</span>である).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0031\">検証者は, 条件1と条件2を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>に対し, これらをアダマール符号で符号化したもの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}(x)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi = \\mathrm{Had}(y)\\)</span>を想定している.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0032\">従って, これらが実際に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>を符号化したものであることと, そして確かに<span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,y)\\)</span>が条件1と条件2を満たすことを検証することになる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0033\">定理2.1.16の検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span>を用いる(<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=b\\)</span>とする)と条件2を定数回のオラクルアクセスで確率的に検証できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0034\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>がそれぞれ線形関数に近いかどうかは定理2.1.6より, 定数回のオラクルアクセスで確率的に検証できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0035\">従って, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi,\\pi_x\\)</span>がそれぞれ線形関数に非常に近いことを仮定した上で条件1を検証するPCP検証者を構成すればよい.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-223-テンソル積の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0036\">2.2.3 テンソル積の検証</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0037\">我々の目標は, ある二つのベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi,\\mathrm{Had}(y))\\le 0.01\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}(x))\\le 0.01\\)</span>となることが保証されている二つのオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>への定数回のオラクルアクセスを用いて, その<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>が条件1を満たすことを検証することである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0038\">なお, そのような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>が存在するならばそれらは一意であることが演習4から保証されている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0039\">ここで, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\times n\\)</span>-行列として表現したものを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=xx^\\top\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0040\">条件1は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X\\)</span>を満たすことと同値である.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-222テンソル積の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0041\">補題 2.2.2（テンソル積の検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0042\">ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2})\\le 0.01\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n)\\le 0.01\\)</span>となる二つのオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>への定数回のオラクルアクセスを用いて, その<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>が全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>を満たすことを検証するPCP検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span>が存在する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0043\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=(y_{i,j})_{i,j\\in [n]}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=xx^\\top \\in \\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>に対し,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0044\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X\\)</span>ならば確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span>は受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0045\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\ne X\\)</span>ならば確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span>は拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0046\">方針としては, 式 (2.1) の考え方を行列に拡張することである.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-8\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0047\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0048\">任意の行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A,B\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-random-quadratic-form\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n}\\left[ r_1^\\top X r_2 \\ne r_1^\\top Y r_2 \\right] =\n\\begin{cases}\n0 &amp; \\text{if } X=Y,\\\\\n1-\\frac{1}{q} &amp; \\text{if } X\\ne Y.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{2.6}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0049\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0050\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを用いて式 (2.6) の両辺を計算し, その結果を比較することで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X\\)</span>かどうかを確認できる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0051\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=xx^\\top\\)</span>であるため, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\in\\mathbb{F}^V\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_1^\\top X r_2 &amp;= \\sum_{i,j\\in [n]} r_1(i) r_2(j) x_i x_j \\\\\n&amp;= \\sum_{i\\in [n]} r_1(i) x_i \\sum_{j\\in [n]} r_2(j) x_j \\\\\n&amp;= \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_1)\\cdot \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_2)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0052\">である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0053\">特に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\le 0.01\\)</span>より, 一様ランダムに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を選んだとき, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_1)=\\pi_x(r_1)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_2)=\\pi_x(r_2)\\)</span>となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0054\">従って式 (2.6) の左辺は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>への2回のオラクルアクセスを用いて確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>で計算できる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0055\">次に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1^\\top Y r_2\\)</span>を計算する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0056\">任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{r}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>を, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((i,j)\\)</span>番目の文字が<span class=\"math inline\">\\((r_1)_i\\cdot (r_2)_j\\)</span>であるような文字列とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0057\">このとき</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_1^\\top Y r_2 &amp;= \\sum_{i,j\\in [n]} (r_1)_i (r_2)_j y_{i,j} \\\\\n&amp;= \\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0058\">である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0059\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2})\\le 0.01\\)</span>より, 補題2.1.14を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varepsilon=0.01\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f=\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\pi_y\\)</span>として適用すると, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\\)</span>を計算できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0060\">すなわち, 式 (2.6) の右辺は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y\\)</span>への2回のオラクルアクセスを用いて確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>で計算できる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-223\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0061\">アルゴリズム 2.2.3</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0062\">PCP検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span>を以下のように記述する:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0063\">入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n\\)</span>を受け取り, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取る.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0064\">一様ランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を選択する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0065\">オラクルアクセスを用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r_1),\\pi_x(r_2)\\)</span>を計算する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0066\">補題2.1.14を用いて, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\\)</span>を計算する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0067\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{r}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>は, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((i,j)\\)</span>番目の文字が<span class=\"math inline\">\\((r_1)_i\\cdot (r_2)_j\\)</span>であるような文字列である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0068\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r_1)\\cdot \\pi_x(r_2)\\ne \\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\\)</span>ならば拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0069\">ステップ2-5を10回繰り返し, 一度も拒否しなければ受理して終了する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0070\">ステップ3,4では確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.96\\)</span>で式 (2.6) の両辺を計算でき, さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\ne Y\\)</span>ならば (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>を選ぶランダムネスに関して)確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/q\\)</span>で拒否できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0071\">従って, 一回の試行で少なくとも確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.96-\\frac{1}{q} \\ge 0.4\\)</span> (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\because q\\ge 2\\)</span>)で拒否できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0072\">この試行を十分大きい定数(10回で十分)だけ繰り返すことによって, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\ne Y\\)</span>である時の拒否確率は任意に増幅できる.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-224-弱いpcp定理の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0073\">2.2.4 弱いPCP定理の証明</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0074\">まず, 補題2.1.17を, 線型方程式ではなく回路に対する検証に拡張する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-224回路の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0075\">定理 2.2.4（回路の検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0076\">以下を満たす定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span>と多項式時間乱択オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>が存在する:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0077\">入力として回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon \\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>を受け取り, 二つのオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取り, 全てのオラクルに対し合計で高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行う.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0078\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たすベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>であるならば, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0079\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>と任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/circuit_verification.png\" alt=\"定理2.2.4の検証者のイメージ\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0080\">図 2.2: 定理2.2.4の検証者のイメージ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0081\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>ならば, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0082\">一方, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-9\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0083\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0084\">入力として与えられた回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>に対し, 定理1.2.13で与えたカープ帰着を用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in [m]}\\)</span>を構成する (ここで, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>のサイズ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(m=n+s+1\\)</span>である).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0085\">さらにこの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in[m]}\\)</span>に対し, 式 (2.4), (2.5) で定義される行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n^2}\\)</span>とベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>を構成する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0086\">このとき, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対し, 以下は同値である:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0087\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span> (ここでは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>の元として扱う)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0088\">全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[m]\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(x)=0\\)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0089\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}:=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>で定まるベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>を満たす.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0090\">以下では, 三つ目の条件を検証するPCP検証者を与える.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-225\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0091\">アルゴリズム 2.2.5</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0092\">PCP検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(M,b)\\)</span>を以下で記述する:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0093\">入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{m\\times n^2}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^m\\)</span>および証明として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取る.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0094\">定理2.1.6のアルゴリズムを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>のそれぞれに対して実行し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>が線形関数に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-近いかどうかを検証する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0095\">もしいずれかの実行においてこのアルゴリズムが拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0096\">補題2.2.2の検証者を, オラクルとして<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を用いて実行する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0097\">この検証者が拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0098\">補題2.1.17の検証者を, 入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,b)\\)</span>, オラクルとして<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を用いて実行する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0099\">この検証者が拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0100\">ここまでのステップで拒否していなければ受理して終了する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0101\">この検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}\\)</span>が所望のPCP検証者であることを確認する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0102\">定理2.1.6, 定理2.1.16, 補題2.2.2, 補題2.1.17ではそれぞれオラクルアクセスは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>回であり, 内部で用いるランダムビットは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n^2)\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0103\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>となるような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>であるとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0104\">このとき入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,b)\\)</span>は, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>で定まるベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0105\">この<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)\\)</span>とする (補題の仮定より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>である).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0106\">このとき,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0107\">ステップ2では, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>はどちらも線形関数であるため, 拒否する確率は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0108\">ステップ3では, 実際に全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>が成り立つため, 拒否する確率は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0109\">ステップ4では, 実際に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>の解であるため, 拒否する確率は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0110\">以上より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0111\">次にオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>が, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0112\">を満たすとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0113\">オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>を任意に固定する.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0114\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi,\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2})\\ge 0.01\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n)\\ge 0.01\\)</span>ならば, 検証者はステップ2において確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0115\">以降では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi,\\pi_x\\)</span>はどちらも線形関数に十分近いとし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に最も近い線形関数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)\\)</span>とし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>に最も近い線形関数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0116\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>の仮定から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=0\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0117\">ステップ3は, 補題2.2.2の仮定が満たされているため, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in[n]\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}\\ne x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>となるならば, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0118\">従って以降ではさらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>が, 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>を満たすとする.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0119\">ステップ4は, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=0\\iff My\\ne b\\)</span>であるため, 補題2.1.17の検証者が確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0120\">以上より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}\\)</span>がステップ2-4のループで拒否する確率は少なくとも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.97\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0121\">実は定理2.2.4から即座に弱いPCP定理が示せる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-221-の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0122\">定理 2.2.1 の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0123\">定理1.2.11より, 回路充足可能性判定問題はNP完全である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0124\">従って, この問題に対するPCP検証者を与えればよい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0125\">実は, 定理2.2.4の検証者がこの条件を満たす.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0126\">実際, 入力として与えられた回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>がYesインスタンスならば, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0127\">この<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>の元とみなして, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x = \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>とすれば, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0128\">一方, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>がNoインスタンスならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>となるような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>は存在しないため, どのような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>に対しても, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-226近接pcp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0129\">注意 2.2.6（近接PCP）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0130\">定理2.2.4のように, 入力として回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>が与えられ, さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>が受け取る入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>を符号化したもの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>および「追加の情報」をもつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスが与えられたとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>の充足割り当てを符号化したものから遠い場合に高確率で拒否するような検証者は一般に充足可能性の検証よりも強いことを主張しており, これを達成するような証明のことを<strong>近接PCP</strong> (PCP of Proximity)と呼び, 検証者のことをPCPP検証者と呼ぶ.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-23-二入力回路の割り当てに対するpcp検証者\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0131\">2.3 二入力回路の割り当てに対するPCP検証者</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0132\">定理2.2.4はある意味で, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>を全て見ることなく, 与えられた回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>となるかどうかを検証するPCP検証者を構成した (実際には<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>および追加の情報<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスも仮定している).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0133\">ここでは回路<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>が二つの入力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>を受け取る場合でも同様のPCPP検証者が構成できることを示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0134\">この設定は人工的な問題設定に見えるが, PCP定理の証明において重要な役割を果たす.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-231二入力回路の割り当てに対するpcp検証者\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0135\">定理 2.3.1（二入力回路の割り当てに対するPCP検証者）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0136\">以下を満たす定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span>と多項式時間乱択オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>が存在する:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0137\">入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\times\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>および三つのオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y, \\pi\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを受け取り, これらのオラクルに対して合計で高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行う.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0138\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y=\\mathrm{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>であるならば, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2n} + 2^{4n^2}}\\)</span>が存在して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0139\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge0.01\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_n(y))\\ge0.01\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>および任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0140\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,y)\\)</span>を一つの文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>とみなして定理2.2.4を適用したとき, 検証者は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_z=\\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(z)\\)</span>と追加の情報<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>へのオラクルアクセスを持つことになるが, 定理2.3.1の検証者は, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>それぞれの符号化<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>へのオラクルを持つという点が異なっている.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0141\">定理2.3.1の証明においては, ある文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>が別の文字<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n+m}\\)</span>の接頭辞かどうかを(オラクルアクセスを用いて)局所的に検証することを意味する以下の補題を用いる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-232接頭辞の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0142\">補題 2.3.2（接頭辞の検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0143\">以下を満たす定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span>と多項式時間乱択オラクルアルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}(1^n,1^m)\\)</span>が存在する:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0144\">オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}\\)</span>は次が保証されている: ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n+m}\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))&lt;0.01\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_{\\overline{x}},\\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x}))&lt;0.01\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0145\">検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}\\)</span>は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行う.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0146\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x = \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}=\\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>であって, さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>の先頭<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>文字が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>と一致するならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0147\">一方, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>の先頭<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>文字が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>に一致しない場合, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-10\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0148\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0149\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}\\)</span>は単純で, 以下のように記述できる:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0150\">一様ランダムなベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>を選び, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{r}=(r,0^m)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n+m}\\)</span>とする.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0151\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r) \\ne \\pi_{\\overline{x}}(\\overline{r})\\)</span>ならば拒否する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0152\">ここで, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}(\\overline{r})\\)</span>は補題2.1.14を用いて計算する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0153\">ステップ1-2を10回繰り返し, 一度も拒否しなければ受理して終了する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0154\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>の最初の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>文字を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0155\">もしも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}=\\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>であって, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y=x\\)</span>ならば, ステップ2においてどのような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>を選んでも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r) = \\mathrm{Had}_n(y)(r) = \\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x})(r,0^m)\\)</span>となり, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>でアルゴリズムは受理する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0156\">一方で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\ne y\\)</span>の場合を考える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0157\">オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-近いため, 補題2.1.14より, ステップ2において, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}(\\overline{x})=\\mathrm{Had}_n(y)(r)\\)</span>を計算できる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0158\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span>が一様ランダムなので, 確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r)=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r)\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0159\">従って一度の試行で少なくとも確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.97\\)</span>で検証者は拒否するため, ステップ1-2を10回だけ繰り返せば少なくとも確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で検証者は拒否する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-233接尾辞の検証\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0160\">注意 2.3.3（接尾辞の検証）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0161\">補題2.3.2のアルゴリズムは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>の接頭辞かどうかの検証を行うが, 同様のアルゴリズム (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0^m\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span>の順番を反転させるだけ)を考えれば接尾辞の検証にも利用できる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-231-の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0162\">定理 2.3.1 の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0163\">三つ目のオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^{2^{2n} + 2^{4n^2}}\\)</span>を, 以下の二つの関数の組として解釈する:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0164\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1 \\colon \\mathbb{F}_2^{2n} \\to \\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0165\">この関数は, ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}:=(x,x&#39;)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1 = \\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>であることを想定する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0166\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2 \\colon \\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2} \\to \\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0167\">この関数は, 上記のベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{y}_{i,j} = \\overline{x}_i\\cdot \\overline{x}_j\\)</span>で定まるベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{y}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2}\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2 = \\mathrm{Had}_{4n^2}(\\overline{y})\\)</span>であることを想定する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0168\">なお, 実際には<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span>は関数としては解釈できるが, その関数が線形関数であるとは限らない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0169\">アルゴリズム2.2.5を少し修正した以下の検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{y},\\pi}(C)\\)</span>を考える:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-234\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0170\">アルゴリズム 2.3.4</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0171\">PCP検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{y},\\pi}(C)\\)</span>を以下で記述する:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0172\">オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>を二つの関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2,\\pi_2\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>の組として解釈する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0173\">入力で与えられた回路を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^{2n}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>とみなし, 定理2.2.4の証明で考えた行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{m\\times {(2n)^2}}\\)</span>とベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^m\\)</span>を計算する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0174\">定理2.1.6のアルゴリズムを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span>に対して実行し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span>がそれぞれ線形関数に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-近いかどうかを検証する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0175\">もしいずれかの実行においてこのアルゴリズムが拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0176\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x),\\mathrm{Had}_n(y),\\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(z)\\)</span>をそれぞれ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi_1\\)</span>に最も近い線形関数とする.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0177\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>の前半の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>文字が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>, 後半の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>文字が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>に一致するかどうかをそれぞれ補題2.3.2のアルゴリズムを用いて検証する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0178\">もしいずれかの検証においてこのアルゴリズムが拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0179\">補題2.2.2の検証者を, オラクルとして<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2\\)</span>を用いて実行する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0180\">この検証者が拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0181\">補題2.1.17の検証者を, 入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,b)\\)</span>, オラクルとして<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2\\)</span>を用いて実行する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0182\">この検証者が拒否したら拒否する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0183\">ここまでのステップで拒否していなければ受理して終了する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0184\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y=\\mathrm{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0185\">このとき, 文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>を結合して得られる文字列を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>, また文字列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2}\\)</span>を, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w_{i,j}=z_i\\cdot z_j\\)</span>と定め, 最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi = \\left( \\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(z), \\mathrm{Had}_{4n^2}(w) \\right)\\)</span>とすると, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\mathrm{Had}_n(x),\\mathrm{Had}_n(y),\\pi}(C)\\)</span>は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>で受理する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0186\">一方, オラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>が, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_n(y))\\ge0.01\\)</span>の少なくとも一方が成り立つとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0187\">任意にオラクル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span>を固定する.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0188\">ステップ3では全てのオラクルが線形関数に近いかどうかを確認しており, もしもどれかが線形関数から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-遠いならば, 定理2.1.6より確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で検証者は拒否する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0189\">それぞれに最も近い文字列を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n,z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>とする.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0190\">ステップ4では<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=(x,y)\\)</span>かどうかが確認される.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0191\">もしも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\ne(x,y)\\)</span>ならば確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で拒否される.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0192\">ステップ5以降は定理2.2.4と同一であり, 特に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>の任意の充足割り当てから<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-遠い場合は確率<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>で検証者は拒否する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0193\">さらにこのとき, 任意の充足割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\((x^*,y^*)\\in C^{-1}(1)\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(x,x^*)\\ge0.01\\)</span>もしくは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(y,y^*)\\ge 0.01\\)</span>が成り立つ (演習6).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習-6二つに分割したベクトルの距離\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0194\">演習 6（二つに分割したベクトルの距離）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0195\">ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>に対し, その前半<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>個の成分からなる部分ベクトルを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{head}(z)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>, 後半<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>個の成分からなる部分ベクトルを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{tail}(z)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0196\">集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>を任意に固定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0197\">もしもベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(z,S)\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span>, すなわち, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in S\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(z,s)\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span>を満たすならば, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in S\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0198\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\mathrm{head}(z),\\mathrm{head}(s))\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0199\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\mathrm{tail}(z),\\mathrm{tail}(s))\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0200\">の少なくとも一方が成り立つことを示せ.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"jp-第3章-制約充足問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0001\">第3章 制約充足問題</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0002\">制約充足問題 (Constraint Satisfaction Problem, CSP) は、計算量理論において重要な問題の一つであり、PCP定理の証明においても中心的な役割を果たす。</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-31-制約充足問題の定義\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0003\">3.1 制約充足問題の定義</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0004\">制約充足問題とは端的に言えば連立方程式の解の存在性判定を問う判定問題である。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-311制約充足問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0005\">定義 3.1.1（制約充足問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0006\"><em>制約充足問題 (CSP)</em> とは次の要素からなる組 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> をインスタンスとする判定問題である：</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0007\"><em>アルファベット</em>：有限集合 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0008\"><em>変数列</em>：<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=(\\mathrm{x}_1,\\dots,\\mathrm{x}_n)\\)</span>。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0009\">制約の引数の列：<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}=(I_1,\\dots,I_m)\\)</span>。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0010\">ここで、各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_i\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_i\\ne\\emptyset\\)</span> かつ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_i\\subseteq[n]\\)</span> を満たす。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0011\"><em>制約の列</em>：<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}=(c_I)_{I\\in\\mathcal{I}}\\)</span>。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0012\">ここで、各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\subseteq\\Sigma^{\\lvert I\\rvert}\\)</span> を満たす。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0013\">各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}\\in X\\)</span> を<em>変数</em>、各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\in\\mathcal{C}\\)</span> を<em>制約</em>という。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0014\">入力 <span class=\"math inline\">\\((X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> は、ある変数への割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> が存在して、任意の <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I=\\{i_1,\\dots,i_k\\}\\in\\mathcal{I}\\)</span>（ただし <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i_1&lt;\\dots&lt;i_k\\)</span>）について、</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n(a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}),\\dots,a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}))\\in c_I\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0015\">であるとき、かつその時に限りYesインスタンスである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0016\">また、変数の個数と制約の個数の和 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n+m\\)</span> を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の<em>サイズ</em>といい、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi)\\)</span> と表す。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0017\">全ての <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[m]\\)</span> について <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I_i\\rvert\\le q\\)</span> であるとき、このCSPは*<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP*という。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0018\">また、固定した割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> に対する <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の<em>不満足値</em>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)=\\Pr_{\\{i_1,\\dots,i_k\\}\\sim\\mathcal{I}}\\left[(a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}),\\dots,a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}))\\notin c_I\\right]\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0019\">とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0020\">ここで <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\sim\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> から一様ランダムに選ばれたことを意味する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0021\">さらに、全ての割り当てに関して不満足値の最小値</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=\\min_{a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0022\">を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の<em>不満足値</em>という。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0023\">各制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\in\\mathcal{C}\\)</span> は、その制約が充足される割り当ての集合を表す。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0024\">例えば <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I=\\Sigma^{\\lvert I\\rvert}\\)</span> であるならば、任意の割り当てに対して <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> は充足されるし、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I=\\emptyset\\)</span> であるならば、どの割り当ても <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> を充足しない。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-例-312グラフ彩色問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0025\">例 3.1.2（グラフ彩色問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0026\">グラフ彩色問題（例 1.2.4）は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSPである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0027\">実際、グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span> に対して</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0028\">変数列を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=V\\)</span> とする。</span></p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0029\">アルファベットを <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma=[k]\\)</span> とする。</span></p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0030\">辺の順序が固定された辺集合 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E=\\{e_1,\\dots,e_m\\}\\)</span> に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}=(e_1,\\dots,e_m)\\)</span> とする。</span></p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0031\">各制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_e\\)</span> は各辺 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span> に付随していて、</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n(a_u,a_v)\\in c_e\\iff a_u\\ne a_v\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0032\">と定義する。</span></p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0033\">このとき、グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-彩色可能であることと、このCSPがYesインスタンスであることは同値である。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-例-313二次方程式系の充足可能性判定問題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0034\">例 3.1.3（二次方程式系の充足可能性判定問題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0035\">定理 1.2.13で定義した判定問題 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSPである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0036\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> のインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1,\\dots,f_m\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> に対し、変数を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)</span> とし、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_i(x)=0\\)</span> という制約を考えると、各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_i\\)</span> の出力は高々三つの変数の値にのみ依存するため、これは <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSPである。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0037\">以後は、例 3.1.3のように、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPの定義を与える際は、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> や <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}\\)</span> を明示せずに全ての変数と制約を記述することによって与えることもある。</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-311-pcpとの関係\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0038\">3.1.1 PCPとの関係</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0039\">ランダムシード長 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=r(n)\\)</span> かつクエリ数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> のPCP検証者は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma=\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> の場合の <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPによって表現でき、逆に <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPはPCP検証者として表現できる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0040\">実際、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> クエリのPCP検証者 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)\\)</span> を考えよう。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0041\">証明の長さを <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\pi\\rvert=\\ell\\)</span> とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0042\">入力 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> とランダムシード <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> を固定したときの <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x;s)\\)</span> が証明中の読み込む文字のインデックスの集合を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_s\\subseteq[\\ell]\\)</span> とする（ここで <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I_s\\rvert\\le q\\)</span>）。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0043\">このとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x;s)\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^{I_s}\\)</span> を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> に写す関数を定める。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0044\">この関数を制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_s\\)</span> とみなすことで、検証者 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンスとして表現できる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0045\">このとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンスの変数集合は証明 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> に対応する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0046\">もし <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span> であるならば、確率 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> で <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=1\\)</span> となるような <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> が存在する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0047\">つまり、全てのランダムシード <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> に対して <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x;s)=1\\)</span> となるような <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> が存在するため、先ほど構成した <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンスはYesインスタンスである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0048\">逆に <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\notin L\\)</span> であるならば、全ての <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> に対して確率 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> 以上で <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=0\\)</span> となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0049\">これは、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPインスタンスに対して、全ての割り当てを考えても、全体の制約のうち少なくとも <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> の割合は充足されない（すなわち、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> の値が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> 以上となる）ことを意味する。</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0050\">PCP検証者</span></th>\n<th><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0051\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP</span></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0052\">PCP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0053\">割り当て</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0054\">ランダムネスを固定した時の判定</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0055\">CSPの制約</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0056\">PCP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> を拒否する確率</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0057\">割り当ての不満足値 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\pi)\\)</span></span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0058\">表 3.1：PCPとCSPの対応関係</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0059\">この対応関係に基づいて、PCP定理をCSPを用いた言葉で表すことができる。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-314pcp定理のcsp版\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0060\">定理 3.1.4（PCP定理のCSP版）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0061\">ある関数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K(s)=s^{O(1)}\\)</span>、定数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha&gt;0\\)</span>、および次の性質を満たす多項式時間決定的アルゴリズムが存在する：</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0062\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> のインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> を入力として受け取り、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> を出力する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0063\">このインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> は、与えられた <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> のサイズが <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s=\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi_0)\\)</span> であるとき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi)=K(s)\\)</span>、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert=c\\)</span> となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0064\">さらにこのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> は</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0065\">入力 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)=0\\)</span> である（すなわち <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> のYesインスタンスである）とき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span> となる。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0066\">入力 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)&gt;0\\)</span> である（すなわち <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> のNoインスタンスである）とき、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\ge\\alpha\\)</span> となる。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-315\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0067\">注意 3.1.5</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0068\">一般に <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span> 個の制約からなるCSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> がNoインスタンスであることは <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)\\ge\\frac{1}{m}\\)</span> と同値である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0069\">ところが定理 3.1.4では、変換によって得られるCSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> のサイズが元のCSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> のサイズ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> に<em>よらない定数になる</em>点が重要である。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0070\">PCP検証者の構成とCSPの変換が同値であることを証明する。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-316pcp検証者とcspの変換の同値性\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0071\">補題 3.1.6（PCP検証者とCSPの変換の同値性）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0072\">定理 3.1.4と定理 1.2.14は同値である。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-11\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0073\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0074\">それぞれの方向を別々に証明する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0075\"><em>定理 3.1.4 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)</span> 定理 1.2.14の証明。</em></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0076\">ある <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=O(\\log n)\\)</span>、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;=O(1)\\)</span> に対して、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> に対するシード長 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span>、クエリ回数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;\\)</span> のPCP検証者 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> を構成する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0077\">検証者はまず入力として <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> のインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> を受け取り、定理 3.1.4のアルゴリズムを用いて <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> を計算する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0078\">また、証拠 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> をこの <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の割り当てとして解釈し、PCP検証者 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0)\\)</span> は以下の操作を十分大きな定数回繰り返す：一様ランダムに <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> を選択し、その制約に含まれる変数に対する割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> の値を読み込み、この制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> が読み込んだ割り当てによって充足<em>されない</em>ならば <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> を出力し終了する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0079\">何度も繰り返した末に終了しなかったのであれば、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> を出力して終了する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0080\">この検証者は繰り返しの回数が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> であり、それぞれの繰り返しにおいては高々 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> 個の変数の値を読み込むため、クエリ回数は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;=O(q)=O(1)\\)</span> となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0081\">なお、ここでアルファベットサイズ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span> が定数であるため、全体で読み込む証明のビット数は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;\\cdot\\lceil\\log_2 c\\rceil=O(1)\\)</span> ことに留意する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0082\">また、ランダムシードはランダムな制約を選ぶために使われてるため、その長さは <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log K(\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi_0)))=O(\\log\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi_0))\\)</span> となる。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0083\">もし <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> がYesインスタンスならば、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> もYesインスタンスであるため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> をその充足割り当てとすれば、全ての制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> が充足されるため、（制約の選び方のランダムネスに関して）確率 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> で <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0)=1\\)</span> となる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0084\">もし <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> がNoインスタンスならば、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\ge\\alpha\\)</span> である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0085\">従って、任意の割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> に対して、一様ランダムな制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> が充足される確率は高々 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-\\alpha\\)</span> である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0086\">よって、この操作を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lceil10/\\alpha\\rceil=O(1)\\)</span> 回繰り返すと、少なくとも確率 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> で充足されない制約が一度以上選ばれ、検証者は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> を出力する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0087\"><em>定理 1.2.14 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)</span> 定理 3.1.4の証明。</em></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0088\">仮定より、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> に対する、ランダムシード長 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=O(\\log n)\\)</span>、クエリ回数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> のPCP検証者 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> が存在する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0089\">アルゴリズム <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> は、入力 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> に対して、全てのランダムシード <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in\\{0,1\\}^r\\)</span> を列挙して、それぞれに対して「<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0;s)=1\\)</span>」という制約を加えて得られるCSPを出力する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0090\">各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0;s)\\)</span> は、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> を変数とみなしたとき、高々 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> 個の変数の値を読み込むため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\((c_s)_{s\\in\\{0,1\\}^r}\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^r=n^{O(1)}\\)</span> 個の制約からなる <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPとなる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0091\">なお、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> は多項式時間アルゴリズムなので、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> も多項式時間アルゴリズムである。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0092\">従って、以降は定理 3.1.4の証明に注力する。</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-312-制約グラフ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0093\">3.1.2 制約グラフ</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0094\">PCP定理の証明、すなわち定理 3.1.4の証明は、まず <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSPの特殊ケースである <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSPに変換し、この <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSPに対して様々な変換を繰り返し施すことによって得られる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0095\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSPは各制約が高々一つの変数への割り当てのみに依存するものであるため、その制約をグラフとして記述することができる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0096\">このような <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSPのグラフ表現を<em>制約グラフ</em>という。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-317制約グラフ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0097\">定義 3.1.7（制約グラフ）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0098\">2-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> に対し、以下で定まる組 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> を<em>制約グラフ</em>という<a href=\"#jp-fn9\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref9\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>9</sup></a>：</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0100\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> はグラフである。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0101\">ただし頂点集合は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V=X\\)</span> であり、辺集合は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E=\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0102\">なお、ここで考えるグラフは多重辺や自己ループを持ちうるもの<a href=\"#jp-fn10\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref10\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>10</sup></a>とし、特に <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I_i\\rvert=1\\)</span> の場合は対応する辺は自己ループとする。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0104\">詳細は定義 3.2.1を参照。</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0105\">アルファベット <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0106\">制約の列 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}&#39;=(c&#39;_e)_{e\\in E}\\)</span> は、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert e\\rvert=2\\)</span> ならば <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e=c_e\\)</span> とし、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert e\\rvert=1\\)</span> ならば <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e=\\{(u,u)\\in\\Sigma^2\\colon u\\in e\\}\\)</span> とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0107\">これにより、全ての <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^2\\)</span> の部分集合となる。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0108\">制約グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> および割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> に対して、その<em>不満足値</em>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)=\\Pr_{e\\sim E}[a(e)\\notin c_e]\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0109\">と定義し（ここで <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\)</span>（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u&lt;v\\)</span>）に対して <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(e)=(a(u),a(v))\\in\\Sigma^2\\)</span> とする）、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> の不満足値を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=\\min_{a\\in\\Sigma^V}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0110\">と定義する。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0111\">制約グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> に対して、その<em>サイズ</em>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{size}(G)=\\lvert V\\rvert+\\lvert E\\rvert\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0112\">と定義する。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-318入力長とサイズの関係\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0113\">注意 3.1.8（入力長とサイズの関係）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0114\">本講義で考える制約グラフのアルファベットサイズ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span> は常に定数、すなわち <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert\\)</span> や <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span> に依存しない値であるとする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0115\">この仮定の下、制約グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> を指定するために必要なビット数を考える。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0116\">グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> は隣接行列で表現すると <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert^2\\)</span> ビットで表現できる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0117\">各辺 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span> に付随する制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\subseteq\\Sigma^2\\)</span> は、各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a,b)\\in\\Sigma^2\\)</span> について <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a,b)\\in c&#39;_e\\)</span> かどうかを表すビットを <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a,b)\\)</span> について並べれば良いため、全部で <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\cdot\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert^2\\)</span> ビットで表現できる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0118\">よって、制約グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert^2+\\lvert E\\rvert\\cdot\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert^2=O(\\operatorname{size}(G)^2)\\)</span> ビットで表現できる。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0119\">このことから、制約グラフを入力として受け取るアルゴリズムが多項式時間かどうかを議論する際は、その時間計算量が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span> に関して多項式かどうかを議論すれば良いことになる。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0120\">制約グラフは2-CSPを表現するためのものであるが、一般の <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPもまた制約グラフとして表現することができる。</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-319k-cspの制約グラフ表現\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0121\">補題 3.1.9（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPの制約グラフ表現）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0122\">任意の <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> に対し、ある定数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> および多項式時間アルゴリズム <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> が存在して次が成り立つ：</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0123\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> を入力として受け取り、以下を満たす制約グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma^k,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> を出力する。</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0124\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\le ck\\cdot(\\lvert X\\rvert+\\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert)\\)</span>。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0125\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span> ならば <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0126\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)&gt;0\\)</span> ならば <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\ge\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\)</span>。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-12\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0127\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0128\">入力として与えられた <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPの変数集合 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=\\{\\mathrm{x}_1,\\dots,\\mathrm{x}_n\\}\\)</span> および制約集合 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}=(c_I)_{I\\in\\mathcal{I}}\\)</span> に対し、新たに構成する制約グラフは次のような構造を持つ：</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0129\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の各変数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_i\\)</span> に対し頂点 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span> を用意し、各制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span>（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\in\\mathcal{I}\\)</span>）に対し頂点 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span> を用意する。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0130\">変数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_i\\)</span> が制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> に含まれる、すなわち <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_i\\in I\\)</span> ならば、二頂点 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span> と <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span> の間に辺を引いて得られる二部グラフを <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0131\">簡単のため、以下では各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\in\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> に対して <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I\\rvert=k\\)</span> とする（そうでない制約が存在するならば、そのような各制約は同じ変数を複数回読み込むことを許し、対応する辺は多重辺とすることで、読み込み回数をちょうど <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> にする）。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0132\">この二部グラフは、各 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_i\\)</span> の次数はちょうど <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> であり、サイズは（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> のみに依存する）定数倍で抑えられる。</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/k-to-two.png\" alt=\"制約グラフの構成例。例えば、変数y_1に対応する制約は変数x_1,x_2,x_3に依存している。\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0133\">図 3.1：制約グラフの構成例。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0134\">例えば、変数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{y}_1\\)</span> に対応する制約は変数 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_1,\\mathrm{x}_2,\\mathrm{x}_3\\)</span> に依存している。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0135\">新たな制約グラフのアルファベットは <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^k\\)</span> とする。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0136\">各辺 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{x_i,y_I\\}\\)</span> に対応する制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\)</span> は、割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\leftarrow\\vec{\\sigma}=(\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_k)\\in\\Sigma^k\\)</span>、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\leftarrow\\vec{\\tau}=(\\tau_1,\\dots,\\tau_k)\\in\\Sigma^k\\)</span> が次の条件を満たすとき、かつその時に限り充足される：<span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span> の隣接頂点を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{i_1},\\dots,x_{i_k}\\)</span>（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i_1&lt;\\dots&lt;i_k\\)</span>）としたとき、</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0137\">部分割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}\\leftarrow\\tau_1,\\dots,\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}\\leftarrow\\tau_k)\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> を充足する。</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0138\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau_i=\\sigma_1\\)</span>。</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0139\">ここでは、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span> への割り当てのうち、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_2,\\dots,\\sigma_k\\)</span> は無視されていることに留意されたい。</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/k-to-two2.png\" alt=\"割り当ての例。ここでは、(tau_1,tau_2,tau_3)がy_1に対する制約を充足し、さらにsigma_j=tau_jならば、辺{y_1,x_j}に対応する制約グラフの制約が満たされる。\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0140\">図 3.2：割り当ての例。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0141\">ここでは、<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\tau_3)\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{y}_1\\)</span> に対する制約を充足し、さらに <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_j=\\tau_j\\)</span>（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j=1,2,3\\)</span>）であれば、辺 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{y_1,x_j\\}\\)</span> に対応する制約グラフの制約が満たされる。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0142\">元の <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> が充足可能、すなわち <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span> ならば、その充足割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> に対し、制約グラフ <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> の割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to\\Sigma^k\\)</span> を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;a&#39;(x_i)=(a(\\mathrm{x}_i),\\underbrace{\\ast,\\dots,\\ast}_{k-1}) \\quad (i\\in[n]),\\\\\n&amp;a&#39;(y_I)=\\left(a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}),\\dots,a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}),\\sigma_1\\right) \\quad (I=\\{i_1,\\dots,i_k\\}\\in\\mathcal{I}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0143\">と定める（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ast\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span> の任意の元でよい）。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0144\">この割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> の全ての制約を満たすため、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span> である。</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0145\">逆に、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> の任意の割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to\\Sigma^k\\)</span> に対し、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> の割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\na(\\mathrm{x}_i)=a&#39;(x_i)_1 \\quad (i\\in[n])\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0146\">と定める。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0147\">割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> が頂点 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span> に接続する全ての辺 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{x_i,y_I\\}\\)</span>（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\forall i\\in I\\)</span>）に対応する <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> の制約を充足するならば、割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> は制約 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> を満たす。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0148\">対偶をとると、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> が <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> を満たさないならば、<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> は少なくとも1つの辺 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{\\{x_i,y_I\\}\\}_{i\\in I}\\)</span> に対応する制約が充足されない。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0149\">すなわち <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G)\\ge\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)\\)</span> である。</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0150\">従って、割り当て <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span> となるように選ぶと</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G)\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)}{k}\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)}{k}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0151\">より、主張を得る。</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-32-多重グラフの導入とエクスパンダーグラフ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0001\">3.2 多重グラフの導入とエクスパンダーグラフ</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0002\">以後, 制約グラフに関する様々な操作をしていく上で多重グラフのフォーマルな定義を与えておく.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-321多重グラフ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0003\">定義 3.2.1（多重グラフ）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0004\">有限集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>と多重集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span>の組<span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span>を<strong>多重グラフ</strong>という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0005\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\cup \\binom{V}{2}\\)</span>の元から構成される多重集合であり, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert e\\rvert=1\\)</span>ならば<strong>自己ループ</strong>であるという.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0006\">二頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V\\)</span>の間の<strong>重み</strong>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nw(u,v) = \\lvert \\{e\\in E \\colon e = \\{u,v\\}\\}\\rvert\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0007\">と定義し, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>の<strong>次数</strong>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\deg(u) = \\sum_{v\\in V} w(u,v)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0008\">と定義する.<a href=\"#jp-fn11\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref11\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>11</sup></a></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0009\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W=(w(u,v))_{u,v\\in V}\\)</span>を<strong>重み行列</strong>と呼び, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P(u,v):=\\frac{w(u,v)}{\\deg(u)}\\)</span>で定まる行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span>を<strong>遷移確率行列</strong>という.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0011\">グラフの次数を並べたベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d=(\\deg(v))_{v\\in V}\\in \\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span>を<strong>次数ベクトル</strong>という.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0012\">次数ベクトルは</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nd = W\\mathbf{1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0013\">で与えられる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0014\">グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が自己ループを含まない場合は握手補題が成り立つため<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sum_{u\\in V}\\deg(u) = 2\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>が成り立つが, そうでない場合は次数の総和は自己ループを一度ずつしかカウントしないため, 自己ループの個数を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>とすると</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-hand-shake-multigraph\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\sum_{u\\in V} \\deg(u) = 2\\lvert E\\rvert - \\ell \\le 2\\lvert E\\rvert \\tag{3.1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0015\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0016\">以下に正則グラフとエクスパンダーグラフの定義を述べる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-322正則性とエクスパンダー性\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0017\">定義 3.2.2（正則性とエクスパンダー性）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0018\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>頂点の多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>の全ての頂点の次数が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>に等しいとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は**<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則**であるという.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0019\">また, 遷移確率行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span>の固有値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1=\\lambda_1\\ge \\lambda_2\\ge \\cdots \\ge \\lambda_n \\ge -1\\)</span>が</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\lambda(P):=\\max\\{\\lvert\\lambda_2\\rvert, \\lvert\\lambda_n\\rvert\\} \\le \\lambda\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0020\">を満たすとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は**<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダー**であるという.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0021\">多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が正則ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span>は対称行列となるため実固有値を持つことが直ちに従うが, 一般の場合でも実固有値を持つことが示せる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0022\">さらに, Gershgorinの定理からそれらの固有値の絶対値は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>以下であることが従う.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0023\">また, 全成分<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>のベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span>の固有値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>に対する固有ベクトルとなるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_1=1\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習問題-7\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0024\">演習問題 7</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0025\">任意の多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の遷移確率行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span>は実固有値を持つことを示せ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0026\">第一固有値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>の多重度はグラフの連結成分の個数に等しいことが知られている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0027\">ここではその特殊ケースである以下の事実を用いる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-命題-323\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0028\">命題 3.2.3</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0029\">多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が連結であるならば, 遷移確率行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span>の固有値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>の多重度は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_2&lt;1\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-321-正則エクスパンダーの性質\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0030\">3.2.1 正則エクスパンダーの性質</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0031\">この節では正則かつエクスパンダー性を持つ単純グラフの性質は同様に多重グラフに対しても成り立つことを確認する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0032\">特に, 正則性より遷移確率行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span>は対称となることに留意されたい.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-324エクスパンダー混交補題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0033\">補題 3.2.4（エクスパンダー混交補題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0034\">連結な頂点数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>の多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーであるとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0035\">二つの頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S,T\\subseteq V\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW(S,T) = \\sum_{u \\in S, v\\in T} w(u,v)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0036\">とすると, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S,T\\subseteq V\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\left\\lvert W(S,T) - \\frac{d}{n}\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert \\right\\rvert \\le \\frac{\\lambda d}{n} \\sqrt{\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus S\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus T\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0037\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0038\">特に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S\\rvert\\le n/2\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq V\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW(S,V\\setminus S) \\ge (1-\\lambda)\\frac{d\\lvert S\\rvert}{2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0039\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注釈-325直感的な意味\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0040\">注釈 3.2.5（直感的な意味）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0041\">簡単のため自己ループを持たないグラフを考える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0042\">このグラフが<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則ならば全部で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(nd/2\\)</span>本の辺を持つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0043\">全部で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\binom{n}{2}\\approx n^2/2\\)</span>個の頂点対があるため, 辺密度は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d/n\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0044\">さて, グラフの辺が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\binom{V}{2}\\)</span>に「均一に」散らばっていると仮定すると, 任意に固定した頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S,T\\subseteq V\\)</span>に対してその間をまたがる辺の本数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W(S,T)\\)</span>はおよそ<span class=\"math inline\">\\((d/n)\\cdot \\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\\)</span>であることが期待される.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0045\">グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>がエクスパンダー性を持つ場合, この期待値からのずれの上からの評価を与えるのがエクスパンダー混交補題である.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-13\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0046\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0047\">「特に, ...」の部分は前半の主張に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T=V\\setminus S\\)</span>を代入して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\setminus S\\rvert\\ge n/2\\)</span>を用いれば示せるので, 前半の主張を証明する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0048\">全成分<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>の行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(J \\in \\mathbb{R}^{V\\times V}\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M:=P - \\frac{1}{n}J\\)</span>とおく.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0049\">また, 頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq V\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S\\in \\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathbf{1}_S(u) = \\begin{cases}\n1 &amp; \\text{if } u\\in S \\\\\n0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0050\">で定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0051\">二つのベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span>を,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;x = \\mathbf{1}_S - \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert}{n}\\mathbf{1},\\\\\n&amp;y = \\mathbf{1}_T - \\frac{\\lvert T\\rvert}{n}\\mathbf{1}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0052\">とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0053\">ベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}\\)</span>に直交するので<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S = x + \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert}{n}\\mathbf{1}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S\\)</span>の直交分解となっていること, 及び<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W\\mathbf{1}=d\\mathbf{1}\\)</span>に着目すると,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(S,T) &amp;= \\mathbf{1}_S^T W \\mathbf{1}_T \\\\\n&amp;= x^T W y + \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert}{n^2}\\mathbf{1}^T W \\mathbf{1} \\\\\n&amp;= x^\\top W y + \\frac{d}{n}\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0054\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0055\">従って</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\lvert W(S,T) - \\frac{d}{n}\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert \\right\\rvert\n&amp;= \\lvert x^\\top W y\\rvert \\le \\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\lVert W y\\rVert_2 &amp;&amp; \\text{Cauchy-Schwarzの不等式による} \\\\\n&amp;\\le d \\lambda \\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\lVert y\\rVert_2 &amp;&amp; \\text{レイリー商と固有値の関係による} \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{\\lambda d}{n} \\sqrt{\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus S\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus T\\rvert} &amp;&amp; \\text{$\\lVert x\\rVert_2^2 = \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert(n-\\lvert S\\rvert)}{n}$による}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0056\">を得る.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0057\">次にグラフの<strong>べき乗</strong>の操作を定義する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-326グラフのべき乗\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0058\">定義 3.2.6（グラフのべき乗）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0059\">重み行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W\\)</span>を持つ多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\ge 0\\)</span>に対し, 多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k=(V,E^k)\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW&#39; = W^k\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0060\">を重み行列とする多重グラフと定める.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0061\">元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の各二頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>間の長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>の路の個数だけ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>間の辺を追加することで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span>を得られる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-327正則エクスパンダー性のべき乗\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0062\">補題 3.2.7（正則エクスパンダー性のべき乗）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0063\">頂点数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである多重グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が与えられたとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0064\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d^k\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda^k\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-14\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0065\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0066\">べき乗で得られるグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span>の重み行列は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W&#39;=W^k\\)</span>であるため, その次数ベクトルは</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW&#39;\\mathbf{1} = W^k\\mathbf{1} = d^k\\mathbf{1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0067\">となるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d^k\\)</span>-正則である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0068\">さらに, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span>の遷移確率行列は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P^k\\)</span>で与えられるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1=\\lambda_1 \\ge \\dots \\ge \\lambda_n \\ge -1\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P^k\\)</span>の固有値は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1=\\lambda_1^k \\ge \\dots \\ge \\lambda_n^k \\ge -1\\)</span>となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0069\">今, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\max\\{\\lvert\\lambda_2\\rvert, \\lvert\\lambda_n\\rvert\\} \\le \\lambda\\)</span>であるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\max\\{\\lvert\\lambda_2^k\\rvert, \\lvert\\lambda_n^k\\rvert\\} \\le \\lambda^k\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0070\">よって, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda^k\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-322-エクスパンダーグラフの構成\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0071\">3.2.2 エクスパンダーグラフの構成</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0072\">エクスパンダーグラフは, その構造から様々な応用が知られている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0073\">特に, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\ge 2\\)</span>に対して頂点数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>のエクスパンダーグラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>時間で構成できることが知られている.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-328エクスパンダーグラフの構成\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0074\">定理 3.2.8（エクスパンダーグラフの構成）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0075\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\ge 3\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0&lt;1\\)</span>および以下を満たす多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が存在する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0076\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n=(\\underbrace{1,\\dots,1}_n)\\)</span>を受け取り, 頂点数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-エクスパンダーグラフの隣接行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W\\)</span>を出力する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0077\">定理 3.2.8の証明はエクスパンダーグラフの構成に関するブレイクスルーの結果[RVW02]の結果に軽微な修正を施すことによって得られる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-329エクスパンダーグラフ族の構成\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0078\">定理 3.2.9（エクスパンダーグラフ族の構成）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0079\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d&#39;_0\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&#39;_0&lt;1\\)</span>および以下を満たす多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span>が存在する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0080\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in \\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^{2^k}\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, 頂点数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^k\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d&#39;_0\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&#39;_0\\)</span>-エクスパンダーグラフの隣接行列<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W&#39;\\)</span>を出力する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0081\">定理 3.2.9の証明はジグザク積と呼ばれるグラフの積に関する手法を用いることで得られるが, 本講義のスコープからは逸脱するので割愛する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0082\">定理 3.2.8の証明, すなわち一般の頂点数のエクスパンダーグラフを得るには, まず<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{k-1}&lt; n \\le 2^k\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>に対して定理 3.2.9を適用し, その後にそのグラフが<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>頂点になるようにうまく二つの頂点を縮約し, 必要に応じて頂点に自己ループまたは多重辺を追加することによって正則にすることによって得られる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0083\">なお, 補題 3.2.7より, 次数を大きくすることによって固有値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>をいくらでも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>に近づけることが可能である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0084\">ギャップ増幅補題では与えられた制約グラフを変換していく.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0085\">表記の簡略化のため, グラフに対する性質を表す用語を制約グラフにもそのまま適用することとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0086\">例えば<span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span>が連結であるときに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>は連結であるという.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0087\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span>が正則であるときに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>は正則であるという.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-33-制約グラフの定数次数エクスパンダー化\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0001\">3.3 制約グラフの定数次数エクスパンダー化</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0002\">ここでは, 与えられた制約グラフを, 不満足値をそれほど減らさずに定数次数の正則性かつエクスパンダー性を持つように変形できることを示す.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-331定数次数エクスパンダー化\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0003\">補題 3.3.1（定数次数エクスパンダー化）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0004\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&lt;1\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して, 任意の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, 以下の性質を満たす別の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>を出力する決定的多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が存在する:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0005\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は自己ループを持つ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0006\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c\\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0007\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0008\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\beta\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0009\">この補題の証明は次数の削減とエクスパンダー化の二つのステップからなる.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-331-次数の削減\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0010\">3.3.1 次数の削減</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0011\">まず, 与えられた制約グラフを定数次数の正則グラフに変換する補題を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0012\">この変換によって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>の値は定数倍しか変化しない.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-332次数削減補題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0013\">補題 3.3.2（次数削減補題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0014\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して, 任意の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, 以下の性質を満たす別の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>を出力する決定的多項式時間アルゴリズムが存在する:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0015\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0016\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V&#39;\\rvert \\le 2\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0017\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0018\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge c&#39;\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-15\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0019\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0020\">与えられた制約グラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>とし, 変換によって得られる制約グラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0021\">フォーマルな構成を与える前に, まず図例を先に示す.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/degree_reduction.png\" alt=\"次数削減変換の例\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0022\">図 3.3: 次数削減変換の例.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0023\">橙色の内部の頂点集合がクラウドであり, クラウド内の辺は定理 3.2.8によって構成されるが, ここでは図の簡単のため<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_d\\)</span>を長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>の閉路としている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0024\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>における黒い辺の制約は対応する元のグラフの辺と同一の制約とし, クラウド内の赤辺の制約は等式制約とする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0025\">元のグラフの各頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>に対し,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n[u]=\\left\\{(u,e)\\in\\{u\\}\\times E \\colon e=\\{u,v\\}\\text{ for some }v\\in V\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0026\">を(この証明のローカルな用語として)<em><span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>-クラウド</em>と呼ぶことにする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0027\">新しい頂点集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V&#39;\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V&#39;=\\bigcup_{u\\in V}[u]\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0028\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>をそれに接続する辺の本数(自己ループは1個分としてカウント)だけコピーして得られる集合が<span class=\"math inline\">\\([u]\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0029\">次に辺集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E&#39;\\)</span>を以下のように構成する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0030\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((X_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\)</span>を定理 3.2.8によって構成される<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-正則<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーの族とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0031\">元のグラフの各頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d=\\lvert[u]\\rvert\\)</span>としたとき, 頂点集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\([u]\\)</span>上で<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_d\\)</span>と同型なグラフを構成し, それを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(([u],E_u)\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0032\">このとき, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>-クラウド内部の辺集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\)</span>は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nE_{\\mathrm{inner}}=\\bigcup_{u\\in V}E_u\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0033\">とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0034\">次に異なるクラウド間を繋ぐ辺集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nE_{\\mathrm{outer}}=\\left\\{\\left\\{(u,e),(v,e)\\right\\}\\colon e\\notin[u]\\cap[v]\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0035\">とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0036\">このとき, グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>の辺集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E&#39;\\)</span>は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nE&#39;=E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\cup E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0037\">となる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0038\">次に各辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in E&#39;\\)</span>の制約<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e&#39;}\\)</span>を以下のように定める:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0039\">辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\\)</span>がクラウド間をつなぐ辺ならば, その制約は対応する元の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>の制約と同じとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0040\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;=\\{(u,e),(v,e)\\}\\)</span>ならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e&#39;}=c_e\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0041\">辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\)</span>がクラウド内部の辺ならば, その制約を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e&#39;}=\\{(\\sigma,\\sigma)\\colon\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\}\\)</span>, すなわち等式制約とする.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0042\">このようにして得られる制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>が補題の主張を全て満たすことを確認する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0043\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\((d_0+1)\\)</span>-正則である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0044\">実際, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>の各頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\in V&#39;\\)</span>に接続する辺は, クラウド内の辺が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>本, クラウド間の辺が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>本である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0045\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>から<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>を構成する際に新たに追加する辺は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\)</span>のみであり, これは高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sum_{u\\in V}\\frac{d_0\\deg_G(u)}{2}\\le d_0\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>本である (ここで式 (3.1)を用いた).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0046\">従って</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-bound_of_E-prime\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert=\\lvert E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\rvert+\\lvert E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\\rvert\\le(d_0+1)\\lvert E\\rvert \\tag{3.2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0047\">を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0048\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V&#39;\\)</span>の要素数は次数の総和に等しいため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V&#39;\\rvert\\le2\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0049\">次に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0050\">実際, 元の制約グラフの全ての制約を満たす割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>の割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V&#39;\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\na&#39;(u,e)=a(u)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0051\">と定めると, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>の制約を満たす (同一クラウド内の頂点には全て同じ値が割り当てられるため, クラウド内の辺の制約は全て満たされ, クラウド間の辺に対応する制約は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>の取り方により全て満たされることがわかる).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0052\">最後の主張, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)\\ge c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>となる定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span>が存在すること示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0053\">定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-c_of_degree_reduction\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nc&#39;=\\min\\left\\{\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8(d_0+1)},\\frac{1}{2(d_0+1)}\\right\\} \\tag{3.3}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0054\">と定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0055\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)\\)</span>を満たす割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V&#39;\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>を任意に取る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0056\">この割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>に対し, 元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\na(u)=\\mathrm{Maj}((a&#39;(u,e))_{(u,e)\\in[u]})\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0057\">と定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0058\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(\\cdot)\\)</span>は多数決関数であり, タイは任意に選ぶとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0059\">例えば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,2)=2\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,3,3)=3\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,2,3,3)=2\\)</span>である (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,2,3,3)=3\\)</span>としても良いが, ここでは便宜上小さい方の数字を採用している).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0060\">以降, 割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V&#39;\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\in V&#39;\\)</span>への割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u,e)\\)</span>を頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\)</span>の<em>意見</em>と呼ぶこととする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0061\">同様に, 割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>への割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\)</span>を頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>の意見と呼ぶこととする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0062\">直感的には頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>の意見は対応する<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>-クラウド内の頂点の意見の多数決である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0063\">辺集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)</span>を割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>によって充足されない辺の集合, すなわち</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nF=\\left\\{e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\colon(a(u),a(v))\\notin c_e\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0064\">と定義する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0065\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}=(c_e)_{e\\in E}\\)</span>は元の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の制約である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0066\">同様に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F&#39;\\subseteq E&#39;\\)</span>を割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>によって充足されない辺の集合とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0067\">このとき<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)=\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\frac{\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0068\">頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq V&#39;\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>の多数決で選ばれなかった意見を持つ頂点の集合, すなわち</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS=\\left\\{(u,e)\\in V&#39;\\colon a(u)\\ne a&#39;(u,e)\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0069\">と定義し, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in V\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v=S\\cap[v]\\)</span>とし, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma=\\{(u,e)\\in S^v\\colon a&#39;(u,e)=\\sigma\\}\\)</span>とする (図 3.4).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0070\">なお, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>が多数決の意見 (すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma=a(v)\\)</span>)のとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma=\\emptyset\\)</span>とする.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/majority_degree_reduction.png\" alt=\"多数決によって選ばれなかった頂点の集合\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0071\">図 3.4: 多数決によって選ばれなかった<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>-クラウド内の頂点の集合を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S_v\\subseteq[v]\\)</span>とする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0072\">以下の二つのケースを考える:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-ケース1-lvert-srvertgefracoperatornameunsatag2lvert-ervertの場合\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0073\">ケース1. <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S\\rvert\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>の場合.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0074\">各頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in V\\)</span>と多数決によって選ばれなかった意見<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\setminus\\{a(v)\\}\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S^v_\\sigma\\rvert\\le\\frac{\\lvert[v]\\rvert}{2}\\)</span>である (そうでなければ意見<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)</span>が多数決によって選ばれなかったことに矛盾する).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0075\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>-クラウド内部のグラフは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-正則<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-エクスパンダーであるため, その頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma\\subseteq[v]\\)</span>に対するエクスパンダー混交補題 (補題 3.2.4)より,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW(S^v_\\sigma,[v]\\setminus S^v_\\sigma)\\ge(1-\\lambda_0)\\cdot\\frac{d_0\\lvert S^v_\\sigma\\rvert}{2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0076\">となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0077\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(W(S,T)\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>-クラウド内部のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(([v],E_v)\\)</span>の辺であって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T\\)</span>の間をまたがる辺の本数である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0078\">ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\([v]\\setminus S^v_\\sigma\\)</span>をまたがる全ての辺の両端点の意見は異なるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>はこれらの辺を充足しない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0079\">よってこれらの辺は全て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F&#39;\\)</span>に含まれる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0080\">従って</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert &amp;\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{v,\\sigma}W(S^v_\\sigma,[v]\\setminus S^v_\\sigma) \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{4}\\sum_{v,\\sigma}\\lvert S^v_\\sigma\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{4}\\lvert S\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\cdot\\lvert E\\rvert &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{ケース1の仮定} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8(d_0+1)}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\cdot\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert. &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{式 (3.2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0081\">特にこれから<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\frac{\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\ge\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8(d_0+1)}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-ケース2-lvert-srvertfracoperatornameunsatag2lvert-ervertの場合\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0082\">ケース2. <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S\\rvert&lt;\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>の場合.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0083\">任意の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in F\\)</span>に対して, それに対応する<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;=\\{(u,e),(v,e)\\}\\)</span>を考える.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0084\">もしも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)=a&#39;(u,e)\\)</span>かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(v)=a&#39;(v,e)\\)</span>が成り立つならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>は同じ制約を持ち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in F\\)</span>であることから<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in F&#39;\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0085\">そうでない, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\ne a&#39;(u,e)\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(v)\\ne a&#39;(v,e)\\)</span>が成り立つならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\((v,e)\\)</span>のうち少なくとも一方はその意見が多数決として選ばれない, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\in S\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\((v,e)\\in S\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0086\">以上より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F\\rvert\\le\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert+\\lvert S\\rvert\\)</span>が成り立つので</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert &amp;\\ge\\lvert F\\rvert-\\lvert S\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\lvert E\\rvert\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)-\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{ケース2の仮定} \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2(d_0+1)}\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{式 (3.2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0087\">となり, これから</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\frac{\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\ge\\frac{1}{2(d_0+1)}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0088\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0089\">ケース1,2より, 式 (3.3)で定まる定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)\\ge c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\ge c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"jp-332-エクスパンダー化\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0090\">3.3.2 エクスパンダー化</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0091\">次に, 与えられた正則な制約グラフをエクスパンダーグラフに変換する補題を示す.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-333エクスパンダー化補題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0092\">補題 3.3.3（エクスパンダー化補題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0093\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&lt;1,d,d_0\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>および次を満たす多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が存在する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0094\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は入力として<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則な制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>を受け取り, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d+d_0+1)\\)</span>-正則で全頂点が自己ループを持ち, さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>を出力する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0095\">さらに, この制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>を満たす.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-16\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0096\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0097\">入力として与えられた制約グラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0098\">変換は以下のようにして行われる:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0099\">まず, 定理 3.2.8を用いて頂点集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>上<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-正則<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-エクスパンダーグラフを構成し, その辺集合を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span>に追加する (この際多重辺も許す).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0100\">次に, 各頂点に自己ループを付与する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0101\">ステップ1,2で追加した辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>に対応する制約<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\)</span>を自明な制約<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e=\\Sigma^2\\)</span>とする.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0102\">このようにして得られる制約グラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0103\">元の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則であることから, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\((d+d_0+1)\\)</span>-正則である (自己ループの次数への寄与は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>であることに注意).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0104\">また, ステップ2より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は全頂点が自己ループを持つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0105\">次に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0106\">任意の割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, ステップ1,2で追加した辺に対応する制約は常に充足されるため, 両者が充足<em>しない</em>制約の個数は一致する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0107\">すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)=\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G&#39;)\\)</span>が成り立つので</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)&amp;=\\min_a\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G&#39;) \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\min_a\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G) \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0108\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0109\">最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda:=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}+\\frac{\\lambda_0(d_0+1)}{d+d_0+1}\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーであることを示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0110\">元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の遷移確率行列を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span>とし, ステップ1,2で追加した辺からなるグラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span>とし, その遷移確率行列を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P_0\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0111\">また, 最終的に得られるグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>の遷移確率行列を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P&#39;\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0112\">元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則, 追加したグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\((d_0+1)\\)</span>-正則であるため</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nP&#39;=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}P+\\frac{d_0+1}{d+d_0+1}P_0\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0113\">となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0114\">さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(P_0\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-エクスパンダーであるため, 全成分<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>のベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\boldsymbol{1}\\)</span>と直交する任意のベクトル<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^\\top P&#39;x&amp;=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}x^\\top Px+\\frac{d_0+1}{d+d_0+1}x^\\top P_0x \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\lVert x\\rVert^2+\\frac{d_0+1}{d+d_0+1}\\lambda_0\\lVert x\\rVert^2 &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{$P_0$のエクスパンダー性} \\\\\n&amp;\\le\\left(\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}+\\frac{\\lambda_0(d_0+1)}{d+d_0+1}\\right)\\lVert x\\rVert^2\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0115\">より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda(P&#39;)\\le\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}+\\frac{\\lambda_0(d_0+1)}{d+d_0+1}\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0116\">これにより補題 3.3.1を示す準備が整った.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-331の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0117\">補題 3.3.1の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0118\">与えられた制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>に対し, まず<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>を入力として補題 3.3.2のアルゴリズムを実行し, その出力を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0119\">この制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span>は補題 3.3.2の主張より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0120\">次に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span>を入力として補題 3.3.3のアルゴリズムを実行し, その出力を最終的な出力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0121\">この制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は補題 3.3.3の主張より, 全頂点が自己ループを持ち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d+d_0+1)\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0122\">また, この一連の変換によって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;)\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>の定数倍にしかならない.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0123\">最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>の値を考える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0124\">元の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば, 補題 3.3.2および補題 3.3.3より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1)=0\\)</span>を得る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0125\">一方で</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1)\\ge\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\cdot c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0126\">すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\cdot c&#39;\\)</span>に対して主張が成り立つことが示された.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"jp-第4章-pcp定理の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0001\">第4章 PCP定理の証明</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0002\">本チャプターはPCP定理の証明を与える.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-41-ギャップ増幅補題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0003\">4.1 ギャップ増幅補題</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0004\">DinurによるPCP定理の証明は, 与えられた制約グラフの不満足値を段階的に増幅していくアプローチに基づく.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0005\">その中核を担うのがこのギャップ増幅補題であり, 制約グラフの不満足値を増幅できることを保証する補題である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0006\">ギャップ増幅補題は以下のように述べられる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-411ギャップ増幅補題\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0007\">補題 4.1.1（ギャップ増幅補題）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0008\">任意のアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, ある二つの定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0,\\alpha=\\alpha(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)\\in (0,1)\\)</span>, アルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0\\)</span>, および以下の性質を満たす決定的多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が存在する:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0009\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal C\\rangle\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, 次の性質を満たす別の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,\\mathcal C&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>を出力する:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0010\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;)\\le c\\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0011\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0012\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)\\ge \\min\\{\\alpha, 2\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0013\">PCP定理（定理3.1.4）の証明は, まずNP完全問題である<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{ThreeQuadEQ}\\)</span>を補題3.1.9を用いて制約グラフに変換し, その制約グラフに対して補題4.1.1を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log s)\\)</span>回だけ繰り返し適用することによって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>の値を定数まで増幅することで得られる.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-42-制約グラフのべき乗\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0014\">4.2 制約グラフのべき乗</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0015\">補題3.3.1により, 任意の制約グラフを定数次数エクスパンダーに変換することができる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0016\">この節では, そのような定数次数エクスパンダー性を持つ制約グラフに対し, 以下で定義する<em>べき乗</em>という操作を考えることで, その<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>の値を増幅させることができることを示す.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定義-421制約グラフの冪乗\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0017\">定義 4.2.1（制約グラフの冪乗）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0018\">パラメータ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\in\\mathbb N\\)</span>および全頂点が自己ループを持つ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則な制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal C\\rangle\\)</span>に対し, べき乗<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell = \\langle(V,\\mathbf E),\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}},\\mathcal C^\\ell\\rangle\\)</span>を, 以下のようにして定義する:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0019\">- 頂点集合は同じ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0020\">なお, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>の元は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_1&lt;\\dots &lt; v_n\\)</span>のように順序づけられているとする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0021\">- グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,\\mathbf E)\\)</span>をべき乗（定義3.2.6）によって得られるグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)^\\ell\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0022\">すなわち, 各二頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V\\)</span>に対し, 長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>-路の個数と同じだけ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>間に多重辺を用意する（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>-路と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(vu\\)</span>路の個数は一致するため, 無向グラフとして定義できる）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0023\">このようにして得られる辺の多重集合を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf E\\)</span>とし, その元を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e = \\{u,v\\} \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span>とする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0024\">- アルファベット集合は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0025\">ここで, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}=(\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_{d^{\\ell}}) \\in \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>が割り当てられたとき, 次の意味を持つ: 元の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>上で頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から距離<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>以内の頂点の集合を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Gamma(u)\\)</span>とし, その元を頂点番号の大小順で並べて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Gamma(u)=\\{v_1,\\dots,v_\\ell\\}\\)</span>とする（ここで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則性より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell \\le d^{\\ell}\\)</span>である）.<a href=\"#jp-fn12\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref12\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>12</sup></a></span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0027\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\)</span>は各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in\\Gamma(u)\\)</span>に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}_u\\in \\Sigma\\)</span>を割り当てる写像とみなし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}_v\\)</span>を*<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>に対する意見*と呼ぶ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0028\">なお, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell &lt; d^{\\ell}\\)</span>の場合は相異なる二つの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma},\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;\\in\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>が同一の写像としてみなされることもある（これらは制約を充足するか否かにおいては区別されない）.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0029\">- 辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e = \\{u,v\\} \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span>に対応する制約<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf c_{\\mathbf e}\\in \\mathcal C^\\ell\\)</span>は次の二つを満たす割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\vec{\\sigma},\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;)\\in \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}} \\times \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>によって満たされる:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0030\">元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の全ての辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{s,t\\}\\in E\\)</span>（ただし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(s&lt;t\\)</span>）に対し,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0031\"> - <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in \\Gamma(u), t\\in\\Gamma(v)\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\vec{\\sigma}(s), \\vec{\\sigma}&#39;(t))\\in c_e\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0032\"> - <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in\\Gamma(v), t\\in\\Gamma(u)\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;(s), \\vec{\\sigma}(t))\\in c_e\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/gap_amplification.png\" alt=\"制約グラフのべき乗の図例\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0033\">図4.1: 制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>のべき乗<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>の図例.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0034\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\)</span>にそれぞれ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma},\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;\\in\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>が割り当てられているとき, それぞれ関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\colon \\Gamma(u)\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;\\colon \\Gamma(v)\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>とみなす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0035\">なお, 元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>が自己ループを持つことから, 距離<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>以内の全ての頂点に対して意見が定義される.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0036\">パラメータ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>は制約グラフのサイズ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>には依存しない定数であることを常に想定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0037\">べき乗によって得られる制約グラフは元の制約グラフに対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>の値が増幅する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-422べき乗の性質\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0038\">補題 4.2.2（べき乗の性質）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0039\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\beta(\\lambda,d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)\\)</span>が存在して以下が成り立つ:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0040\">十分大きいパラメータ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell=\\ell(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert,\\lambda) \\le \\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>および全頂点が自己ループを持つ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則な制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal C\\rangle\\)</span>に対し, べき乗<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell = \\langle(V,\\mathbf E),\\Sigma^{\\ell},\\mathcal C^\\ell\\rangle\\)</span>を, 定義4.2.1で定義する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0041\">このとき</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0042\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G^\\ell) \\le \\operatorname{size}(G)\\cdot d^\\ell\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0043\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)=0\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0044\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell) \\ge \\beta\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\}\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-注意-423定数の意味\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0045\">注意 4.2.3（「定数」の意味）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0046\">主張や証明の中でしばし「定数」という言葉を多用するが, ここでは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span>をユニバーサルな定数で固定し, これらの値に応じて定まる値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta,\\ell\\)</span>なども定数と呼ぶ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0047\">ただし, 定数はグラフの頂点数や辺数には依存しない.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-17\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0048\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0049\">定義より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathbf E\\rvert \\le \\lvert V\\rvert d^{\\ell}\\)</span>であるから, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G^\\ell) \\le \\operatorname{size}(G)\\cdot d^\\ell\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0050\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば, 全制約を満たす割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V \\to \\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u)\\)</span>に</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\vec{\\sigma}(u)\\colon w \\mapsto a(w)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0051\">で定まる関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\colon \\Gamma(w)\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>とみなせる値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\in\\Sigma^{d^\\ell}\\)</span>を割り当てる（候補が複数存在する場合はどれでもよい）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0052\">このようにして定まる<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to\\Sigma^{\\ell}\\)</span>を考えると, これは全ての制約を満たす割り当てであるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)=0\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0053\">最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)\\)</span>の下界を証明する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0054\">簡単のため<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>は偶数であるとする（奇数の場合は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell/2\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lfloor\\ell/2\\rfloor\\)</span>に置き換えれば同じ証明が成立する）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0055\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>値をとる確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}_j(u)\\)</span>を, グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>上で頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から開始する長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j\\)</span>のランダムウォークの最終到達頂点とする（長さとは辿った辺の本数のことである）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0056\">べき乗の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>における割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V \\to \\Sigma^{\\ell}\\)</span>に対し, 元の制約グラフに対する任意の割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>を</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-argmax-of-RW\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\na(u) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{\\tau \\in \\Sigma} \\left\\{\\Pr\\left[a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_{\\ell/2}(u))_u = \\tau\\right]\\right\\}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0057\">によって定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0058\">常に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}_{\\ell/2}(u)\\in \\Gamma(u)\\)</span>であるから<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}(u))_u\\)</span>は必ず存在する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0059\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から開始したランダムウォークが最も「拾いやすい」意見を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\)</span>としている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0060\">このようにして定めた割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V \\to \\Sigma\\)</span>は, 適当な定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\beta(\\lambda,d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-lower-bound-of-UNSAT\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell) \\ge \\beta\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\right\\}. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0061\">を満たすことを後で示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0062\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>に対して式(4.2)を適用し, さらに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\le \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>を代入すると<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)\\)</span>の所望の下界が得られて証明は完了する.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"jp-式42の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0063\">式(4.2)の証明.</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0064\">割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>を固定し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>を式(4.1)によって定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0065\">辺部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_0\\subseteq E\\)</span>を, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>によって充足されない辺の集合とし, さらにその部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq F_0\\)</span>を,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0066\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F_0\\rvert \\le \\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_0=F\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0067\">- そうでない場合は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F\\rvert = \\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\right\\rfloor\\)</span>となるように<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\)</span>を任意に固定する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0068\">このとき, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\ge 1\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\ge x/2\\)</span>であることから, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\ge 1\\)</span>の仮定を用いて</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-abs-F\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert F\\rvert &amp;\\ge \\min\\left\\{\\lvert F_0\\rvert, \\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\right\\rfloor\\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\min\\left\\{\\lvert F_0\\rvert, \\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{2}\\min\\left\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0069\">を得る（なお, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\ge \\ell\\)</span>の仮定を用いるのはここのみである）.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0070\">べき乗グラフの辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>上の長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>の路に対応する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0071\">従って<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e = (u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span>（ただし全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[\\ell]\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in E\\)</span>）と表し, 元のグラフの辺と区別するため<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>辺, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\in\\mathbf E\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>辺と呼ぶことにする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0072\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e=(u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>番目の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e_i:=\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\)</span>は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0073\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e_i \\in F\\)</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0074\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u_{0})_{u_{i-1}} = a(u_{i-1})\\)</span>かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u_\\ell)_{u_i} = a(u_i)\\)</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0075\">をどちらも満たすとき, <em>悪い</em>ということにする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0076\">一様ランダムに<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\)</span>を選んだとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_i\\in\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>を辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e_i\\)</span>が悪いならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_i=1\\)</span>, そうでないならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_i=0\\)</span>とする（これらは確率変数である）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0077\">さらに</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nI = \\left[\\frac{\\ell}{2} - \\sqrt{\\ell}, \\frac{\\ell}{2} + \\sqrt{\\ell}\\right] \\cap \\mathbb{Z}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0078\">に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B=\\sum_{i\\in I}B_i\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0079\">もしも<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B&gt;0\\)</span>ならば, 辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\)</span>の制約<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{\\mathbf e}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>によって充足されない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0080\">従って, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell) \\ge \\Pr_{\\mathbf e\\sim \\mathbf E}[B\\ge 1]\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0081\">Paley-Zygmundの不等式（補題A.1.3）より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B]\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B^2]\\)</span>を評価することで<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[B\\ge 1]\\)</span>の下界を得ることができる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0082\">これらは以下のように評価できる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-424bの期待値\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0083\">主張 4.2.4（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span>の期待値）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0084\">ある十分小さな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C=C(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span>の期待値は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathbb E[B] \\ge C\\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-425b2の期待値\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0085\">主張 4.2.5（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B^2\\)</span>の期待値）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0086\">ある十分大きな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C&#39;=C&#39;(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B^2\\)</span>の期待値は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathbb E[B^2] \\le C&#39;\\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0087\">これらの主張の証明は後で与える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0088\">ここでは, これらを用いて式(4.2)を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0089\">実際, 補題A.1.3, 主張4.2.4, 主張4.2.5より, 定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\frac{C^2}{2C&#39;}&gt;0\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell) &amp;\\ge \\Pr[B\\ge 1] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{\\mathbb E[B]^2}{\\mathbb E[B^2]} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{C^2}{C&#39;} \\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\beta \\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\min\\left\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\right\\} &amp;&amp; \\text{式(4.3)による}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0090\">となり主張を得る.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0091\">最後に残された二つの主張の証明を与える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0092\">なお, 制約グラフのエクスパンダー性を用いるのは主張4.2.5の証明のみである.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張424の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0093\">主張4.2.4の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0094\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B_i]=\\Pr_{\\mathbf e\\sim \\mathbf E}[e_i\\text{ が悪い}]\\)</span>を下から抑えればよい.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0095\">一様ランダムな<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e=(u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\sim\\mathbf E\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>上の長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>のランダムウォークであるから, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\)</span>は以下のプロセスによって生成される:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0096\">1. 辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim E\\)</span>を一様ランダムに選ぶ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0097\">2. 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1}\\)</span>から開始して長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i-1\\)</span>のランダムウォークを行い, 辿った頂点を順番に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1},u_{i-2},\\dots,u_0\\)</span>とする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0098\">3. 同様に, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i\\)</span>から開始して長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell-i\\)</span>のランダムウォークを行い, 辿った頂点を順番に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i,u_{i+1},\\dots,u_\\ell\\)</span>とする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0099\">4. 頂点列<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span>に対応する<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span>を出力する.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/randomwalk_process.png\" alt=\"ランダムウォークの図例\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0100\">図4.2: ランダムウォークの図例.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0101\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0\\)</span>から開始して長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i-1\\)</span>のランダムウォークを行い, 辿った頂点を順番に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1},u_{i-2},\\dots,u_0\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0102\">同様に, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i\\)</span>から開始して長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell-i\\)</span>のランダムウォークを行い, 辿った頂点を順番に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i,u_{i+1},\\dots,u_\\ell\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0103\">この二つのランダムウォークを組み合わせて, 長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>のランダムウォークを生成する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0104\">このプロセスに基づいて, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[B_i=1]\\)</span>を評価する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0105\">まず, ステップ1で選ばれた辺が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span>である確率は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0106\">次にステップ1で選ばれた辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span>で条件つけたとき, ステップ2と3は独立なランダムネスに基づく試行であるため, それぞれの両端点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0,u_\\ell\\)</span>は独立な確率変数である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0107\">従って</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-Pr-B_i\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\Pr[B_i=1] \\\\\n&amp;\\quad = \\mathbb E_{\\text{step 1-3}}\\left[\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F \\text{ and }a&#39;(u_0)_{u_{i-1}} = a(u_{i-1})\\text{ and }a&#39;(u_\\ell)_{u_i} = a(u_i)\\right] \\\\\n&amp;\\quad = \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\cdot \\mathbb E_{\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim F}\\left[ \\underbrace{\\Pr_{\\text{step 2}}[a&#39;(u_0)_{u_{i-1}} = a(u_{i-1})]}_{=:p_0} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Pr_{\\text{step 3}}[a&#39;(u_\\ell)_{u_i} = a(u_i)]}_{=:p_\\ell}\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.4}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0108\">を得る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0109\">二つ目の等式では, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span>で条件つけて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim E\\)</span>をランダムに選ぶというのは, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\)</span>内の辺を一様ランダムに選ぶことに他ならないことに留意する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0110\">次に, 任意の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span>を固定して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_0\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_\\ell\\)</span>を評価する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0111\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1}\\)</span>を任意に固定して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_0\\)</span>を考える（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_\\ell\\)</span>も同様に示せる）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0112\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>および非負整数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\ge 0\\)</span>に対し, 確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,m}\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>を, （<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>における）<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}_m(u)\\)</span>の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>に対する意見, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,m} = a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_m(u))_u\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0113\">式(4.1)より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m=\\ell/2\\)</span>のとき,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-center_opinion\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X_{u_{i-1},\\ell/2} = a(u_{i-1})] \\ge \\frac{1}{\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert} \\tag{4.5}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0114\">である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0115\">ステップ2で選ばれた端点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0=\\operatorname{RW}_i(u_{i-1})\\)</span>であるから, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u_{i-1},i}=a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_i(u_{i-1}))_{u_{i-1}} = a&#39;(u_0)_{u_{i-1}}\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0116\">従って,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-p_0\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\np_0 = \\Pr[X_{u_{i-1},i} = a(u_{i-1})] \\tag{4.6}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0117\">である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0118\">任意に頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>を固定して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,\\ell/2}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,i}\\)</span>の分布を比較する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0119\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>なので, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert i-\\ell/2\\rvert \\le \\sqrt{\\ell}\\)</span>である.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0120\">元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の各頂点に対し, その頂点が持つ自己ループの一つを赤く塗る（仮定より元のグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則かつ各頂点が自己ループを少なくとも一つ持つ）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0121\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から開始する長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell/2\\)</span>のランダムウォークを考え, 確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_{\\ell/2}\\)</span>を, このランダムウォークが辿った赤<em>以外</em>の辺の個数とする（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_i\\)</span>も同様に定義する）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0122\">各頂点がちょうど一つの赤い自己ループを持つため, 確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_{\\ell/2}\\)</span>の分布は始点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>に依存せず二項分布<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)\\)</span>である（同様に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_i\\)</span>の分布は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(i,1-1/d)\\)</span>である）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0123\">また, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_k(u)\\)</span>を, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から開始する赤い辺を選ばない長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>のランダムウォークの最終到達頂点とする（すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の各頂点から赤い辺を除いて得られるグラフ上の長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>のランダムウォークである）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0124\">従って, 任意の頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\subseteq V\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-RW_i_distribution\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u)\\in A] &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^{i} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u) \\in A \\mid Y_{i}=k] \\cdot \\Pr[Y_{i}=k] \\\\\n&amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^{i} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_k(u) \\in A] \\cdot \\Pr[Y_{i}=k]\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.7}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0125\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0126\">ここで, 以下の主張を後で示す:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張-426\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0127\">主張 4.2.6</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0128\">十分大きな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1=C_1(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>と十分小さな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_2=C_2(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して以下が成り立つ:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0129\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(K:= \\{k\\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0} \\colon \\lvert k-(1-\\frac{1}{d})\\frac{\\ell}{2}\\rvert \\le C_1\\sqrt{\\ell}\\}\\)</span>としたとき,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0130\">1. <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2} \\notin K] \\le \\frac{1}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0131\">2. 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in K\\)</span>と全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[Y_i=k] \\ge C_2\\cdot \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k]\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0132\">主張4.2.6を仮定し, その定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1,C_2\\)</span>を用いて主張4.2.4を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0133\">パラメータ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>に依存して十分大きくとる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0134\">このとき, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\subseteq [0,\\ell/2-\\sqrt{\\ell}] \\subseteq [0,i]\\)</span>が成り立つから</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-RW_i_distribution_2\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u)\\in A] &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^{i} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\Pr[Y_{i}=k] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\sum_{k\\in K} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\underbrace{\\Pr[Y_{i}=k]}_{\\ge C_2 \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k]} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge C_2 \\sum_{k \\in K} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge C_2 \\left(\\sum_{0\\le k \\le \\ell/2} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k] - \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}\\notin K]\\right) \\\\\n&amp;\\ge C_2 \\left(\\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{\\ell/2}(u)\\in A] - \\frac{1}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.8}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0135\">を得る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0136\">ここで頂点部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\subseteq V\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A = \\{v \\in V \\colon a&#39;(v)_u = a(u)\\}\\)</span>として式(4.8)を適用すると</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X_{u,i}=a(u)] = \\Pr[a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u))_u = a(u)] = \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u)\\in A] \\ge \\frac{C_2}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0137\">となるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u=u_{i-1}\\)</span>とすると, 式(4.6)より</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\np_0 = \\Pr[X_{u_{i-1},i} = a(u_{i-1})]\\ge \\frac{C_2}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0138\">を得る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0139\">同様に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_\\ell\\)</span>についても, 適当な定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C&#39;_2&gt;0\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_{\\ell}\\ge \\frac{C&#39;_2}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\\)</span>が成り立つため, 式(4.4)より, 適当な定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_3=C_3(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[B_i=1] \\ge \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\cdot \\mathbb E_{\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim F}[p_0 \\cdot p_\\ell] \\ge C_3\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0140\">を得る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0141\">最後に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B=\\sum_{i\\in I}B_i\\)</span>より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B]\\ge C_3\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span>を得る.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張426の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0142\">主張4.2.6の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0143\">一つ目の主張は, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_{\\ell/2}\\)</span>の分布が二項分布<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)\\)</span>であることとChebyshevの不等式（補題A.1.2）を用いれば示せる（演習8）.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0144\">二つ目の主張を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0145\">すなわち, 二つの二項分布<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(i,1-1/d)\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)\\)</span>を比較する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0146\">一つ目の主張を満たすように任意に定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1&gt;0\\)</span>を固定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0147\">このとき, 二つ目の主張が成り立つようにうまく<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_2&gt;0\\)</span>を選べることを示せばよい.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0148\">一般に, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\in\\mathbb N\\)</span>, 定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\in(0,1)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C&gt;0\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb N\\)</span>（ただし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert k-mp\\rvert\\le C\\sqrt{m}\\)</span>を満たす）に対し,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-de-moivre-laplace\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{Bin}(m,p) = k] = \\frac{1 \\pm O_{C,p}(1/m)}{\\sqrt{2\\pi m p(1-p)}} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{(k-mp)^2}{2m p(1-p)}\\right) \\tag{4.9}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0149\">が成り立つ（De Moivre-Laplaceの定理）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0150\">特に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert k-mp\\rvert\\le C\\sqrt{m}\\)</span>を満たすため, 右辺は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Theta(1/\\sqrt{m})\\)</span>で評価できる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0151\">従って, 適当な定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(D_1,D_2&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>が十分大きいとき,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\Pr[Y_i=k]=\\Pr[\\operatorname{Bin}(i,1-1/d)=k] \\ge \\frac{D_1}{\\sqrt{i}}, \\\\\n&amp;\\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k]=\\Pr[\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)=k] \\le \\frac{D_2}{\\sqrt{\\ell/2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0152\">を満たす.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0153\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>より, 主張を得る.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-演習-8主張の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0154\">演習 8（主張の証明）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0155\">主張4.2.6の一つ目の主張, すなわちある十分大きな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1=C_1(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2} \\notin K] \\le \\frac{1}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0156\">が成り立つことを示せ.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-主張425の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0157\">主張4.2.5の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0158\">ランダムウォーク<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e=(u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\sim\\mathbf E\\)</span>に対し, 確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z_i\\)</span>を, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z_i=1\\)</span>, そうでなければ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z_i=0\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0159\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_i\\le Z_i\\)</span>であるから, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z:=\\sum_{i\\in I} Z_i\\)</span>に対し<span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\le Z\\)</span>であり, 特に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B^2]\\le \\mathbb E[Z^2]\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0160\">以下, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[Z^2]\\)</span>を評価する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0161\">まず, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[Z_i]=\\Pr[\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F]=\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span>である（ランダムウォークの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>番目の辺の周辺分布は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span>上の一様分布だから）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0162\">定義より</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-E-Z2\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[Z^2] &amp;= \\sum_{i,j\\in I} \\mathbb E[Z_i Z_j] = \\mathbb E[Z] + 2 \\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\mathbb E[Z_i Z_j] \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\lvert I\\rvert + 2 \\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\mathbb E[Z_iZ_j].\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.10}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0163\">ここで, 固定した<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i&lt;j\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[Z_iZ_j]\\)</span>を上から評価する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0164\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーグラフ上の長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>のランダムウォーク<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span>を考えたとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>番目の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j\\)</span>番目の辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{j-1},u_j\\}\\)</span>が同時に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\)</span>に含まれる確率を評価する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0165\">辺部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)</span>の辺に接続している頂点部分集合を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_F:=\\bigcup_{e\\in F} e\\)</span>とすると, ランダムウォーク<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_{i-1},\\dots,u_j)\\)</span>に関する確率について,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"jp-eq-V_F-bound\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F \\text{ and } \\{u_{j-1},u_j\\}\\in F] \\le \\Pr[u_{i-1}\\in V_F\\text{ and }u_j\\in V_F] \\tag{4.11}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0166\">となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0167\">ここでは, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1}\\)</span>を一様ランダムに選び, そこから開始する長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j-i+1\\)</span>のランダムウォーク<span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_{i-1},u_i,\\dots,u_{j-1},u_j)\\)</span>に関する確率を考えている.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0168\">このランダムウォークは, べき乗グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^{j-i+1}\\)</span>上の長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>のランダムウォークと一致する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0169\">補題3.2.7より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^{j-i+1}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d^{j-i+1}\\)</span>-正則かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda^{j-i+1}\\)</span>-エクスパンダーである.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0170\">従って, 補題3.2.4より, 式(4.11)の右辺は</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[u_{i-1}\\in V_F\\text{ and }u_j\\in V_F] &amp;= \\frac{W(V_F,V_F)}{nd^{j-i+1}} \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\left(\\frac{\\lvert V_F\\rvert}{n}\\right)^2 + \\lambda^{j-i+1} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert V_F\\rvert}{n} \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\left(\\frac{2\\lvert F\\rvert}{n}\\right)^2 + \\lambda^{j-i+1} \\cdot \\frac{2\\lvert F\\rvert}{n} \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{d\\lvert F\\rvert^2}{\\lvert E\\rvert^2} + \\frac{\\lambda^{j-i+1}d\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0171\">を得る.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0172\">ここで, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F\\rvert/\\lvert E\\rvert \\le 1/\\ell\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I\\rvert \\le 2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\)</span>を用いると, ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C=C(d,\\lambda)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\mathbb E[Z_iZ_j] &amp;\\le d\\underbrace{\\lvert I\\rvert^2 \\left(\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\right)^2}_{\\le 4\\lvert F\\rvert/\\lvert E\\rvert} + \\frac{d\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\lambda^{j-i+1} \\\\\n&amp;\\le 4d \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} + d\\lvert I\\rvert\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\cdot \\sum_{i\\ge 0} \\lambda^i \\\\\n&amp;\\le C\\lvert I\\rvert\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0173\">これと式(4.10)を組み合わせると, 主張を得る.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"jp-43-アルファベット削減\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0001\">4.3 アルファベット削減</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0002\">ギャップ増幅補題（補題4.1.1）では変換前後で制約グラフのアルファベットは同一であることを主張しているが, べき乗をとることによって, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>の値は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sqrt{\\ell}\\)</span>倍に増幅する一方でアルファベットは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^\\ell\\)</span>になってしまう.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0003\">そこで, アルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^\\ell\\)</span>上の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>を, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>や<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}\\)</span>をそれほど損なわずアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>上の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>に変換する必要がある.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理-431アルファベット削減\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0004\">定理 4.3.1（アルファベット削減）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0005\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma&gt;0\\)</span>, アルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0\\)</span>, および多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>が存在して以下が成り立つ: アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,C\\rangle\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, 別の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,C&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>を出力する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0006\">ただし<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>は, ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>に対して次を満たす.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0007\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c\\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0008\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0009\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\gamma\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0010\">定理4.3.1の証明では, まず, あるアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_1\\)</span>と定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>が存在して, 与えられた制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>を, アルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_1\\)</span>上の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=\\langle(X,\\Sigma_1,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\rangle\\)</span>に変換する多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span>が存在することを示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0011\">次にこの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSPを補題3.1.9を用いて<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0:=\\Sigma_1^k\\)</span>上の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>に変換することによって証明は完了する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-432\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0012\">補題 4.3.2</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0013\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>と多項式時間アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span>が存在して以下が成り立つ: 任意のアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>に対し, ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在し, アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span>は制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,C\\rangle\\)</span>を入力として受け取り, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\{0,1\\},\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\)</span>を出力する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0014\">ただし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>は次を満たす.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0015\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert X\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0016\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0017\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi) \\ge 0.99\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-18\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0018\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0019\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(b = \\lceil\\log_2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\rceil\\)</span>とし, アルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^b\\)</span>と同一視する（<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span>が2べきでない場合はダミーの値を追加して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert=\\lvert\\{0,1\\}^b\\rvert\\)</span>とし, 各制約はダミーの値を読み込んだ場合は常に拒否するようにすればよい）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0020\">制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の各辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span>に対応する制約<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_e\\subseteq \\Sigma^2\\)</span>を指示関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_e\\colon\\Sigma^2\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>と同一視し, これを二入力の論理回路として表現したものを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e\\colon \\{0,1\\}^b\\times \\{0,1\\}^b\\to \\{0,1\\}\\)</span>とする（任意の論理関数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon\\{0,1\\}^\\ell\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>はサイズ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\ell2^\\ell)\\)</span>の論理回路で表現できるため, この回路はサイズが高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{O(b)}\\)</span>であるが, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b=\\log_2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span>は定数として扱うのでこの回路のサイズも定数である）.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/newCSP.png\" alt=\"2-CSPの各辺の制約を論理回路として表現したもの\" /></p>\n<p><em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0021\">図4.3: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSPの各辺の制約を論理回路として表現したもの.</span></em></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0022\">各辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>に対し, このようにして得られた各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e\\)</span>に対して定理2.3.1を適用する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0023\">この定理から得られる検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e)\\)</span>に対し, ランダムシード<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\operatorname{poly}(b)}\\)</span>を固定したときの決定的な検証者を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0024\">なお, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>の時間計算量は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{poly}(b)\\le T\\)</span>であるとする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0025\">これは以下の性質を持つ.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0026\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)=1\\)</span>を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\)</span>に対して <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u=\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u)\\)</span>および<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_v=\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v)\\)</span>であるならば, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span>が存在して, 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span>は受理する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0027\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)=1\\)</span>を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\)</span>に対して</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u))\\ge0.01 \\text{ or }\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi_v,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v))\\ge0.01\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0028\">を満たす任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u,\\pi_v\\)</span>および任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e \\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span>に対して,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_r\\left[V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\text{ rejects}\\right]\\ge 0.99.\n\\]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0029\">ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span>が存在して検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e)\\)</span>は高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行う.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0030\">なお, 最終的に構成するCSPは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPとなる.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/newCSP2.png\" alt=\"構成するq_0-CSPの図\" /></p>\n<p><em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0031\">図4.4: 構成する<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSPの図.</span></em> <em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0032\">PCPP検証者がアクセスするオラクルを文字列とみなした時の各成分が変数に対応する.</span></em> <em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0033\">実際には, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span>を列挙してそれらを全て制約に追加する.</span></em></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0034\">最終的に出力する新しい<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>を以下のように構成する.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0035\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>と各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in [2^b]\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{u,i}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>を変数とし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u=(\\pi_{u,i})_{i\\in[2^b]}\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^b}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0036\">同様に, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span>と各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j\\in [2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}]\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{e,j}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>を変数とし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e=(\\pi_{e,j})_{j\\in[2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}]}\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0037\">全体の変数集合は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=\\{\\pi_{u,i}\\colon u\\in V,i\\in[2^b]\\} \\cup \\{\\pi_{e,j}\\colon e\\in E,j\\in[2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}]\\}\\)</span>となる.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0038\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>と各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^{T}\\)</span>に対し, 「<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)=1\\)</span>」という制約を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e,r}\\)</span>とし, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E,r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>全てに対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e,r}\\)</span>を集めたものを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0039\">この検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span>は, 変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e\\)</span>をオラクルとして実行する.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0040\">各制約「<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)=1\\)</span>」は, 検証者が高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>回のオラクルアクセスを行うことから, 高々<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>個の変数の値に依存する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0041\">すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSPである.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0042\">この<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>が所望の性質を満たすことを以下で確認する.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"jp-varphiのサイズ\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0043\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>のサイズ</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0044\">頂点（変数）の個数は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^b\\cdot\\lvert V\\rvert + \\lvert E\\rvert\\cdot(2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}) \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>であり, 制約の個数は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^T\\cdot\\lvert E\\rvert \\le 2^{\\operatorname{poly}(b)}\\cdot\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>である（検証者の時間計算量が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>に関して多項式で抑えられることから<span class=\"math inline\">\\(T\\le\\operatorname{poly}(b)\\)</span>である）.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"jp-充足割り当ての存在性\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0045\">充足割り当ての存在性</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0046\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の制約全てを満たす割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>が存在すると仮定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0047\">このとき, 各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\pi_u = \\operatorname{Had}_b(a(u))\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0048\">とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0049\">このとき, 各辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\((a(u),a(v))\\)</span>は辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>における制約を充足する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0050\">従って, 定理2.3.1より, ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span>が存在して, 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span>は受理する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0051\">このように構成された<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\pi_u)_{u\\in V}\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\pi_e)_{e\\in E}\\)</span>から定まる<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>の割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon X\\to \\{0,1\\}\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>の制約全てを充足する.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"jp-不満足値の性質\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0052\">不満足値の性質</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0053\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\ge 0.99\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>を示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0054\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>の任意の割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon X\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>を固定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0055\">各頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>が定める変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u\\)</span>に対する部分割り当ての値を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi&#39;_u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{b}\\)</span>とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0056\">元の制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>の各頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>を「<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi&#39;_u\\)</span>の復号化」, すなわち次で定める.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0057\">ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>が存在して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma)) \\le 0.01\\)</span>ならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)=\\sigma\\)</span>とする（このような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)</span>は存在するならば一意であることが演習問題4から保証されている）.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0058\">そうでないならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>は任意に選ぶ.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0059\">辺部分集合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\)</span>に寄与する辺の集合とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0060\">すなわち, 辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>であって, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a(u),a(v))\\)</span>が辺<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>における制約を充足しないようなものの集合とする.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0061\">ある<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)\\in c_e\\)</span>であって, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u))\\le0.01\\)</span>かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_v,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v))\\le0.01\\)</span>を満たすものが存在するならば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)=\\sigma_u\\)</span>かつ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(v)=\\sigma_v\\)</span>であるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\notin F\\)</span>となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0062\">対偶をとると, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in F\\)</span>と任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)\\in c_e\\)</span>に対して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u))&gt;0.01\\)</span>または<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_v,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v))&gt;0.01\\)</span>が成り立つことがわかる.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0063\">従って, 検証者<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(C_e)\\)</span>の性質（定理2.3.1）より, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in F\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\forall \\pi&#39;_e \\in \\{0,1\\}^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}},\\quad \\Pr_{r\\sim\\{0,1\\}^T}\\left[ V^{\\pi&#39;_u,\\pi&#39;_v,\\pi&#39;_e}(C_e;r)\\text{が拒否する} \\right] \\ge 0.99\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0064\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0065\">すなわち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in F\\)</span>に関する<span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^T\\)</span>個の制約のうち, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\cdot 2^T\\)</span>個の制約は割り当て<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u\\leftarrow \\pi&#39;_u,\\,\\pi_v\\leftarrow \\pi&#39;_v,\\,\\pi_e\\leftarrow \\pi&#39;_e\\)</span>に対して充足されない.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0066\">従って,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;\\varphi) &amp;= \\Pr_{e\\sim E,r\\sim\\{0,1\\}^T}\\left[ V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\text{が拒否する} \\right] \\\\\n&amp;= \\Pr_{e\\sim E,r\\sim\\{0,1\\}^T}\\left[ V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\text{が拒否する} \\mid e\\in F \\right]\\cdot\\Pr[e\\in F] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge 0.99\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\\\\n&amp;= 0.99\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0067\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0068\">最後に補題3.1.9を用いて, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSPを制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>に変換して証明が完了する.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理431の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0069\">定理4.3.1の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0070\">入力として受け取った制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>に対し, 補題4.3.2を用いて, アルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>上の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=\\langle(X,\\{0,1\\},\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\rangle\\)</span>を構成する（これは多項式時間で行える）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0071\">さらにこの<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSPインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>を, 補題3.1.9を用いて制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\{0,1\\}^{q_0},\\mathcal{C}&#39;&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>に変換する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0072\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>はこのようにして得られる制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>を出力する.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0073\">以下に<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>が所望の性質を持つことを確認する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0074\">まず, 補題4.3.2より, ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>は次を満たす.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0075\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert X\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0076\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0077\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi) \\ge 0.99\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0078\">このとき, 補題3.1.9より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>はアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^{q_0}\\)</span>上の制約グラフであって, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>のみに依存するある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;\\)</span>に対して次を満たす.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0079\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c&#39;q_0\\cdot (\\lvert X\\rvert + \\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0080\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0081\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)&gt;0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\frac{1}{q_0}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0082\">これらを組み合わせると次が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0083\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c\\cdot c&#39;q_0\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0084\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0 \\Rightarrow \\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\Rightarrow \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0085\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\frac{1}{q_0}\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi) \\ge \\frac{0.99}{q_0}\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"jp-44-ギャップ増幅補題とpcp定理の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0086\">4.4 ギャップ増幅補題とPCP定理の証明</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0087\">ギャップ増幅補題（補題4.1.1）は, べき乗によって<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>を増幅させ, それに伴い増幅したアルファベットを定理4.3.1によって削減することによって得られる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題411の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0088\">補題4.1.1の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0089\">入力として受け取った制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>に対し, 以下を適用する.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0090\">与えられた制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>を入力として補題3.3.1のアルゴリズムを実行し, その出力を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span>とする（エクスパンダー化）.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0091\">後で定めるパラメータ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_2 = G_1^\\ell\\)</span>とする（べき乗操作）.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0092\">定理4.3.1のアルゴリズムを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_2\\)</span>を入力として実行し, その出力<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_3\\)</span>を出力して終了する（アルファベット削減）.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0093\">ユニバーサルな定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d,\\lambda\\)</span>を補題3.3.1の定数とする.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0094\">また, べき乗操作のパラメータ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span>のみに依存し, 十分大きくとる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0095\">補題3.3.1, 補題4.2.2, 定理4.3.1より, 次が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0096\">最終的な制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_3\\)</span>のサイズは, 適当な<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert,\\ell)\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G_3) \\le c \\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0097\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3) = \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_2) = \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1) = 0\\)</span>である.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0098\">最後に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>を適切に選ぶことによって, 定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha:=\\lceil2/\\ell\\rceil\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3)\\ge \\min\\{ \\alpha, 2\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G) \\}\\)</span>にできることを示す.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0099\">まず, 補題3.3.1より, 制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_2\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-正則<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-エクスパンダーであり, さらにある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta_1\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1)\\ge \\beta_1\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0100\">従って, 補題4.2.2より, ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta_2=\\beta_2(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_2) &amp;\\ge \\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\beta_1\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\beta_1\\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0101\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0102\">最後に定理4.3.1より, ある定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma=\\gamma(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>に対して</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3) &amp;\\ge \\gamma\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_2) \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\gamma\\beta_1\\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0103\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0104\">このとき, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell = \\left\\lceil \\left(\\frac{2}{\\gamma\\beta_1\\beta_2}\\right)^2 \\right\\rceil\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha = \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\)</span>とすれば, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3)\\ge \\min\\{ \\alpha, 2\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G) \\}\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0105\">PCP定理を証明する全ての準備が整った.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-定理127の証明\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0106\">定理1.2.7の証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0107\">定理1.2.14を示せば, これと<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>のNP完全性を組み合わせると定理1.2.7が従う（演習問題2）.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0108\">また, 補題3.1.6より, 定理1.2.14と定理3.1.4は同値であるため, 以降では定理3.1.4の証明を与える.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0109\">すなわち, 与えられた<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span>に対して, 多項式時間で定理3.1.4の条件を満たす<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSPのインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>を構成する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0110\">これは以下のアルゴリズムによって構成される.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-アルゴリズム-441\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0111\">アルゴリズム 4.4.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0112\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>を次のように定める.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0113\">ただし入力として, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>のインスタンス<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span>を受け取る.</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0114\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSPとみなし, 補題3.1.9を用いて制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span>に変換する.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0115\">各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i=1,\\dots,\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil\\)</span>に対し, 制約グラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_i\\)</span>を入力として補題4.1.1のアルゴリズムを実行してその出力として得られるグラフを<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_{i+1}\\)</span>とする.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0116\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_{\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil}\\)</span>を出力して終了する.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0117\">元の問題<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>はアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>上の3-CSPであるため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span>のアルファベットは<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2^3\\)</span>である.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0118\">また, 最終的に得られる制約グラフのアルファベットは, 定理4.3.1のアルファベット<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0\\)</span>となる.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0119\">これらのアルファベットをユニバーサルなものとして固定する.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0120\">このとき, 適当な定数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>が存在して各<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i=1,\\dots,\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G_i) \\le c \\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G_{i-1})\\)</span>が成り立つため, 最終的な制約グラフのサイズは</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{size}(G_{\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil}) \\le c^{O(\\log s)} \\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G_0) = s^{O(1)}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0121\">が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0122\">また, 補題3.1.9と補題4.1.1より, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)=0\\)</span>ならば, 全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>について<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_i)=0\\)</span>が成り立つ.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0123\">最後に, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)&gt;0\\)</span>ならば<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)\\ge \\frac{1}{s}\\)</span>であり, 補題3.1.9より<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_0)\\ge \\frac{1}{3s}\\)</span>となり, さらに全ての<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>に対して<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_i) \\ge \\min\\{ 2\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_{i-1}), \\alpha \\}\\)</span>が成り立つため,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\left(G_{\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil}\\right) \\ge \\min\\left\\{ \\alpha, 2^{\\log_2 (3s)}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_0) \\right\\} \\ge \\alpha\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0124\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"jp-bibliography\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0125\">Bibliography</span></h1>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0126\">[AKS04] M. Agrawal, N. Kayal, and N. Saxena. “PRIMES is in P” en. In: <em>Annals of mathematics</em> 160 (2 Sept. 1, 2004), pp. 781-793. doi: 10.4007/annals.2004.160.781 (cit. on p. 13).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0127\">[ALMSS98] S. Arora, C. Lund, R. Motwani, M. Sudan, and M. Szegedy. “Proof verification and the hardness of approximation problems” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 45 (3 May 1, 1998), pp. 501-555. doi: 10.1145/278298.278306 (cit. on p. 7).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0128\">[AS98] S. Arora and S. Safra. “Probabilistic checking of proofs: a new characterization of NP” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 45 (1 Jan. 1, 1998), pp. 70-122. doi: 10.1145/273865.273901 (cit. on p. 7).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0129\">[Bab85] L. Babai. “Trading group theory for randomness” In: <em>Proceedings of the seventeenth annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing - STOC '85</em>. The seventeenth annual ACM symposium (Providence, Rhode Island, United States). New York, New York, USA: ACM Press, 1985, pp. 421-429. doi: 10.1145/22145.22192 (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0130\">[BLR93] M. Blum, M. Luby, and R. Rubinfeld. “Self-testing/correcting with applications to numerical problems” In: <em>Journal of Computer and System Sciences</em> 47 (3 1993), pp. 549-595. doi: 10.1016/0022-0000(93)90044-W (cit. on p. 29).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0131\">[Coo71] S. A. Cook. “The complexity of theorem-proving procedures” en. In: <em>Proceedings of the third annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing - STOC '71</em>. The third annual ACM symposium (Shaker Heights, Ohio, United States). New York, New York, USA: ACM Press, 1971. doi: 10.1145/800157.805047 (cit. on p. 17).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0132\">[Din07] I. Dinur. “The PCP theorem by gap amplification” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 54 (3 June 1, 2007), 12-es. doi: 10.1145/1236457.1236459 (cit. on p. 7).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0133\">[GMR85] S. Goldwasser, S. Micali, and C. Rackoff. “The knowledge complexity of interactive proof-systems” In: <em>Proceedings of the seventeenth annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing - STOC '85</em>. The seventeenth annual ACM symposium (Providence, Rhode Island, United States). New York, New York, USA: ACM Press, 1985, pp. 291-304. doi: 10.1145/22145.22178 (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0134\">[GMW86] O. Goldreich, S. Micali, and A. Wigderson. “Proofs that yield nothing but their validity and a methodology of cryptographic protocol design” en. In: <em>27th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (sfcs 1986)</em>. 27th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (sfcs 1986) (Toronto, ON, Canada). IEEE, Oct. 1986, pp. 174-187. doi: 10.1109/sfcs.1986.47 (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0135\">[Gol10] O. Goldreich. “Short Locally Testable Codes and Proofs: A Survey in Two Parts” In: <em>Property Testing: Current Research and Surveys</em>. Ed. by O. Goldreich. Berlin, Heidelberg: Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 2010, pp. 65-104. doi: 10.1007/978-3-642-16367-8_6 (cit. on p. 30).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0136\">[GS06] O. Goldreich and M. Sudan. “Locally testable codes and PCPs of almost-linear length” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 53 (4 July 1, 2006), pp. 558-655. doi: 10.1145/1162349.1162351 (cit. on p. 29).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0137\">[Kar72] R. M. Karp. “Reducibility among Combinatorial Problems” en. In: <em>Complexity of Computer Computations</em>. Boston, MA: Springer US, 1972, pp. 85-103. doi: 10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9 (cit. on pp. 17, 21).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0138\">[Lev73] L. A. Levin. “Universal Sequential Search Problems” In: <em>Problems of Information Transmission (translated from Problemy Peredachi Informatsii (Russian))</em> 9 (3 1973), pp. 115-116 (cit. on p. 17).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0139\">[LFKN02] C. Lund, L. Fortnow, H. Karloff, and N. Nisan. “Algebraic methods for interactive proof systems” In: <em>Proceedings [1990] 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science</em>. [1990] 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (St. Louis, MO, USA). IEEE Comput. Soc. Press, 2002, 2-10 vol. 1. doi: 10.1109/fscs.1990.89518 (cit. on p. 23).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0140\">[ODo] R. O'Donnell. <em>A history of the PCP Theorem</em>. url: <a href=\"https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse533/05au/pcp-history.pdf\">https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse533/05au/pcp-history.pdf</a> (visited on 05/28/2025) (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0141\">[RVW02] O. Reingold, S. Vadhan, and A. Wigderson. “Entropy Waves, the Zig-Zag Graph Product, and New Constant-Degree Expanders” In: <em>Annals of mathematics</em> 155 (1 Jan. 2002), p. 157. doi: 10.2307/3062153 (cit. on p. 59).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0142\">[Sha02] A. Shamir. “IP=PSPACE (interactive proof=polynomial space)” In: <em>Proceedings [1990] 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science</em>. 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (St. Louis, MO, USA). IEEE Comput. Soc. Press, 2002, 11-15 vol. 1. doi: 10.1109/fscs.1990.89519 (cit. on p. 23).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"jp-付録-a-付録\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0143\">付録 A 付録</span></h1>\n<h2 id=\"jp-a1-基本的な確率の不等式\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0144\">A.1 基本的な確率の不等式</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0145\">この節では, 証明の中で用いられる確率の不等式とその証明を述べる.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-a11markovの不等式\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0146\">補題 A.1.1（Markovの不等式）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0147\">任意の非負の確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>と任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(t&gt;0\\)</span>に対し,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X\\ge t] \\le \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[X]}{t}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0148\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-19\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0149\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0150\">ここでは簡単のため, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>の台<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Omega=\\operatorname{supp}(X)\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}\\)</span>は有限集合, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Omega\\rvert&lt;\\infty\\)</span>と仮定する.<a href=\"#jp-fn13\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"jp-fnref13\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>13</sup></a></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0152\">このとき,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[X] &amp;= \\sum_{x\\in \\Omega} x\\Pr[X=x] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\sum_{x\\in \\Omega,x\\ge t} x\\Pr[X=x] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\sum_{x\\in \\Omega,x\\ge t} t\\Pr[X=x] \\\\\n&amp;= t\\Pr[X\\ge t]\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0153\">を整理すると主張を得る.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-a12chebyshevの不等式\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0154\">補題 A.1.2（Chebyshevの不等式）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0155\">期待値<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]\\)</span>と分散<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{Var}[X]\\)</span>が存在する任意の確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>と任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(t&gt;0\\)</span>に対し,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[|X-\\mathbb{E}[X]|\\ge t] \\le \\frac{\\mathbf{Var}[X]}{t^2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0156\">が成り立つ.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-20\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0157\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0158\">確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2\\)</span>は非負の確率変数であるから, これにMarkovの不等式を適用すると</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr\\left[ \\lvert X - \\mathbb{E}[X]\\rvert \\ge t  \\right] &amp;= \\Pr\\left[ (X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2 \\ge t^2 \\right] \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\frac{\\mathbb{E}\\left[ (X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2 \\right]}{t^2} \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{\\mathbf{Var}[X]}{t^2}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0159\">を得る.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-補題-a13paley-zygmundの不等式\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0160\">補題 A.1.3（Paley-Zygmundの不等式）</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0161\">非負整数値をとる任意の確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>に対し,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X\\ge 1] \\ge \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[X]^2}{\\mathbb{E}[X^2]}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"jp-証明-21\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0162\">証明</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0163\">非負整数値をとる確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>に対し</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[X] &amp;= \\mathbb{E}[X\\cdot \\mathbf{1}_{X\\ge 1}] \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\sqrt{ \\mathbb{E}[X^2]\\cdot \\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{1}_{X\\ge 1}] } &amp;&amp; \\text{Cauchy-Schwarzの不等式}\\\\\n&amp;= \\sqrt{ \\mathbb{E}[X^2]\\cdot \\Pr[X\\ge 1] }.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0164\">両辺を二乗して整理すると主張を得る.</span></p>\n<section id=\"jp-footnotes\" class=\"footnotes footnotes-end-of-document\" role=\"doc-endnotes\">\n<hr />\n<ol>\n<li id=\"jp-fn1\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0021\">ひとまずPythonやC言語などで実装されたプログラムを考えれば良い。</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref1\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn2\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0044\">計算モデルによって一つ一つの計算ステップの定義は異なるが、原理的には1bitの演算や記憶領域への読み書きの回数と思えばよい。</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref2\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn3\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0084\">自然数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[\\lvert\\pi\\rvert]\\)</span>を指定するために<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log \\lvert\\pi\\rvert)\\)</span>ビットを定めなければならないため、一回の質問を行うたびに最悪の場合<span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log \\lvert\\pi\\rvert)\\)</span>時間かかる。</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref3\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn4\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0137\">このような<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\)</span>を原始元という。</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref4\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn5\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0005\">文脈によっては最適化問題や数え上げ問題といった, 判定問題ではない問題に対してもNP困難性の概念が自然に定義されることもあり, この講義でもPCP定理の応用を紹介する際に「最適化問題XはNP困難である」という言い方を用いる箇所がある.</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref5\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn6\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0115\">二つのグラフ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1=(V_1,E_1),G_2=(V_2,E_2)\\)</span>が同型であるとは, 全単射<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\phi\\colon V_1\\to V_2\\)</span>が存在して, 任意の<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V_1\\)</span>に対し, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in E_1\\)</span>と<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in E_2\\)</span>が同値であることをいう.</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref6\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn7\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0128\">このアイデアはくつ下が識別できるという主張のみを証明しており, それぞれのくつ下の色の情報を証明者に伝える必要がない.</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref7\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn8\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-fn-0001\">アルゴリズム<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n\\)</span>を入力として与えられているため, 多項式時間であることは計算量が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span>で抑えられることを意味する.</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref8\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn9\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0099\">制約グラフの表記に用いる括弧は形式的には本来 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(((V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\)</span> のようにすべきだが、可読性のためあえて外側の括弧を <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\langle\\cdot\\rangle\\)</span> としている。</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref9\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn10\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0103\">具体的には <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span> は多重集合であり、自己ループに対応する辺は <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u\\}\\)</span> と表す。</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref10\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn11\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0010\">自己ループの次数への寄与は<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>であることに留意されたい (文脈によってはこの寄与が<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>である場合もある).</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref11\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn12\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0026\">頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>が頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から距離<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>以内であるというのは, 頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>から開始し頂点<span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>に至る長さ<span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>以下の路が存在することをいう.</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref12\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"jp-fn13\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0151\">確率変数<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>のとりうる値の集合, すなわち<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{x\\colon \\Pr[X=x]&gt;0\\}\\)</span>を<span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>の台と呼び, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{supp}(X)\\)</span>と表す.</span><a href=\"#jp-fnref13\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n</ol>\n</section>","targetHtml":"<h1 id=\"en-proof-of-the-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0001\">Proof of the PCP Theorem</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0002\">Nobutaka Shimizu (Institute of Science Tokyo)</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-contents\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0003\">Contents</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0004\">Preface ... 5</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0005\">Chapter 1 Introduction ... 7</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0006\">1.1 Review of Complexity Theory ... 7</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0007\">1.2 Complexity of Verification ... 11</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0008\">1.3 Background of the PCP Theorem: Verifying Proofs Using Randomness ... 22</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0009\">Chapter 2 The Weak PCP Theorem ... 25</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0010\">2.1 Why Is Local Verification Possible? ... 25</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0011\">2.2 The Weak PCP Theorem and Its Proof ... 36</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0012\">2.3 A PCP Verifier for Assignments to Two-Input Circuits ... 43</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0013\">Chapter 3 Constraint Satisfaction Problems ... 47</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0014\">3.1 Definition of Constraint Satisfaction Problems ... 47</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0015\">3.2 Multigraphs and Expander Graphs ... 55</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0016\">3.3 Constant-Degree Expanderization of Constraint Graphs ... 60</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0017\">Chapter 4 Proof of the PCP Theorem ... 69</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0018\">4.1 Gap Amplification Lemma ... 69</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0019\">4.2 Powering a Constraint Graph ... 70</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0020\">4.3 Alphabet Reduction ... 79</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0021\">4.4 Proofs of the Gap Amplification Lemma and the PCP Theorem ... 84</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0022\">Appendix A Appendix ... 91</span>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"meta-0023\">A.1 Basic Probability Inequalities ... 91</span></li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"en-preface\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0001\">Preface</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0002\">These notes are the lecture notes for the intensive course \"Proof and Applications of the PCP Theorem,\" held by the Division of Mathematics and Mathematical Analysis, Graduate School of Science, Kyoto University.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0003\">Computational complexity means the amount of computational resources required to solve a problem, such as computation time, memory size, and the presence or amount of randomness or quantum resources, while computational complexity theory is the theory for determining the computational complexity of individual problems.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0004\">The PCP theorem is a result concerning the complexity required to verify decision problems, namely problems answered by Yes or No; briefly, when a proof claiming that a proposition is true is given as a string, one would ordinarily need to inspect every character to verify it, but the PCP theorem gives the surprising result that one can probabilistically verify whether the proposition is true by inspecting only part of the proof.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0005\">For example, consider the proposition that the system of linear equations <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Ax=b\\)</span> has a solution for a real matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> and a real vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0006\">If this proposition is true, one can present an actual solution <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> as a proof, but to verify that the proof is correct, the verifier must actually compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Ax\\)</span> and check that each component agrees with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0007\">The PCP theorem, however, says that by presenting a cleverly constructed proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, it can be verified correctly with probability 99% without inspecting every character of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> (here we consider probabilistic verification, meaning that the verifier uses randomness).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0008\">Thus, the PCP theorem's ability to infer a global structure using only local information is not merely theoretically interesting, but has broad applications in theoretical computer science, including constructing error-correcting codes, derandomizing probabilistic methods, and deriving limits on approximation ratios for optimization problems, and it remains one of the central research topics in complexity theory.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0009\">Despite this importance, to the author's knowledge at the time of writing, no literature offers comprehensive access in Japanese to a proof of the PCP theorem.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0010\">This course proves the PCP theorem, explains the basic concepts and techniques of complexity theory used in its proof, and also discusses applications of the theorem.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"en-chapter-1-introduction\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0011\">Chapter 1 Introduction</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0012\">This intensive course explains a fundamental result in complexity theory called the PCP theorem and gives its proof.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0013\">The PCP theorem was proved in 1998 by Arora and Safra, and by Arora, Lund, Motwani, Sudan, and Szegedy [AS98, ALMSS98].</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0014\">That proof ingeniously combined error-correcting codes based on algebraic methods and was difficult, but Dinur [Din07] subsequently gave a simpler proof.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0015\">This course presents the relatively simple proof by Dinur [Din07].</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0016\">Incidentally, Arora, Feige, Goldwasser, Lund, Lovász, Motwani, Safra, Sudan, and Szegedy, who made major contributions to the first proof of the PCP theorem and its applications, received the Gödel Prize in 2001, while Dinur, who greatly simplified the proof, received it in 2019.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-11-review-of-complexity-theory\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0017\">1.1 Review of Complexity Theory</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0018\">We first define basic terminology found in any textbook on complexity theory.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0019\">Readers familiar with these terms may begin at Section 1.2.2.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0020\">These notes omit the definition of an algorithm (the definition of a Turing machine) and describe concrete computational procedures when discussing algorithms,<a href=\"#en-fn1\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref1\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>1</sup></a> but briefly address it in Claim 1.2.12.</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0022\">However, all numbers inside a computer are represented as finite binary strings, and reading, writing, and operating on them takes at least time proportional to their number of digits.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0023\">Although irrational numbers such as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sqrt{2}\\)</span> would properly be handled through finite truncated approximations, this course does not treat such decimals and considers only integer values unless otherwise stated.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0024\">We first define basic notation:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0025\">Asymptotic notation: for two functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f,g\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=O(g(n))\\)</span> means that some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> exists such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)\\le cg(n)\\)</span> for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0026\">The notations <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=\\Omega(g(n)), f(n)=o(g(n)), f(n)=\\omega(g(n))\\)</span>, and so forth are defined similarly (as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\to\\infty\\)</span>).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0027\">For a natural number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\([n]=\\{1,\\dots,n\\}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0028\">For a finite set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span>, writing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\sim S\\)</span> means that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is chosen uniformly at random from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0029\">Every finite set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S=\\{s_1,\\dots,s_m\\}\\)</span> appearing in these notes is implicitly equipped with an order <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s_1&lt;s_2&lt;\\dots&lt;s_m\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0030\">Whenever considering a nonempty subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T=\\{t_1,\\dots,t_k\\}\\subseteq S\\)</span>, we assume <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t_1&lt;\\dots&lt;t_k\\)</span> according to this order.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0031\">For example, the vertex set of a graph has a fixed order, and when considering an undirected edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\)</span> we <em>always</em> assume <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u&lt;v\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0032\">For a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in S^n\\)</span> over a set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I=\\{i_1,\\dots,i_\\ell\\}\\subseteq[n]\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_I=(x_{i_1},\\dots,x_{i_\\ell}) \\in S^{\\ell}\\)</span> to be the restriction of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0033\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^*=\\bigcup_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> denote all finite binary strings.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0034\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span> denote the length of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0035\">For an algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\)</span> denote the output of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> on input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x \\in \\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0036\">Here, \"algorithm\" without qualification means a deterministic algorithm; randomized algorithms are mentioned explicitly.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0037\">Consider a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0038\">If, for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>, the maximum number of computational steps before <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\)</span> is at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>, then algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> is said to have complexity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0039\">In particular, an algorithm whose complexity is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n^c)\\)</span> for some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> is called a <em>polynomial-time algorithm</em>.<a href=\"#en-fn2\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref2\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>2</sup></a></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0040\">Following conventional complexity-theory notation, we write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=n^{O(1)}\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span> when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)\\)</span> is polynomial in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>, meaning that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(n)=O(n^c)\\)</span> for some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0041\">Thus, a polynomial-time algorithm is one whose running time on every length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span> for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0042\">An algorithm receiving two strings <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> is written <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x,y)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0043\">For three or more inputs, we similarly write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x,y,z)\\)</span>, and so on.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0045\">For questions of polynomial-time computation, many classical computational models are equivalent.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0046\">Among the most fundamental problems in complexity theory are those called decision problems.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-111-decision-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0047\">Definition 1.1.1 (Decision Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0048\">A subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> is called a <em>decision problem (or language)</em>, and an input given to an algorithm is called an <em>instance</em> of problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0049\">An instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> is called a <em>Yes instance</em> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>, and a <em>No instance</em> otherwise.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0050\">For a string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> as the indicator of whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)=1\\)</span> if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)=0\\)</span> otherwise.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0051\">An algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> is said to solve <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)=L(x)\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-example-112-graph-connectivity-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0052\">Example 1.1.2 (Graph Connectivity Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0053\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A \\in \\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert V\\rvert\\times\\lvert V\\rvert}\\)</span> be the adjacency matrix of a graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0054\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{str}(A)\\)</span> be the representation of this matrix as a binary string of length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert^2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0055\">Namely, the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>th bit of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{str}(A)\\)</span> is the value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(i,j)\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i=\\lceil k/\\lvert V\\rvert\\rceil+1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(j=k\\bmod \\lvert V\\rvert+1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0056\">Then</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nL = \\{ \\mathsf{str}(A)\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert V\\rvert^2} \\colon \\text{$A$ is the adjacency matrix of a connected graph $G$} \\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0057\">is the decision problem of determining whether a given graph is connected.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0058\">In this course, \"efficiently solvable\" means solvable by a polynomial-time algorithm.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0059\">The class of such decision problems is called P.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-113-class-p\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0060\">Definition 1.1.3 (Class P)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0061\">A decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> belongs to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>, written <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L \\in \\mathsf{P}\\)</span>, if there exists a polynomial-time algorithm that solves it.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-114-input-format\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0062\">Remark 1.1.4 (Input Format)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0063\">Example 1.1.2 represented a graph's adjacency matrix as a binary input string, but graphs can also be represented in other ways, such as adjacency lists.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0064\">However, representing a graph by an adjacency matrix or an adjacency list is equivalent with respect to <em>polynomial-time solvability</em>, because conversion between the formats can be performed in polynomial time.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0065\">Thus, strictly speaking, defining a problem requires specifying its input format, such as an adjacency matrix or adjacency list, but whenever conversion between formats is trivially polynomial-time, this course omits such specifications and describes, for example, graph connectivity as \"the problem of determining whether a given graph is connected.\"</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0066\">We next define randomized algorithms.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0067\">Briefly, a randomized algorithm performs coin tosses internally.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0068\">Here, we call an algorithm randomized when it explicitly receives a random seed.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-115-randomized-algorithm\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0069\">Definition 1.1.5 (Randomized Algorithm)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0070\">An algorithm that receives, in addition to input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, another string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> called a random seed (random table) is called a <em>randomized algorithm</em>, and is written <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x;s)\\)</span> to emphasize the random seed.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0071\">We assume that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x;s)\\)</span> halts in finite time for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,s\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> and that its complexity is a function only of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0072\">The length of the random seed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> is always bounded above by the complexity of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0073\">Thus, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> has complexity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span>, then for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> reads at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T(n)\\)</span> characters of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>, so we assume <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in \\{0,1\\}^{T(n)}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0074\">When the random seed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> need not be shown explicitly, we sometimes write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0075\">For probabilities, expectations, and variances over a randomized algorithm's random seed, we use <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_{A}[\\cdot],\\mathbb{E}_A[\\cdot],\\operatorname{Var}_A[\\cdot]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0076\">A randomized algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> solves a decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> if</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_A[A(x)=L(x)]\\geq 2/3\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0077\">holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0078\">We also consider algorithms that receive oracle access to a string in addition to their input.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-116-oracle-algorithm\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0079\">Definition 1.1.6 (Oracle Algorithm)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0080\">For a string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, an algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span> with <em>oracle access</em> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> can read a character at a specified position of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> during its computation.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0081\">That is, it can read the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>th character of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> in time <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log \\lvert\\pi\\rvert)\\)</span> during the computation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0082\">The index <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span> is called a <em>query</em>.<a href=\"#en-fn3\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref3\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>3</sup></a></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0083\">Randomized oracle algorithms are defined similarly.</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0085\">An oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span> is <em>adaptive</em> if the query <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span> of each oracle access depends on the contents of previous oracle accesses, and <em>nonadaptive</em> otherwise.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0086\">This course considers only nonadaptive oracle algorithms.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-12-complexity-of-verification\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0087\">1.2 Complexity of Verification</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0088\">In mathematics generally, verification means checking whether a given proof claiming that a proposition is true actually proves that proposition correctly.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0089\">It may help to imagine refereeing a paper or grading a proof on a written examination.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0090\">Verification and its complexity are important research topics in complexity theory, where the complexity required for verification is studied.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0091\">See Section 1.3 for more detailed background.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-121-efficient-verification-and-class-np\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0092\">1.2.1 Efficient Verification and Class NP</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0093\">Given a decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> and input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, we wish to determine whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0094\">Suppose a proof claiming <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span> is given as a string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0095\">An algorithm called a <em>verifier</em> reads <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and determines whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0096\">The class of decision problems for which this determination can be made in polynomial time is called <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-121-class-np\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0097\">Definition 1.2.1 (Class NP)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0098\">A decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> belongs to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> if there exist a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> and polynomial <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0099\">Algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> receives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> and outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0100\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>, some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^{p(\\lvert x\\rvert)}\\)</span> exists such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0101\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> is said to <em>accept</em> input <span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,\\pi)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0102\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\notin L\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=0\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^{p(\\lvert x\\rvert)}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0103\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> is said to <em>reject</em> input <span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,\\pi)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0104\">Such an algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> is called an <em>NP verifier</em>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> an <em>NP proof</em>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-122-relationship-between-classes-p-and-np\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0105\">Remark 1.2.2 (Relationship Between Classes P and NP)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0106\">If a decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> belongs to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0107\">Indeed, use a verifier that computes and outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L(x)\\)</span> on received input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0108\">Thus <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\subseteq\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0109\">Whether the reverse inclusion <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\subseteq\\mathsf{P}\\)</span> holds is the P vs NP problem, the most important open problem in complexity theory, and many researchers believe that it does not hold.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-example-123-composite-number-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0110\">Example 1.2.3 (Composite Number Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0111\">Consider <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L=\\{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\colon x\\text{ is composite}\\}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0112\">The verifier reads <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and determines whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\notin\\{1,x\\}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> divides <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0113\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>, it is composite, so providing a nontrivial divisor as proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> yields <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0114\">Otherwise no nontrivial divisor exists, so necessarily <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x,\\pi)=0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0115\">Because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> runs in time polynomial in the input length, namely the bit length of the binary representation, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> belongs to NP.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-example-124-graph-coloring-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0116\">Example 1.2.4 (Graph Coloring Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0117\">For a natural number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\ge 2\\)</span> and graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>, if a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\colon V\\to[k]\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c(u)\\neq c(v)\\)</span> for every edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-coloring of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, and a graph possessing a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-coloring is called <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-colorable.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0118\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\ge 2\\)</span>, the decision problem</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nL=\\{G\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\colon G\\text{ is }k\\text{-colorable}\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0119\">belongs to NP.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0120\">The verifier reads <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and determines whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-coloring of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0121\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\in L\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-colorable, so providing a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-coloring as proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> yields <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(G,\\pi)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0122\">Otherwise <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is not <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-colorable, so necessarily <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(G,\\pi)=0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0123\">Because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> runs in polynomial time, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> belongs to NP.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0124\">In Example 1.2.3, we saw that a verifier can verify in polynomial time that a given number is composite by receiving a nontrivial divisor as a witness.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0125\">Can we present a witness that a given number is prime?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0126\">Interestingly, this is indeed possible.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-1-primality-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0127\">Exercise 1 (Primality Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0128\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, denote its binary representation by <span class=\"math inline\">\\((n)_2\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0129\">Consider <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PRIMES}=\\{(a)_2\\in\\mathbb{N}\\colon a\\text{ is prime}\\}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0130\">This problem is known to belong to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{P}\\)</span> [AKS04], but we wish to prove <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PRIMES}\\in \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> elementarily without using that complicated algorithm.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0131\">For this purpose, use the following facts:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0132\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>, the sequence <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots \\pmod a\\)</span> is periodic.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0133\">Furthermore:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0134\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> is prime, some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span> exists for which <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots \\pmod a\\)</span> has period <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span>.<a href=\"#en-fn4\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref4\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>4</sup></a></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0135\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> is not prime, the period of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots \\pmod a\\)</span> is less than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0136\">In particular, its period <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> divides <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0138\">Using these facts, answer the following questions.</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0139\">Consider verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_1\\)</span>: on input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>, it checks every value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma^0,\\gamma^1,\\dots,\\gamma^{a-2} \\pmod a\\)</span> and determines whether they are all distinct.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0140\">Briefly explain why <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_1\\)</span> is <em>not</em> a polynomial-time algorithm.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0141\">For input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, recursively provide as a proof that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> is prime a primitive root <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\in\\{1,\\dots,a-1\\}\\)</span>, the prime factorization <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a-1=p_1^{\\alpha_1}\\cdots p_k^{\\alpha_k}\\)</span>, and proofs that each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_i\\)</span> is prime.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0142\">Using this proof, show that verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_2\\)</span> can determine in polynomial time whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> is prime.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"en-122-local-verification-and-class-pcp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0143\">1.2.2 Local Verification and Class PCP</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0144\">In general, verifying whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span> requires reading every character of proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0145\">Sometimes, however, one can <em>probabilistically</em> determine whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span> by reading only some characters of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0146\">A proof with this property is called a <em>probabilistically checkable proof</em> (PCP).</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-125-probabilistically-checkable-proof\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0147\">Definition 1.2.5 (Probabilistically Checkable Proof)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0148\">For two functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r,q\\colon\\mathbb{N}\\to\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span> be the class of decision problems <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> with the following property: there exists a polynomial-time randomized oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> such that, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0149\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>, some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> exists such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=1\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> over the randomness of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> (<em>completeness</em>);</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0150\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\notin L\\)</span>, then for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=0\\)</span> with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> over the randomness of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> (<em>soundness</em>);</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0151\">and if the input length is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n=\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)\\)</span> reads at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(q(n))\\)</span> characters of oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, while its random-seed length is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(r(n))\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0152\">Such a randomized oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> is called a <em>PCP verifier</em>, and the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is called a <em>PCP</em>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0153\">Consider journal peer review as an example.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0154\">Input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is the claim of the paper under review, and the string representing its proof is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0155\">A PCP verifier probabilistically determines whether the claim is correct by reading only <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> characters of the proof.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0156\">Ordinarily, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\ll \\lvert\\pi\\rvert\\)</span>, this seems impossible.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0157\">Yet tasting food while cooking determines its flavor by eating a small random portion, and in a sense resembles a PCP verifier.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/PCPverifier.png\" alt=\"Illustration of a PCP verifier\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0158\">Figure 1.1: Illustration of a PCP verifier.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-126-length-of-a-pcp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0159\">Remark 1.2.6 (Length of a PCP)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0160\">The length of proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span> is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)2^{r(n)}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0161\">For each random seed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s \\in \\{0,1\\}^{r(n)}\\)</span>, the verifier reads at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)\\)</span> characters of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, so enumerating all random seeds shows that at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)2^{r(n)}\\)</span> characters of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> can possibly be accessed.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0162\">In general, a PCP verifier is considered better when its random-seed length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)\\)</span> and number of characters read <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)\\)</span> are smaller.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0163\">Constructing PCP verifiers is extremely difficult.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0164\">For example, consider the following verifier for the graph-coloring problem in Example 1.2.4:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0165\">It receives a graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span> as input and a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon V\\to[k]\\)</span> as proof, and determines whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is actually a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-coloring of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0166\">The verifier chooses an edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> uniformly at random and decides according to whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(u)\\neq \\pi(v)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0167\">This verifier reads at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q(n)=O(1)\\)</span> characters of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, and its random-seed length is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)=O(\\log n)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0168\">However, this verifier does not satisfy soundness.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0169\">Indeed, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is not <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-colorable, then for every function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon V \\to [k]\\)</span> there is at least one edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(u)=\\pi(v)\\)</span>, but in the worst case the verifier selects such an edge with probability only <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0170\">The PCP theorem guarantees that every decision problem in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> admits PCP verification using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span> random bits and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> oracle accesses.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-127-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0171\">Theorem 1.2.7 (PCP Theorem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0172\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)=\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0173\">For example, the graph-coloring problem belongs to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0174\">The PCP theorem shows that it can be probabilistically verified by reading only <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> characters of the proof.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-128-one-sided-inclusion\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0175\">Remark 1.2.8 (One-Sided Inclusion)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0176\">In the PCP theorem, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)\\subseteq \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> is easy to prove.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0177\">Indeed, by Remark 1.2.6, the length of proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> for a PCP verifier using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(r(n))\\)</span> random bits and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(q(n))\\)</span> oracle accesses is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(q(n))2^{O(r(n))} = n^{O(1)}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0178\">Moreover, because the PCP verifier uses <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span> random bits, one can enumerate all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{O(r(n))}=n^{O(1)}\\)</span> possible random seeds, apply the PCP verifier to each, and take a majority vote of their outputs, thereby performing NP verification in polynomial time of whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span> for input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0179\">The essential difficulty in proving the PCP theorem lies in proving the reverse inclusion <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\subseteq \\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-12-np-completeness-and-the-cook-levin-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0001\">1.2 NP-Completeness and the Cook-Levin Theorem</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0002\">The property of being at least as hard as every problem in the class <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> is called <strong>NP-hardness</strong>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0003\">A problem in NP that is NP-hard is called <strong>NP-complete</strong>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0004\">Here we define NP-hardness and NP-completeness for decision problems.<a href=\"#en-fn5\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref5\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>5</sup></a></span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0006\">In that case, it means that if a polynomial-time algorithm for solving optimization problem X is used, then every problem in NP can be solved in polynomial time.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-129-np-completeness\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0007\">Definition 1.2.9 (NP-Completeness)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0008\">A decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> is called <strong>NP-complete</strong> if, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>, there exists a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> satisfying the following (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> may depend on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>):</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0009\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> denote the output of algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> when given a string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> as input.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0010\">Then, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L&#39;\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A(x)\\in L\\)</span> are equivalent.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0011\">Such an algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> is also called a <strong>Karp reduction</strong> from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-1210-meaning-of-at-least-as-hard-as-np\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0012\">Remark 1.2.10 (Meaning of \"At Least as Hard as NP\")</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0013\">If there is a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0\\)</span> that solves an NP-complete decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>, then for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\in \\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> there is a polynomial-time algorithm that solves <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0014\">Indeed, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> is a Karp reduction from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span>, whether a given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span> can be decided by first computing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y=A(x)\\)</span> and then computing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0(y)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0015\">In this sense, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> can be said to be at least as hard as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0016\">However, when comparing the difficulty of two problems, it is not always necessary to insist on a Karp reduction.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0017\">Indeed, the algorithm above uses the algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0\\)</span> for solving <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> only once, and its output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0(y)\\in\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> directly agrees with the answer to whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L&#39;\\)</span>; but to show that \"<span class=\"math inline\">\\(L&#39;\\)</span> can also be solved if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> can be solved,\" one may compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0(y_1),\\dots,A_0(y_m)\\)</span> for multiple inputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_1,\\dots,y_m\\)</span>, and one may combine those outputs to decide whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0018\">A reduction that permits such flexible operations while ensuring that the entire computation runs in polynomial time whenever <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A_0\\)</span> does is called a <strong>Turing reduction</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0019\">The framework of NP-completeness, including Karp reductions, was specifically established in [Kar72], but even earlier [Coo71] had shown, using a reduction somewhat different from a Karp reduction, that a problem called the <strong>circuit satisfiability decision problem</strong> is NP-complete.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0020\">Although the circuit satisfiability decision problem concerns Boolean circuits and at first glance seems unrelated to graphs and the like, [Kar72] used the fact that the proof of [Coo71] also works under Karp reductions to prove the NP-hardness of various natural combinatorial optimization problems, including the maximum clique problem, Hamiltonian cycle problem, computation of chromatic number, and knapsack problem.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0021\">The problems that [Kar72] proved NP-complete are now called <strong>Karp's 21 NP-complete problems</strong>, and this work led Cook and Karp to receive the Turing Award in 1982 and 1985, respectively.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0022\">It was also discovered that [Lev73] had independently shown a similar result (the fact that the paper was published during the Cold War unfortunately delayed recognition of the difference), and Levin received the Knuth Prize in 2012.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0023\">For this reason, the theorem below establishing the NP-completeness of the circuit satisfiability decision problem defined there is called the <strong>Cook-Levin theorem</strong>, and it is one of the most important theorems in computational complexity theory, forming the foundation of the entire theory of NP-completeness.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-1211-cook-levin-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0024\">Theorem 1.2.11 (Cook-Levin Theorem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0025\">The problem that takes a Boolean circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> as input and decides whether there exists an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span> is called the <strong>circuit satisfiability decision problem</strong> (Circuit SAT).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0026\">The circuit satisfiability decision problem is NP-complete.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0027\">The Cook-Levin theorem is proved by simulating a verifier for the class NP with a nondeterministic Turing machine and representing that machine as a circuit.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0028\">In other words, the fact that circuit computation can simulate a Turing machine is the essential property for completeness, and it can be stated as follows:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-1212-circuits-simulating-turing-machines\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0029\">Claim 1.2.12 (Circuits Simulating Turing Machines)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0030\">For every polynomial-time Turing machine <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span>, there exists a circuit family <span class=\"math inline\">\\((C_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\)</span> such that the following hold:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0031\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_n\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> contains <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span> gates (AND, OR, NOT).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0032\">Here, every gate takes two inputs and has one output.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0033\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span> accepts <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> if and only if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_n(x)=1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0034\">Each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_n\\)</span> can be constructed in time <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0035\">Proving Claim 1.2.12 also requires a rigorous definition of a Turing machine, which will not be treated in detail in this lecture, but an outline is given below.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/Turing.png\" alt=\"An example of a Turing machine. In each state, it follows an enabled edge labeled by the value under the head, rewrites the tape cell if necessary, and then moves the head one cell right, one cell left, or leaves it in place.\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0036\">Figure 1.2: An example of a Turing machine.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0037\">In each state, it transitions along an enabled edge labeled by the value on the memory tape indicated by the head, rewrites the value on the memory tape at the head position if necessary, and finally moves the head one position right, one position left, or leaves it in place.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-outline\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0038\">Proof Outline.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0039\">A Turing machine is a finite-state machine equipped with an infinitely long <strong>memory tape</strong>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0040\">A <strong>memory tape</strong> is an infinitely long sequence of symbols; for an input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, its first <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert x\\rvert\\)</span> symbols initially contain <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>, and all other symbols are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bot\\)</span> (a symbol denoting a blank).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0041\">There is also a pointer called the <strong>head</strong>, which indicates the position on the memory tape whose symbol is currently read or written.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0042\">At each step, the machine refers to its current state and the symbol indicated by the head and determines the next state, what to write at the current head position, and the direction in which to move the head (left, right, or not at all).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0043\">The machine also has two distinct special states, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{accept}}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{reject}}\\)</span>, and the computation ends upon transitioning to either one.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0044\">The output of the Turing machine (accept or reject) is determined by its state at termination (see Figure 1.2).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0045\">The number of steps required before the computation terminates is called the <strong>(time) complexity</strong> of this Turing machine.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0046\">Suppose that the Turing machine <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span> runs in polynomial time.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0047\">Then, for every sufficiently large <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>, the contents of the memory tape at any point in the computation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span> (the number of symbols other than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\bot\\)</span>) can be represented by at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span> symbols.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0048\">Moreover, the position indicated by the head can be represented by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span> bits, and the number of states is at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0049\">Therefore, the state of the Turing machine at any point, including the contents of the memory tape (for convenience, call this the configuration), can be represented by at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span> bits.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0050\">Moreover, rewriting the memory tape changes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> bit, and changes to the head position can be computed using addition and subtraction, so each step of the Turing machine's computation can also be simulated by a small circuit.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0051\">That is, one can represent it as a circuit by preparing a circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_i\\)</span> that takes the current configuration as input and outputs the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>-th bit of the configuration, and placing these circuits in parallel for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[n^{O(1)}]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0052\">Placing these circuits in sequence for all steps yields a polynomial-size circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> that simulates the Turing machine <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\)</span> (that is, a circuit deciding whether the final state of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M(x)\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{accept}}\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_{\\mathrm{reject}}\\)</span>).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0053\">We use this to prove the Cook-Levin theorem.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-theorem-1211\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0054\">Proof of Theorem 1.2.11</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0055\">By the definition of NP, for every decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> there exists a polynomial-time NP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x;\\pi)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0056\">By Claim 1.2.12, there exists a circuit family <span class=\"math inline\">\\((C_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\)</span> that simulates this NP verifier.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0057\">In particular, given an input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>, a circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_x\\colon\\{0,1\\}^{\\lvert\\pi\\rvert}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> simulating the Turing machine <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x;\\cdot)\\)</span> can be constructed in polynomial time.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0058\">It remains only to verify that this is a Karp reduction from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> to the circuit satisfiability decision problem.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0059\">Indeed, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is a Yes instance, then there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(x;\\pi)=1\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_x\\)</span> is satisfiable.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0060\">On the other hand, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is a No instance, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_x\\)</span> is not satisfiable.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0061\">Thus, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\mapsto C_x\\)</span> is indeed a Karp reduction from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\)</span> to the circuit satisfiability decision problem.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0062\">Using the Cook-Levin theorem, one can show that the satisfiability decision problem for systems of quadratic equations given below is NP-complete:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-1213-satisfiability-decision-problem-for-systems-of-quadratic-equations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0063\">Theorem 1.2.13 (Satisfiability Decision Problem for Systems of Quadratic Equations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0064\">Define the decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{QuadEq}\\)</span> as follows:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0065\">We are given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span> quadratic functions in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> variables, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1,\\dots,f_m\\colon \\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0066\">Decide whether there exists an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1(x)=\\dots=f_m(x)=0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0067\">In particular, when for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[m]\\)</span> the value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_i(x_1,\\dots,x_n)\\)</span> depends only on the values of at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> variables <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{i_1},\\dots,x_{i_k}\\)</span>, the problem is called <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{QuadEq}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0068\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span> is NP-complete.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0069\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0070\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span> belongs to NP.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0071\">Indeed, one can provide a solution <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> to the system as an NP witness, and whether it actually satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1(x)=\\dots=f_m(x)=0\\)</span> can be decided in time <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span> (because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1,\\dots,f_m\\)</span> are given as input, their length when represented as a binary string is at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0072\">Next, we construct a Karp reduction from the circuit satisfiability decision problem to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0073\">Given an input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> to the circuit satisfiability decision problem, let its input variables be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0074\">For each gate, introduce a new variable corresponding to that gate, and denote these variables by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{n+1},\\dots,x_{n+s}\\)</span> (where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> is the number of non-input gates in the circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0075\">For each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[s]\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i,x_j\\)</span> be the variables corresponding to the inputs of the gate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span> corresponding to the variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{n+k}\\)</span> (if the gate is a NOT gate, consider only <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span>), and suppose further that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j &lt; n+k\\)</span> (number the gates in order of proximity to the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> so that this holds).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0076\">Define the function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\colon \\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> as follows: for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[s]\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_k(x) = \\begin{cases}\nx_k - x_i x_j &amp; \\text{if gate $c$ is an AND gate}, \\\\\nx_k - (x_i + x_j - x_i x_j) &amp; \\text{if gate $c$ is an OR gate}, \\\\\nx_k - (1-x_i) &amp; \\text{if gate $c$ is a NOT gate}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0077\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k=s+1\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_{s+1}(x) = 1 - x_{n+s}\\)</span> (see Figure 1.3).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0078\">Then, because each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\)</span> depends on at most three variables, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in[s+1]}\\)</span> is an instance of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span> and can be constructed in polynomial time when the circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> is given as input.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0079\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\le s\\)</span>, the constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(x)=0\\)</span> forces the output of the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-th gate to be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_k\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0080\">Therefore, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(x)=0\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[s]\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{n+s}\\)</span> agrees with the output of the circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> on input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0081\">Finally, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_{s+1}(x)=0\\iff x_{n+s}=1\\)</span>, this means that the input satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x_1,\\dots,x_n)=1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/circuit_to_3-QUADEQ.png\" alt=\"Example of a Karp reduction from circuit satisfiability to 3-QuadEq. The left-hand side minus the right-hand side gives the corresponding f_k.\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0082\">Figure 1.3: An example of a Karp reduction from the circuit satisfiability decision problem to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0083\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\text{left-hand side})-(\\text{right-hand side})\\)</span> is the corresponding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0084\">Finally, we show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> is a Yes instance of the circuit satisfiability decision problem if and only if <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in[s+1]}\\)</span> is a Yes instance of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0085\">To distinguish them, let the input to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>, and let the input to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k\\)</span> be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x} \\in \\{0,1\\}^{n+s}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0086\">First, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> is a Yes instance, then there exists an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0087\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span> is constructed by assigning the output value of each intermediate gate in the computation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)\\)</span> directly to the corresponding variable, then it indeed satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(\\overline{x})=0\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0088\">Conversely, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)\\)</span> is a Yes instance, then setting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x=(\\overline{x}_1,\\dots,\\overline{x}_{n})\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0089\">Therefore, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\mapsto (f_k)_{k\\in[s+1]}\\)</span> is indeed a Karp reduction from the circuit satisfiability decision problem to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0090\">Using the Cook-Levin theorem, as in [Kar72], one can establish NP-completeness for various problems that at first glance appear entirely unrelated to circuits.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0091\">For example, the 3-coloring problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3COL}\\)</span> is NP-complete.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0092\">To prove the PCP theorem, it would suffice to construct the desired PCP verifier for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(L\\in\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span>, but in fact it suffices to fix one NP-complete problem (such as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{QuadEq}\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3COL}\\)</span>) and construct a PCP verifier for it.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-1214-pcp-verifier-for-systems-of-quadratic-equations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0093\">Theorem 1.2.14 (PCP Verifier for Systems of Quadratic Equations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0094\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{3\\text{-}QuadEq}\\in \\mathsf{PCP}(\\log n,1)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-2\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0095\">Exercise 2</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0096\">Assuming Theorem 1.2.14 and Theorem 1.2.13, prove the PCP theorem (Theorem 1.2.7).</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-13-background-of-the-pcp-theorem-verifying-proofs-using-randomness\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0097\">1.3 Background of the PCP Theorem: Verifying Proofs Using Randomness</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0098\">The PCP theorem has a remarkable application to combinatorial optimization: the hardness of designing approximation algorithms.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0099\">Originally, however, its background lay in a transformation in how the concept of \"proof verification\" was understood in computational complexity theory.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0100\">Most of the content of this section is based on [ODo], to which the reader is referred for details.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0101\">As is well known, Definition 1.2.1 captures the property that a claim regarded as true has a witness sufficient to establish it, and that this witness can also be verified efficiently (in polynomial time).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0102\">For example, the claim \"graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is 3-colorable\" can be demonstrated by presenting an actual 3-coloring as a proof.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0103\">On the other hand, can one present such a proof for the claim \"graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is not 3-colorable\"?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0104\">According to the important conjecture <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\ne\\mathsf{coNP}\\)</span> in computational complexity theory, such a proof cannot be presented, but the conjecture itself has not been proved.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0105\">Against this background, new notions of proof, such as <strong>interactive proofs</strong>, <strong>zero-knowledge proofs</strong>, and <strong>Arthur-Merlin proofs</strong>, were proposed independently and around the same time by several research groups [GMR85, Bab85].</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0106\">An interactive proof extends the one-round process in which a prover presents a proof and a verifier reads it to a polynomial number of rounds, while also using randomization to verify the proof probabilistically.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0107\">Intuitively, it resembles peer review at an academic journal: the author of a paper (the prover) and the reviewer (the verifier) repeatedly exchange questions and answers and ultimately confirm the correctness of the paper; the verifier is allowed to toss coins, and a genuinely correct proof is accepted with high probability, while an incorrect one is rejected with high probability.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0108\">The class of decision problems that a polynomial-time verifier can verify probabilistically through a polynomial-round conversation with a prover of unlimited computational power is called <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{IP}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0109\">Here, a prover is a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\colon \\{0,1\\}^*\\to\\{0,1\\}^*\\)</span>; the verifier sends a question <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\operatorname{poly}(n)}\\)</span> to the prover, and the prover returns <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P(q)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0110\">The running time for computing the function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\mapsto P(q)\\)</span> need not be polynomial.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0111\">When deterministic verification without coin tosses is considered, it is known to be equivalent to NP verification, so the gap between interactive proofs and NP proofs fundamentally depends on randomness.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0112\">Thus, interactive proofs were initially thought not to increase proving power very much, and according to [ODo], the aforementioned papers appear to have had difficulty gaining acceptance in theoretical computer science.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0113\">A sign that this situation was changing was the construction in [GMW86] of a zero-knowledge proof for the <strong>graph non-isomorphism decision problem (GNI)</strong>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0114\">In this problem, a pair of graphs <span class=\"math inline\">\\((G_1,G_2)\\)</span> is a Yes instance if the graphs are <strong>non-isomorphic</strong>, and a No instance if they are isomorphic.<a href=\"#en-fn6\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref6\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>6</sup></a></span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0116\">The claim \"two graphs are isomorphic\" can be verified by supplying an isomorphism, but how can one prove the impossibility expressed by \"two graphs are non-isomorphic\"?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0117\">An intuitive explanation of the proof follows:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0118\">Consider proving the concept of color to a blind person.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0119\">More concretely, suppose there are two socks, sock 1 and sock 2; how can one convince a blind person named <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> that their colors differ?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0120\">First, give both socks to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0121\">Out of the prover's sight, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> tosses a coin, randomly chooses one of the socks, shows it to the prover, and asks for its number.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0122\">The prover answers with the sock's number, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> checks whether the answer agrees with the description given beforehand.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0123\">If both socks have the same color, the prover cannot distinguish them and can only guess.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0124\">The probability of a correct answer in this case is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/2\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0125\">If the two socks have different colors, the prover can determine the sock's number from its color.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0126\">The probability of a correct answer in this case is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0127\">By repeating this verification multiple times, the probability of correct answers when the socks have the same color can be made arbitrarily close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.<a href=\"#en-fn7\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref7\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>7</sup></a></span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0129\">Such a proof is called a <strong>zero-knowledge proof</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0130\">This suggests that interaction nontrivially increases the power of proof verification, but an even greater increase is in fact possible: [Sha02, LFKN02] showed that every problem in the class called <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PSPACE}\\)</span> (the class of decision problems solvable using polynomially many bits of memory) has an interactive proof, thereby characterizing the decision problems that admit interactive proofs (in theoretical computer science, paper authors are normally listed alphabetically, but the paper by Lund et al. is notable for breaking this convention).</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-131\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0131\">Theorem 1.3.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0132\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{IP}=\\mathsf{PSPACE}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0133\">The theorem above characterizes interactive proofs and, in particular, suggests that adding randomness to verification dramatically increases its power.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0134\">Can the class <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> then be characterized by \"scaling down\" this theorem?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0135\">More concretely, can <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> be characterized by a verifier that has randomness but limited power?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0136\">(When its power is unrestricted, it coincides with the class <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{IP}\\)</span>.)</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0137\">The class <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{PCP}\\)</span> was considered against this background, and the PCP theorem states specifically that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathsf{NP}\\)</span> can be characterized by limiting the number of proof symbols that the verifier can access.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"en-chapter-2-weak-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0001\">Chapter 2 Weak PCP Theorem</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0002\">This chapter aims to explain why the requirement of \"few queries\" can be achieved for PCP verifiers.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0003\">To this end, we first prove the following form of a \"weak PCP theorem,\" temporarily ignoring the shortness of the random bits required by the PCP theorem: every decision problem in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{NP}\\)</span> can be probabilistically verified using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> queries and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span> random bits.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-21-why-is-local-verification-possible\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0004\">2.1 Why Is Local Verification Possible?</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0005\">A PCP verifier must determine whether a proof provided with oracle access is indeed a valid proof by looking at only a constant number of characters.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0006\">Here, we call the property of looking at only a constant number of characters the locality of the verifier.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0007\">As a general intuition, when verifying a proof that demonstrates a claim, it seems that one must read the entire proof unless it is expressed <em>redundantly</em> (if every character has essential meaning, one should have to check them all).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0008\">Conversely, can local verification be made possible by expressing a proof redundantly?</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-211-local-verification-for-linear-equations-12\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0009\">2.1.1 Local Verification for Linear Equations (1/2)</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0010\">Interestingly, we explain that local verification is possible when a proof is given in a highly redundant representation, using verification of a linear equation as an example.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-211-linear-equation\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0011\">Definition 2.1.1 (Linear Equation)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0012\">For a matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n}\\)</span> over a finite field <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> and a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nMy=z\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0013\">let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span> denote the problem of deciding whether there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying this equation.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0014\">Here, assume that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathbb{F}\\rvert=q\\)</span> is a constant independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m,n\\)</span> (thus assuming that operations over the finite field can be computed in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> time).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0015\">Consider a verifier that naively receives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> as a proof and verifies it by checking <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span> (in an actual computer, everything is represented as binary strings, so elements of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> are represented using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lceil\\log_2\\lvert\\mathbb{F}\\rvert\\rceil=O(1)\\)</span> bits).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0016\">Clearly, this verifier must read all components of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span> (that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span> bits) to compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\)</span>, so the number of oracle accesses is large and it lacks locality.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0017\">Of course, since this decision problem is the standard problem of deciding whether a linear equation has a solution, it can be solved in polynomial time without any oracle access to the proof by using Gaussian elimination.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0018\">Nevertheless, we consider it here for the purpose of giving an example construction of local verification.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0019\">As an important property, consider the following property for two vectors <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a,b\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> (the proof is Exercise 3):</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-linear-equation-property\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n}\\left[r^\\top a\\ne r^\\top b\\right]=\\begin{cases}0&amp;\\text{if }a=b,\\\\1-\\frac1q&amp;\\text{if }a\\ne b.\\end{cases}\\tag{2.1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0020\">Now, using the property in Equation (2.1), for a uniformly random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-random-inner-product\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nr^\\top My=r^\\top z\\tag{2.2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0021\">we can probabilistically verify whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span> by checking whether this holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0022\">Indeed, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>, the verifier accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>, and</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0023\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\ne z\\)</span>, the verifier rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/q\\)</span> (by repeating this many times, the rejection probability can be brought arbitrarily close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0024\">Since the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span> is given as input, the right-hand side of Equation (2.2) can be computed in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span> time.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0025\">On the other hand, since the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span> is given as a proof, computing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top My\\)</span> naively takes <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(mn)\\)</span> time and requires reading all components of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0026\">However, by providing as a proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> the length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span> string <span class=\"math inline\">\\((y^\\top w)_{w\\in\\mathbb{F}^n}\\)</span> obtained by concatenating <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y^\\top w\\in\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> for every vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0027\">after computing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top M\\)</span> ourselves, we can compute the value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top My=y^\\top(M^\\top r)\\)</span> by reading <em>one character of the proof</em>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-3-inner-product-with-a-random-vector\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0028\">Exercise 3 (Inner Product with a Random Vector)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0029\">Prove Equation (2.1).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0030\">Thus, the basic idea is to keep the number of proof reads constant by representing the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> redundantly as the <em>truth table (the string listing the evaluation at every point)</em> of the linear function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\mapsto y^\\top w\\)</span> for which it is the coefficient vector.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0031\">The technique of representing a string redundantly in this way is collectively called an <em>error-correcting code</em>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0032\">In this course, we use the following error-correcting code, called the <em>Hadamard code</em>:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-212-hadamard-code\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0033\">Definition 2.1.2 (Hadamard Code)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0034\">For a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> over a finite field <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>, consider the length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span> string</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)=(y^\\top w)_{w\\in\\mathbb{F}^n}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0035\">We consider this string.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0036\">The set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n=\\{\\operatorname{Had}_n(y):y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\}\\)</span> is called the <em>Hadamard code</em>, and</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0037\">its elements are called <em>codewords of the Hadamard code</em>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0038\">We also call <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span> <em>the string obtained by encoding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span> with the Hadamard code</em>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0039\">When the dimension <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> is clear, it is omitted, and we write expressions such as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-213\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0040\">Remark 2.1.3</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0041\">In this course, we sometimes identify strings, vectors, and functions with one another.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0042\">That is, we may simply call a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> a string,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0043\">or regard it as a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\colon[n]\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0044\">For example, the string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span> obtained by encoding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> with the Hadamard code may be regarded as a string of length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>, or</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0045\">it may be regarded as a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0046\">This is because, although it is convenient to treat <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span> as a function during the analysis of the Hadamard code,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0047\">during the proof of the PCP theorem, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span> will be treated as a string and provided as the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0048\">If we naively implement the idea of treating the Hadamard encoding of the naive proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span> as the proof, we can consider the following verifier as a candidate for local verification of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-214\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0049\">Algorithm 2.1.4</span></h4>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0050\">Receive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M,z\\)</span> as input and oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span> as the proof.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0051\">Choose a uniformly random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span> and compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top M\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top z\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0052\">Interpret the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span> as a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>, and use oracle access to obtain <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a=\\pi(M^\\top r)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0053\">Output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a=r^\\top z\\)</span>, and output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> otherwise.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0054\">Does the verifier above have the properties of a PCP verifier?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0055\">First, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span> is a Yes instance, there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0056\">In this case, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(M^\\top r)=y^\\top(M^\\top r)=r^\\top(My)=r^\\top z\\)</span>, so the verifier accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0057\">On the other hand, what is the probability that the verifier rejects when <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span> is a No instance?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0058\">To show that it is a PCP verifier (cf. Definition 1.2.1), for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>, the rejection probability must be at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0059\">Can this be shown?</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0060\">When the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is a codeword of the Hadamard code (that is, it can be written as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span> for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>), the property of Equation (2.2) and the fact that <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span> is a No instance imply <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\ne z\\)</span>, so the verifier rejects with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/q\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0061\">However, a general <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> cannot necessarily be represented as such a linear function, and the verifier must also reject for such <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-212-linearity-test\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0062\">2.1.2 Linearity Test</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0063\">When a given <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,z)\\)</span> is a No instance, in order to reject any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span> with constant probability,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0064\">modify Algorithm 2.1.4 as follows:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0065\">If the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is a codeword of the Hadamard code, execute Steps 2-4 of Algorithm 2.1.4; otherwise, output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0066\">This gives rise to a new problem: how can we locally verify the linearity of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>?</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0067\">In general, to check exactly whether a function given by oracle access <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> is a linear function,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-linearity-test-all-points\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\forall x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,\\quad\\pi(x+y)=\\pi(x)+\\pi(y)\\tag{2.3}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0068\">we need to check whether this holds, which ends up requiring <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span> oracle accesses.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0069\">Thus, instead of locally verifying whether it is \"a linear function,\" we consider locally verifying whether it is \"close to a linear function.\"</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-215-closeness-between-functions\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0070\">Definition 2.1.5 (Closeness Between Functions)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0071\">For two functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f,g\\colon A\\to B\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\\)</span> as</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)=\\Pr_{a\\sim A}[f(a)\\ne g(a)]\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0072\">and, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\\le\\delta\\)</span>, say that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\)</span> is <em><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta\\)</span>-close</em> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(g\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0073\">Also, for a function class <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{F}\\subseteq\\{g\\colon A\\to B\\}\\)</span> and a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon A\\to B\\)</span>, define</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{F})=\\min_{g\\in\\mathcal{F}}\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0074\">and, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta(f,\\mathcal{F})\\le\\delta\\)</span>, say that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\delta\\)</span>-close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{F}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0075\">Identifying a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon A\\to B\\)</span> with a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in B^A\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,g)\\)</span> coincides with the normalized Hamming distance (the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell_0\\)</span> norm) between vectors <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f,g\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0076\">Consider the set of all linear functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\subseteq\\{\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\}\\)</span> (Definition 2.1.2).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0077\">A local verifier can be constructed that verifies whether a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> given by oracle access is close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span> [BLR93].</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-216-linearity-test\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0078\">Theorem 2.1.6 (Linearity Test)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0079\">There exists a randomized polynomial-time oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span> satisfying the following<a href=\"#en-fn8\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref8\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>8</sup></a>:</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0080\">When a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> is given by oracle access,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0081\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0082\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge0.01\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-217-local-testability-of-codes\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0083\">Remark 2.1.7 (Local Testability of Codes)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0084\">Theorem 2.1.6 means that for the Hadamard code <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span>, one can probabilistically determine, using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> oracle accesses, whether a string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span> given by oracle access is a codeword or is far from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0085\">An error-correcting code with this property is called a <em>locally testable code</em>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0086\">This concept was originally proposed by [GS06] in the context of proving the PCP theorem with short proof length.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0087\">Many constructions of locally testable codes are based on techniques nearly identical to those used in the proof of the PCP theorem.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0088\">For details, see Goldreich's survey paper [Gol10].</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-1\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0089\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0090\">The algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi\\)</span> is very simple: it checks the characterization equation (2.3) for being a linear function at random points.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0091\">Specifically, it is given as follows:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-218\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0092\">Algorithm 2.1.8</span></h4>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0093\">Receive a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> by oracle access, and receive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n\\)</span> as input.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0094\">Choose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> uniformly at random, and reject if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x+y)\\ne\\pi(x)+\\pi(y)\\)</span> holds.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0095\">For a sufficiently large constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, repeat Step 2 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\)</span> times.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0096\">If no rejection occurs during these repetitions, accept.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0097\">Indeed, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>, then equation (2.3) holds, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(1^n)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0098\">On the other hand, we want to lower-bound the rejection probability when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge0.01\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0099\">This follows from the following claim:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-219-rejection-probability-of-the-linearity-test\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0100\">Claim 2.1.9 (Rejection Probability of the Linearity Test)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0101\">For any function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{x,y\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n}[\\pi(x+y)\\ne\\pi(x)+\\pi(y)]\\ge\\frac16\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had}).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0102\">We will prove Claim 2.1.9 later.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0103\">By assumption, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge\\delta:=0.01\\)</span>, so</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0104\">by making the constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\)</span> in Step 3 sufficiently large, we can make the rejection probability of Algorithm 2.1.8 greater than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0105\">We next prove Claim 2.1.9.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0106\">In fact, we prove the following more general lemma.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-2110-homomorphism-test\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0107\">Lemma 2.1.10 (Homomorphism Test)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0108\">For finite abelian groups <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G,H\\)</span>, a map <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon G\\to H\\)</span> is called a homomorphism if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)\\)</span> holds for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>, and the set of all homomorphic maps from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(H\\)</span> is denoted by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{H}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0109\">Then, for any function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon G\\to H\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{x,y\\sim G}[f(x+y)\\ne f(x)+f(y)]\\ge\\frac1{12}\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H}).\n\\]</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-2\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0110\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0111\">For notational convenience, write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f:=\\Pr_{x,y\\sim G}[f(x+y)\\ne f(x)+f(y)]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0112\">Also, for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in G\\)</span>, let the map <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_y\\colon G\\to H\\)</span> be defined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_y(x)=f(x+y)-f(y)\\)</span>, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\colon G\\to H\\)</span> be defined by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nv(x)=\\operatorname*{argmax}_{a\\in H}\\{\\Pr_{y\\sim G}[v_y(x)=a]\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0113\">(arbitrarily if there is a tie).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0114\">In other words, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x)\\)</span> is the majority vote among <span class=\"math inline\">\\((v_y(x))_{y\\in G}\\)</span>, namely, the value occurring most frequently.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0115\">The proof follows by combining the following three claims.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-2111\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0116\">Claim 2.1.11</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0117\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(f,v)\\le2\\rho_f\\)</span> holds.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-3\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0118\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0119\">By definition, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f=\\Pr_{x,y}[f(x)+f(y)\\ne f(x+y)]=\\Pr_{x,y}[f(x)\\ne v_y(x)]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0120\">Also, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x)\\ne h\\Rightarrow\\Pr_y[v_y(x)=h]\\le1/2\\iff\\Pr_y[v_y(x)\\ne h]\\ge1/2\\)</span> holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0121\">Indeed, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\)</span> is not selected by majority vote among <span class=\"math inline\">\\((v_y(x))_{y\\in G}\\)</span>, this means that it did not reach a majority.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0122\">In particular, setting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h=f(x)\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_{v(x)\\ne f(x)}\\le2\\Pr_y[v_y(x)\\ne f(x)]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0123\">Taking the expectation over <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\sim G\\)</span> on both sides,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(f,v)=\\Pr_x[v(x)\\ne f(x)]\\le2\\Pr_{x,y}[v_y(x)\\ne f(x)]=2\\rho_f.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0124\">Thus the claim follows.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-2112\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0125\">Claim 2.1.12</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0126\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f&lt;1/6\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_y[v_y(x)=v(x)]&gt;2/3\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in G\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-4\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0127\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0128\">Fix any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in G\\)</span>, and define the random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi\\)</span> by choosing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\sim G\\)</span> uniformly at random and setting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi=v_y(x)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0129\">Our goal is to show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[\\Phi=a]&gt;2/3\\)</span> holds for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\in H\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0130\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi_1,\\Phi_2\\)</span> be independent copies of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi\\)</span> (with the same fixed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0131\">To show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi\\)</span> tends to take some particular value, we first show that the probability that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Phi_1=\\Phi_2\\)</span> is large.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[\\Phi_1=\\Phi_2]&amp;=\\Pr_{y_1,y_2\\sim G}[v_{y_1}(x)=v_{y_2}(x)]\\\\\n&amp;=\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(x+y_1)-f(y_1)=f(x+y_2)-f(y_2)]\\\\\n&amp;=\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(x+y_1)-f(x+y_2)=f(y_1)-f(y_2)]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge1-2\\rho_f\\\\\n&amp;&gt;2/3.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0132\">In the first inequality, we used <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(x+y_1)-f(x+y_2)\\ne f(y_1-y_2)]\\le\\rho_f\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_{y_1,y_2}[f(y_1)-f(y_2)\\ne f(y_1-y_2)]\\le\\rho_f\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0133\">Here, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2/3&lt;\\Pr[\\Phi_1=\\Phi_2]=\\sum_{a\\in H}\\Pr[\\Phi=a]^2\\le\\max_a\\Pr[\\Phi=a]\\cdot\\sum_{a\\in H}\\Pr[\\Phi=a]=\\max_a\\Pr[\\Phi=a]\\)</span>, the claim follows.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-2113\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0134\">Claim 2.1.13</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0135\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_y[v_y(x)=v(x)]&gt;2/3\\)</span> holds for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in G\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in\\mathcal{H}\\)</span>, that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(h\\)</span> is a homomorphism.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-5\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0136\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0137\">It suffices to show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)+f(y)=f(x+y)\\)</span> holds for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0138\">Fix arbitrary <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0139\">Choose a uniformly random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\sim G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0140\">Then, by the assumption,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0141\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_z[v_z(x)\\ne v(x)]&lt;1/3\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0142\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_z[v_{z-y}(y)\\ne v(y)]&lt;1/3\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0143\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_z[v_{z-y}(x+y)\\ne v(x+y)]&lt;1/3\\)</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0144\">Therefore, by the union bound, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in G\\)</span> there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in G\\)</span> such that the following three statements hold simultaneously:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0145\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x)=v_z(x)=f(x+z)-f(z)\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0146\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(y)=v_{z-y}(y)=f(z)-f(z-y)\\)</span>,</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0147\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(v(x+y)=v_{z-y}(x+y)=f(x+z)-f(z-y)\\)</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0148\">From these equalities,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nv(x)+v(y)-v(x+y)=f(x+z)-f(z-y)-(f(x+z)-f(z-y))=0.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0149\">Thus the claim follows.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0150\">Finally, we complete the proof of Lemma 2.1.10.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0151\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f&lt;1/6\\)</span>, then by Claims 2.1.12 and 2.1.13, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in\\mathcal{H}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0152\">Moreover, by Claim 2.1.11,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\rho_f\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(f,v)}2\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H})}2\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0153\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0154\">On the other hand, otherwise <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f\\ge1/6\\ge\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H})/6\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0155\">In either case, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\rho_f\\ge\\operatorname{dist}(f,\\mathcal{H})/6\\)</span> holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0156\">Thus, we have shown that it is possible to locally verify whether the given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0157\">Now suppose that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>, and</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0158\">let the closest linear function be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0159\">Such an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span> is unique (Exercise 4).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0160\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x)=f(x)\\)</span> holds for 99% of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0161\">On the other hand, Algorithm 2.1.4 requires computing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(M^\\top r)\\)</span>, and</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0162\">in general, for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>, the distribution of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M^\\top r\\)</span> is not necessarily uniformly random.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0163\">Therefore, it is necessary to compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)\\)</span> for every point <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>, and</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0164\">the following lemma shows that this is possible.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-2114-evaluation-of-the-closest-linear-function-at-an-arbitrary-point\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0165\">Lemma 2.1.14 (Evaluation of the Closest Linear Function at an Arbitrary Point)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0166\">Suppose that the function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\le\\epsilon\\)</span>, and</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0167\">let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span> be the closest linear function to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0168\">Then there exists a randomized oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span> that takes any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> as input and, using two oracle accesses to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-2\\epsilon\\)</span> in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span> time.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-6\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0169\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0170\">For the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A^\\pi(x)\\)</span> chooses a random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> and outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x+r)-\\pi(r)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0171\">Clearly, the time complexity is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n)\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr_r[\\pi(x+r)\\ne f(x+r)],\\Pr_r[\\pi(r)\\ne f(r)]\\)</span> are each at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\epsilon\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0172\">Therefore, with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-2\\epsilon\\)</span>, the output is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(x+r)-\\pi(r)=f(x+r)-f(r)=f(x)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-4-uniqueness-of-the-closest-linear-function\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0173\">Exercise 4 (Uniqueness of the Closest Linear Function)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0174\">When a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\le0.01\\)</span>,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0175\">show that there exists a unique <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\in\\operatorname{Had}\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,f)\\le0.01\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-213-local-verification-of-linear-equations-22\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0176\">2.1.3 Local Verification of Linear Equations (2/2)</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0177\">Using the discussion so far, we construct a local verifier for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-2115\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0178\">Algorithm 2.1.15</span></h4>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0179\">Receive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M,z\\)</span> as input, and receive oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb{F}^{q^n}\\)</span> as a proof.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0180\">Run the algorithm of Theorem 2.1.6 with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> as an oracle, and if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had})\\ge0.01\\)</span>, reject and terminate.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0181\">Choose a uniformly random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>, and compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top M\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r^\\top z\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0182\">Run the algorithm of Lemma 2.1.14 with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M^\\top r\\)</span> as input and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> as an oracle, and let its output be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0183\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\ne r^\\top z\\)</span>, reject.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0184\">Repeat Steps 3-5 three times, and if it never rejects, accept and terminate.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0185\">Compared with the verifier of Algorithm 2.1.4,</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0186\">first, a step has been added to verify that the function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> given as the proof is a linear function.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0187\">Also, in Step 4, the algorithm of Lemma 2.1.14 is used instead of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi(M^\\top r)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0188\">From the discussion so far, we can see that Algorithm 2.1.15 is a local verifier for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{LinEq}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0189\">Step 6 is simply a repetition to amplify the success probability.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-2116-local-verification-of-linear-equations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0190\">Theorem 2.1.16 (Local Verification of Linear Equations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0191\">Let the verifier of Algorithm 2.1.15 be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0192\">Then, for any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n},z\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span>, the following hold:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0193\">The number of oracle accesses to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0194\">If there exists <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>, then, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0195\">If there is no <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>, then for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2/3\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-7\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0196\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0197\">The number of oracle accesses is clearly <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0198\">Also, if there exists <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>, then, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}(y)\\)</span>, it accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0199\">On the other hand, consider the probability that the verifier rejects when there is no <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0200\">Consider the following two cases for the oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-case-1-pi-is-within-001-of-a-linear-function\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0201\">Case 1: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is within <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span> of a linear function.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0202\">In this case, we evaluate the probability that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> rejects in Step 5.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0203\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> be the linear function closest to the oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0204\">Then, by Lemma 2.1.14 (with input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M^\\top r\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\epsilon=0.01\\)</span>), the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> computed in Step 4 equals <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(M^\\top r)\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-2\\epsilon=0.98\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0205\">Here, assuming that the linear function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\)</span> can be written as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f(x)=x^\\top y\\)</span>, the rejection probability satisfies</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[a\\ne r^\\top z]&amp;\\ge\\Pr_r[a\\ne r^\\top z\\mid a=f(M^\\top r)]\\cdot\\Pr_r[a=f(M^\\top r)]\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\Pr_r[r^\\top My\\ne r^\\top z]\\cdot0.98\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\left(1-\\frac1q\\right)\\cdot0.98\\\\\n&amp;\\ge0.4\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0206\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0207\">The third inequality uses Equation (2.2) and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My\\ne z\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0208\">The fourth inequality uses <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\ge2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0209\">Because this is repeated four times, the rejection probability is at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-(1-0.4)^3\\ge\\frac23\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-case-2-pi-is-more-than-001-from-a-linear-function\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0210\">Case 2: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is more than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span> from a linear function.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0211\">In this case, by Theorem 2.1.6, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> rejects in Step 2 with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0212\">Therefore, in either case, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> rejects with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2/3\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0213\">Using the discussion so far, we can construct a verifier that efficiently verifies solutions to linear equations using an oracle, as follows.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-2117-verification-of-solutions-to-linear-equations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0214\">Lemma 2.1.17 (Verification of Solutions to Linear Equations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0215\">There exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> and a randomized <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(mn)\\)</span>-time oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0216\">It receives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n},z\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span> as input and oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> as an oracle, and makes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> total oracle accesses to these oracles.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0217\">For every oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, the following hold:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0218\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span> for some vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0219\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi,\\operatorname{Had}_n(y))\\ge0.01\\)</span> for every vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/linear_verification.png\" alt=\"Image of the verifier from Lemma 2.1.17\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0220\">Image of the verifier from Lemma 2.1.17.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0221\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span> for a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=z\\)</span> is given as the oracle, it always accepts.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0222\">On the other hand, it rejects with high probability for a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> that is far from all such <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-5-verification-of-solutions-to-linear-equations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0223\">Exercise 5 (Verification of Solutions to Linear Equations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-0224\">Prove Lemma 2.1.17 (Hint: use Equation (2.2) and Lemma 2.1.14).</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-22-the-weak-pcp-theorem-and-its-proof\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0001\">2.2 The Weak PCP Theorem and Its Proof</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0002\">What is needed to extend the idea of Theorem 2.1.16 and prove the PCP theorem?</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0003\">First, although <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{LinEQ}\\)</span> is in fact a decision problem solvable in polynomial time, we must construct a verifier like the one in Theorem 2.1.16 for some NP-complete decision problem.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0004\">Next, the verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,z)\\)</span> in Theorem 2.1.16 requires <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Omega(n)\\)</span> bits of randomness when it randomly chooses <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r,r&#39;\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0005\">Indeed, when the function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> given as a witness is represented as a string, its length is proportional to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span>, so specifying a random index of that string necessarily requires <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Omega(n)\\)</span> bits of randomness.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0006\">We therefore need to reduce the length of the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> from the exponential length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{\\Theta(n)}\\)</span> to the polynomial length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span> (then the number of bits needed to specify an index is at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log n)\\)</span>).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0007\">In this section, we resolve the former issue and prove the following weak PCP theorem.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-221-weak-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0008\">Theorem 2.2.1 (Weak PCP Theorem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0009\">For some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)=\\operatorname{poly}(n),q(n)=O(1)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{NP} \\subseteq \\mathrm{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span> holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0010\">The PCP theorem (Theorem 1.2.7), which is our ultimate goal, requires the PCP verifier's random-bit length to be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)=O(\\log n)\\)</span>, so the value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r(n)\\)</span> in Theorem 2.2.1 is exponentially larger, but the two are the same in that the number of proof symbols read is bounded by a constant independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0011\">The idea behind the proof of Theorem 2.2.1 will also be used later in the proof of Theorem 1.2.7.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-221-strategy\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0012\">2.2.1 Strategy</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0013\">It suffices to construct a PCP verifier for the problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span> defined in Theorem 1.2.13.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0014\">More generally, here we show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\in\\mathrm{PCP}(r,q)\\)</span> for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=O(1),q=\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0015\">An instance of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span> consists of</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;f_1(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = 0,\\\\\n&amp;\\quad\\vdots\\\\\n&amp;f_m(x_1,\\dots,x_n) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0016\">a system of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span> quadratic equations over <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0017\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\)</span> be the vector formed by arranging <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_ix_j\\)</span> (here <span class=\"math inline\">\\((i,j)\\)</span> is treated as an element of <span class=\"math inline\">\\([n^2]\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0018\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[m]\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in[n]\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-M-matrix\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nM_{k,(i,j)} = \\begin{cases}\n1 &amp; \\text{if }f_k(x_1,\\dots,x_n)\\text{ contains the term }x_i x_j,\\\\\n0 &amp; \\text{otherwise},\n\\end{cases}\\tag{2.4}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-b-vector\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nb_k = f_k(0)\\tag{2.5}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0019\">define a matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{m\\times n^2}\\)</span> and vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^m\\)</span>, and the system of equations can then be expressed as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0020\">Therefore, the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span> instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_i)_{i\\in[m]}\\)</span> being a Yes instance is equivalent to the existence of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> satisfying</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0021\"><strong>Condition 1</strong> For all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0022\"><strong>Condition 2</strong> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0023\">these conditions.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0024\">Our strategy is to construct PCP verifiers for these two conditions and combine them to obtain a verifier for the problem above.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0025\">For Condition 2, it suffices to run <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(M,b)\\)</span> using the verifier from Theorem 2.1.16.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0026\">This verifier makes <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> oracle accesses and has random-bit length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n^2)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-222-construction-of-the-proof\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0027\">2.2.2 Construction of the Proof</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0028\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> be the number of elements of the finite field <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0029\">The proof received by the PCP verifier consists of two functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\colon\\mathbb{F}^n\\to\\mathbb{F}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0030\">Specifically, the proof is the string obtained by concatenating the strings of lengths <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^{n^2}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^n\\)</span> representing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>, respectively (so its total length is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q^{n^2}+q^n\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0031\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> satisfying Conditions 1 and 2, the verifier expects their Hadamard encodings <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}(x)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi = \\mathrm{Had}(y)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0032\">It must therefore verify that these actually encode <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>, and that <span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,y)\\)</span> indeed satisfies Conditions 1 and 2.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0033\">Using the verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> from Theorem 2.1.16 (with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=b\\)</span>), Condition 2 can be probabilistically verified with a constant number of oracle accesses.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0034\">Moreover, by Theorem 2.1.6, whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> are each close to a linear function can be probabilistically verified with a constant number of oracle accesses.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0035\">Thus, it suffices to construct a PCP verifier for Condition 1 under the assumption that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi,\\pi_x\\)</span> are each very close to a linear function.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-223-verifying-the-tensor-product\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0036\">2.2.3 Verifying the Tensor Product</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0037\">Our goal is to use a constant number of accesses to two oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>, guaranteed to satisfy <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi,\\mathrm{Had}(y))\\le 0.01\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}(x))\\le 0.01\\)</span> for some vectors <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>, to verify that these <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span> satisfy Condition 1.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0038\">If such <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span> exist, Exercise 4 guarantees that they are unique.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0039\">Regard <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> as an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\times n\\)</span> matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=xx^\\top\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0040\">Condition 1 is equivalent to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-222-verifying-the-tensor-product\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0041\">Lemma 2.2.2 (Verifying the Tensor Product)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0042\">For some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span>, there exists a PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span> that uses a constant number of accesses to two oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2})\\le 0.01\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n)\\le 0.01\\)</span>, and verifies that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span> satisfy <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0043\">That is, for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=(y_{i,j})_{i,j\\in [n]}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=xx^\\top \\in \\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0044\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0045\">if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y\\ne X\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0046\">The strategy is to extend the idea of Equation (2.1) to matrices.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-8\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0047\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0048\">For arbitrary matrices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A,B\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n\\times n}\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-random-quadratic-form\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_{r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n}\\left[ r_1^\\top X r_2 \\ne r_1^\\top Y r_2 \\right] =\n\\begin{cases}\n0 &amp; \\text{if } X=Y,\\\\\n1-\\frac{1}{q} &amp; \\text{if } X\\ne Y.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{2.6}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0049\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0050\">Using oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>, we can compute both sides of Equation (2.6) and compare the results to determine whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=X\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0051\">First, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=xx^\\top\\)</span>, for arbitrary <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\in\\mathbb{F}^V\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_1^\\top X r_2 &amp;= \\sum_{i,j\\in [n]} r_1(i) r_2(j) x_i x_j \\\\\n&amp;= \\sum_{i\\in [n]} r_1(i) x_i \\sum_{j\\in [n]} r_2(j) x_j \\\\\n&amp;= \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_1)\\cdot \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_2)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0052\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0053\">In particular, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\le 0.01\\)</span>, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span> are chosen uniformly at random, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_1)=\\pi_x(r_1)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r_2)=\\pi_x(r_2)\\)</span> hold with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0054\">Therefore, the left-hand side of Equation (2.6) can be computed with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span> using two oracle accesses to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0055\">Next, we compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1^\\top Y r_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0056\">For arbitrary <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\in\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{r}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> be the string whose <span class=\"math inline\">\\((i,j)\\)</span>th symbol is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((r_1)_i\\cdot (r_2)_j\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0057\">Then</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nr_1^\\top Y r_2 &amp;= \\sum_{i,j\\in [n]} (r_1)_i (r_2)_j y_{i,j} \\\\\n&amp;= \\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0058\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0059\">Also, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2})\\le 0.01\\)</span>, applying Lemma 2.1.14 with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varepsilon=0.01\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f=\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\pi_y\\)</span> lets us compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0060\">That is, the right-hand side of Equation (2.6) can be computed with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span> using two oracle accesses to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-223\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0061\">Algorithm 2.2.3</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0062\">The PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y}(1^n)\\)</span> is described as follows:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0063\">Receive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n\\)</span> as input and oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0064\">Choose uniformly random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0065\">Use oracle access to compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r_1),\\pi_x(r_2)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0066\">Use Lemma 2.1.14 to compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0067\">Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{r}\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> is the string whose <span class=\"math inline\">\\((i,j)\\)</span>th symbol is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((r_1)_i\\cdot (r_2)_j\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0068\">Reject if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r_1)\\cdot \\pi_x(r_2)\\ne \\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)(\\overline{r})\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0069\">Repeat Steps 2-5 ten times, and if no repetition rejects, accept and halt.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0070\">In Steps 3 and 4, both sides of Equation (2.6) can be computed with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.96\\)</span>, and if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\ne Y\\)</span>, rejection occurs with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-1/q\\)</span> (over the randomness used to choose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r_1,r_2\\sim\\mathbb{F}^n\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0071\">Therefore, a single trial rejects with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.96-\\frac{1}{q} \\ge 0.4\\)</span> (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\because q\\ge 2\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0072\">By repeating this trial a sufficiently large constant number of times (ten suffices), the rejection probability when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\ne Y\\)</span> can be amplified arbitrarily.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-224-proof-of-the-weak-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0073\">2.2.4 Proof of the Weak PCP Theorem</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0074\">First, we extend Lemma 2.1.17 from verification for linear equations to verification for circuits.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-224-circuit-verification\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0075\">Theorem 2.2.4 (Circuit Verification)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0076\">There exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> and a polynomial-time randomized oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0077\">It receives a circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon \\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> as input and oracle access to two oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>, and makes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> oracle accesses in total to all the oracles.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0078\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span> for a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>, then for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0079\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>, and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/circuit_verification.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the verifier in Theorem 2.2.4\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0080\">Figure 2.2: Illustration of the verifier in Theorem 2.2.4.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0081\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, then for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0082\">On the other hand, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{n^2}}\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-9\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0083\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0084\">For the input circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>, use the Karp reduction given in Theorem 1.2.13 to construct a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span> instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in [m]}\\)</span> (where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m=n+s+1\\)</span> for the size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0085\">Furthermore, for this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{QuadEq}\\)</span> instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\((f_k)_{k\\in[m]}\\)</span>, construct the matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}^{m\\times n^2}\\)</span> and vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}^m\\)</span> defined in Equations (2.4) and (2.5).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0086\">Then, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>, the following are equivalent:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0087\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span> (here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is treated as an element of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0088\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_k(x)=0\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in[m]\\)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0089\">The vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\)</span> defined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}:=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0090\">Below, we give a PCP verifier for the third condition.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-225\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0091\">Algorithm 2.2.5</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0092\">The PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(M,b)\\)</span> is described as follows:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0093\">Receive <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{m\\times n^2}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^m\\)</span> as input, and oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> as the proof.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0094\">Run the algorithm of Theorem 2.1.6 on each of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span> to verify whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span> are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-close to linear functions.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0095\">Reject if the algorithm rejects in either run.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0096\">Run the verifier of Lemma 2.2.2 using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> as its oracles.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0097\">Reject if this verifier rejects.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0098\">Run the verifier of Lemma 2.1.17 with input <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,b)\\)</span> and oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0099\">Reject if this verifier rejects.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0100\">If none of the preceding steps has rejected, accept and halt.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0101\">We verify that this verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}\\)</span> is the desired PCP verifier.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0102\">In each of Theorem 2.1.6, Theorem 2.1.16, Lemma 2.2.2, and Lemma 2.1.17, the number of oracle accesses is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span> and the number of random bits used internally is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(n^2)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0103\">Suppose that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span> for an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0104\">Then the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,b)\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span> for the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\)</span> defined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0105\">For this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi=\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)\\)</span> (by the lemma's assumption, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0106\">Then,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0107\">in Step 2, both <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span> are linear functions, so the rejection probability is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0108\">in Step 3, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span> actually holds for all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>, so the rejection probability is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0109\">in Step 4, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span> is actually a solution to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(My=b\\)</span>, so the rejection probability is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0110\">It follows that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0111\">Next, suppose that the oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> satisfies</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0112\">for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0113\">Fix an arbitrary oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0114\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi,\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2})\\ge 0.01\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n)\\ge 0.01\\)</span>, the verifier rejects in Step 2 with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0115\">Henceforth, suppose that both <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi,\\pi_x\\)</span> are sufficiently close to linear functions, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_{n^2}(y)\\)</span> be the linear function closest to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span> the linear function closest to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0116\">Then the assumption on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> implies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0117\">Since the assumptions of Lemma 2.2.2 are satisfied in Step 3, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}\\ne x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span> for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in[n]\\)</span>, it rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0118\">Thus, henceforth suppose in addition that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}^{n^2}\\)</span> satisfy <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_{i,j}=x_i\\cdot x_j\\)</span> for all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i,j\\in [n]\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0119\">In Step 4, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=0\\iff My\\ne b\\)</span>, the verifier of Lemma 2.1.17 rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0120\">It follows that the probability that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}\\)</span> rejects in the loop of Steps 2-4 is at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.97\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0121\">In fact, the weak PCP theorem follows immediately from Theorem 2.2.4.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-theorem-221\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0122\">Proof of Theorem 2.2.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0123\">By Theorem 1.2.11, the circuit satisfiability decision problem is NP-complete.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0124\">It therefore suffices to give a PCP verifier for this problem.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0125\">In fact, the verifier in Theorem 2.2.4 satisfies this condition.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0126\">Indeed, if the input circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> is a Yes instance, there exists an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0127\">Regarding this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> as an element of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> and setting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x = \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0128\">On the other hand, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> is a No instance, there is no <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span>, so for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-226-pcp-of-proximity\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0129\">Remark 2.2.6 (PCP of Proximity)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0130\">A verifier like that of Theorem 2.2.4, which is given a circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> as input as well as oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x\\)</span> encoding the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> received by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> and to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> containing \"additional information,\" and rejects with high probability when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is far from an encoding of a satisfying assignment of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>, asserts something stronger than satisfiability verification in general; a proof achieving this is called a <strong>PCP of Proximity</strong>, and its verifier is called a PCPP verifier.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-23-a-pcp-verifier-for-assignments-to-a-two-input-circuit\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0131\">2.3 A PCP Verifier for Assignments to a Two-Input Circuit</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0132\">In a sense, Theorem 2.2.4 constructed a PCP verifier that verifies whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x)=1\\)</span> for a given circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> without seeing all of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> (in fact, it also assumes oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span> and to additional information <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y\\)</span>).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0133\">Here we show that a similar PCPP verifier can be constructed even when the circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span> receives two inputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0134\">Although this setting may appear artificial, it plays an important role in the proof of the PCP theorem.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-231-pcp-verifier-for-assignments-to-a-two-input-circuit\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0135\">Theorem 2.3.1 (PCP Verifier for Assignments to a Two-Input Circuit)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0136\">There exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> and a polynomial-time randomized oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0137\">It receives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^n\\times\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> and oracle access to three oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y, \\pi\\)</span> as input, and makes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> oracle accesses to these oracles in total.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0138\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y=\\mathrm{Had}_n(y)\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>, then there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2n} + 2^{4n^2}}\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0139\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge0.01\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_n(y))\\ge0.01\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>, and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi}(C)\\)</span> rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0140\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\((x,y)\\)</span> is regarded as one string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span> and Theorem 2.2.4 is applied, the verifier has oracle access to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_z=\\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(z)\\)</span> and additional information <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, whereas the verifier in Theorem 2.3.1 differs in having oracles for the separate encodings <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0141\">In the proof of Theorem 2.3.1, we use the following lemma, which locally verifies (using oracle access) whether a string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> is a prefix of another string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n+m}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-232-prefix-verification\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0142\">Lemma 2.3.2 (Prefix Verification)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0143\">There exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> and a polynomial-time randomized oracle algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}(1^n,1^m)\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0144\">The oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}\\)</span> are guaranteed to satisfy the following: for some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n+m}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))&lt;0.01\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_{\\overline{x}},\\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x}))&lt;0.01\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0145\">The verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}\\)</span> makes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> oracle accesses.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0146\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x = \\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}=\\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>, and moreover the first <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> symbols of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span> agree with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0147\">On the other hand, if the first <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> symbols of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span> do not agree with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span>, it rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-10\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0148\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0149\">The algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_0^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{\\overline{x}}}\\)</span> is simple and can be described as follows:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0150\">Choose a uniformly random vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span>, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{r}=(r,0^m)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{n+m}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0151\">Reject if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r) \\ne \\pi_{\\overline{x}}(\\overline{r})\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0152\">Here, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}(\\overline{r})\\)</span> is computed using Lemma 2.1.14.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0153\">Repeat Steps 1-2 ten times, and if no repetition rejects, accept and halt.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0154\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\)</span> be the first <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> symbols of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0155\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}=\\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y=x\\)</span>, then for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\sim\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> chosen in Step 2, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r) = \\mathrm{Had}_n(y)(r) = \\mathrm{Had}_{n+m}(\\overline{x})(r,0^m)\\)</span>, so the algorithm accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0156\">Now consider the case <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\ne y\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0157\">Since the oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}(\\overline{x})\\)</span>, by Lemma 2.1.14, in Step 2 we can compute <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{\\overline{x}}(\\overline{x})=\\mathrm{Had}_n(y)(r)\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.98\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0158\">Also, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span> is uniformly random, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x(r)=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)(r)\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0159\">Therefore, the verifier rejects with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.97\\)</span> in one trial, so repeating Steps 1-2 only ten times makes the verifier reject with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-233-suffix-verification\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0160\">Remark 2.3.3 (Suffix Verification)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0161\">The algorithm of Lemma 2.3.2 verifies whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> is a prefix of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span>, but a similar algorithm (merely reversing the order of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0^m\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span>) can also be used to verify a suffix.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-theorem-231\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0162\">Proof of Theorem 2.3.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0163\">Interpret the third oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\in\\{0,1\\}^{2^{2n} + 2^{4n^2}}\\)</span> as a pair of the following two functions:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0164\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1 \\colon \\mathbb{F}_2^{2n} \\to \\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0165\">This function is intended to satisfy <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1 = \\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(\\overline{x})\\)</span> for the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}:=(x,x&#39;)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0166\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2 \\colon \\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2} \\to \\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0167\">For the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{x}\\)</span> above and the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{y}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2}\\)</span> defined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\overline{y}_{i,j} = \\overline{x}_i\\cdot \\overline{x}_j\\)</span>, this function is intended to satisfy <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2 = \\mathrm{Had}_{4n^2}(\\overline{y})\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0168\">Although <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span> can in fact be interpreted as functions, those functions need not be linear.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0169\">Consider the following verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{y},\\pi}(C)\\)</span>, obtained by slightly modifying Algorithm 2.2.5:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-234\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0170\">Algorithm 2.3.4</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0171\">The PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_x,\\pi_{y},\\pi}(C)\\)</span> is described as follows:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0172\">Interpret the oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> as a pair of functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2,\\pi_2\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2}\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0173\">Regard the input circuit as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\colon\\{0,1\\}^{2n}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>, and compute the matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{m\\times {(2n)^2}}\\)</span> and vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^m\\)</span> considered in the proof of Theorem 2.2.4.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0174\">Run the algorithm of Theorem 2.1.6 on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span> to verify whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span> are each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-close to a linear function.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0175\">Reject if the algorithm rejects in any run.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0176\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Had}_n(x),\\mathrm{Had}_n(y),\\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(z)\\)</span> be the linear functions closest to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y,\\pi_1\\)</span>, respectively.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0177\">Use the algorithm of Lemma 2.3.2 to verify, respectively, whether the first <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> symbols of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span> agree with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> and the last <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> symbols agree with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0178\">Reject if the algorithm rejects in either verification.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0179\">Run the verifier of Lemma 2.2.2 using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2\\)</span> as its oracles.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0180\">Reject if this verifier rejects.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0181\">Run the verifier of Lemma 2.1.17 with input <span class=\"math inline\">\\((M,b)\\)</span> and oracle <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_2\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0182\">Reject if this verifier rejects.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0183\">If none of the preceding steps has rejected, accept and halt.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0184\">For <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>, suppose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x=\\mathrm{Had}_n(x)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_y=\\mathrm{Had}_n(y)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0185\">Then let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span> be the string obtained by concatenating <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span>, define the string <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{4n^2}\\)</span> by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(w_{i,j}=z_i\\cdot z_j\\)</span>, and finally set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi = \\left( \\mathrm{Had}_{2n}(z), \\mathrm{Had}_{4n^2}(w) \\right)\\)</span>; then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\mathrm{Had}_n(x),\\mathrm{Had}_n(y),\\pi}(C)\\)</span> accepts with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0186\">On the other hand, suppose that for the oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_x,\\pi_y\\)</span>, at least one of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_x,\\mathrm{Had}_n(x))\\ge 0.01\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\pi_y,\\mathrm{Had}_n(y))\\ge0.01\\)</span> holds for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C(x,y)=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0187\">Fix arbitrary oracles <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1,\\pi_2\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0188\">Step 3 checks whether all the oracles are close to linear functions, and if any is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-far from a linear function, the verifier rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span> by Theorem 2.1.6.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0189\">Let the closest strings to them be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n,y\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n,z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>, respectively.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0190\">Step 4 checks whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z=(x,y)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0191\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\ne(x,y)\\)</span>, it rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0192\">Steps 5 onward are identical to Theorem 2.2.4; in particular, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_1\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.01\\)</span>-far from every satisfying assignment of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C\\)</span>, the verifier rejects with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0193\">Furthermore, in this case, for every satisfying assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\((x^*,y^*)\\in C^{-1}(1)\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(x,x^*)\\ge0.01\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(y,y^*)\\ge 0.01\\)</span> holds (Exercise 6).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-6-distance-of-a-vector-split-in-two\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0194\">Exercise 6 (Distance of a Vector Split in Two)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0195\">For a vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{head}(z)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> be the subvector consisting of its first <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> components, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{tail}(z)\\in\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\)</span> be the subvector consisting of its last <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> components.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0196\">Fix an arbitrary set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_2^{2n}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0197\">If the vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(z\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(z,S)\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span>, that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(z,s)\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in S\\)</span>, show that for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in S\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0198\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\mathrm{head}(z),\\mathrm{head}(s))\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span></span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0199\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{dist}(\\mathrm{tail}(z),\\mathrm{tail}(s))\\ge \\varepsilon\\)</span></span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p04-0200\">at least one of these holds.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"en-chapter-3-constraint-satisfaction-problems\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0001\">Chapter 3 Constraint Satisfaction Problems</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0002\">The constraint satisfaction problem (CSP) is one of the important problems in computational complexity theory and also plays a central role in the proof of the PCP theorem.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-31-definition-of-the-constraint-satisfaction-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0003\">3.1 Definition of the Constraint Satisfaction Problem</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0004\">Briefly stated, the constraint satisfaction problem is a decision problem that asks whether a system of equations has a solution.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-311-constraint-satisfaction-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0005\">Definition 3.1.1 (Constraint Satisfaction Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0006\">A <em>constraint satisfaction problem (CSP)</em> is a decision problem whose instances are tuples <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> consisting of the following elements:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0007\"><em>Alphabet</em>: a finite set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0008\"><em>Sequence of variables</em>: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=(\\mathrm{x}_1,\\dots,\\mathrm{x}_n)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0009\">Sequence of constraint arguments: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}=(I_1,\\dots,I_m)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0010\">Here, each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_i\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_i\\ne\\emptyset\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_i\\subseteq[n]\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0011\"><em>Sequence of constraints</em>: <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}=(c_I)_{I\\in\\mathcal{I}}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0012\">Here, each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\subseteq\\Sigma^{\\lvert I\\rvert}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0013\">Each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}\\in X\\)</span> is called a <em>variable</em>, and each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\in\\mathcal{C}\\)</span> is called a <em>constraint</em>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0014\">The input <span class=\"math inline\">\\((X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> is a Yes instance if and only if there exists an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> to the variables such that, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I=\\{i_1,\\dots,i_k\\}\\in\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> (where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i_1&lt;\\dots&lt;i_k\\)</span>),</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n(a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}),\\dots,a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}))\\in c_I\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0015\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0016\">Also, the sum <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n+m\\)</span> of the number of variables and the number of constraints is called the <em>size</em> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> and is denoted by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0017\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I_i\\rvert\\le q\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[m]\\)</span>, this CSP is called a <em><span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP</em>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0018\">For a fixed assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, define the <em>unsatisfiability value</em> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)=\\Pr_{\\{i_1,\\dots,i_k\\}\\sim\\mathcal{I}}\\left[(a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}),\\dots,a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}))\\notin c_I\\right].\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0019\">This gives the definition.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0020\">Here, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\sim\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> means that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\)</span> is chosen uniformly at random from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0021\">Furthermore, the minimum unsatisfiability value over all assignments,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=\\min_{a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi),\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0022\">is called the <em>unsatisfiability value</em> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0023\">Each constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\in\\mathcal{C}\\)</span> represents the set of assignments that satisfy that constraint.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0024\">For example, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I=\\Sigma^{\\lvert I\\rvert}\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> is satisfied by every assignment, while if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I=\\emptyset\\)</span>, then no assignment satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-example-312-graph-coloring-problem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0025\">Example 3.1.2 (Graph Coloring Problem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0026\">The graph coloring problem (Example 1.2.4) is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSP.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0027\">Indeed, for a graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span>, set the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0028\">Set the sequence of variables to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=V\\)</span>.</span></p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0029\">Set the alphabet to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma=[k]\\)</span>.</span></p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0030\">For an edge set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E=\\{e_1,\\dots,e_m\\}\\)</span> with a fixed ordering of its edges, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}=(e_1,\\dots,e_m)\\)</span>.</span></p></li>\n<li><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0031\">Associate each constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_e\\)</span> with its edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span> and define it by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n(a_u,a_v)\\in c_e\\iff a_u\\ne a_v.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0032\">This defines the constraint.</span></p></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0033\">Then the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-colorable if and only if this CSP is a Yes instance.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-example-313-satisfiability-problem-for-systems-of-quadratic-equations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0034\">Example 3.1.3 (Satisfiability Problem for Systems of Quadratic Equations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0035\">The decision problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> defined in Theorem 1.2.13 is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSP.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0036\">Given an instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_1,\\dots,f_m\\colon\\mathbb{F}_2^n\\to\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>, take the variables to be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_1,\\dots,x_n\\)</span> and consider the constraints <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_i(x)=0\\)</span>; because the output of each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f_i\\)</span> depends on the values of at most three variables, this is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSP.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0037\">Henceforth, as in Example 3.1.3, when defining a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP we may sometimes give the definition by describing all variables and constraints without explicitly specifying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-311-relationship-with-pcps\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0038\">3.1.1 Relationship with PCPs</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0039\">A PCP verifier with random seed length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=r(n)\\)</span> and query complexity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> can be represented by a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma=\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>, and conversely a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP can be represented as a PCP verifier.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0040\">Indeed, consider a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-query PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0041\">Let the proof length be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\pi\\rvert=\\ell\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0042\">For fixed input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> and random seed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I_s\\subseteq[\\ell]\\)</span> be the set of indices of proof symbols read by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x;s)\\)</span> (where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I_s\\rvert\\le q\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0043\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x;s)\\)</span> defines a function from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^{I_s}\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0044\">By regarding this function as a constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_s\\)</span>, the verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)\\)</span> can be represented as an instance of a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0045\">The variable set of this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance corresponds to the proof <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0046\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in L\\)</span>, there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=1\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0047\">In other words, because there exists a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x;s)=1\\)</span> for every random seed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span>, the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance constructed above is a Yes instance.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0048\">Conversely, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\notin L\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(x)=0\\)</span> with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0049\">This means that, for the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance, even considering every assignment, at least a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> fraction of all constraints are not satisfied (that is, the value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> is at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span>).</span></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0050\">PCP verifier</span></th>\n<th><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0051\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP</span></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0052\">PCP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0053\">Assignment</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0054\">Decision with randomness fixed</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0055\">CSP constraint</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0056\">Probability of rejecting PCP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span></span></td>\n<td><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0057\">Unsatisfiability value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\pi)\\)</span> of the assignment</span></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0058\">Table 3.1: Correspondence between PCPs and CSPs</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0059\">Based on this correspondence, the PCP theorem can be stated in the language of CSPs.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-314-csp-version-of-the-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0060\">Theorem 3.1.4 (CSP Version of the PCP Theorem)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0061\">There exist a function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K(s)=s^{O(1)}\\)</span>, constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha&gt;0\\)</span>, and a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm having the following properties:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0062\">It receives an instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> and outputs a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0063\">If the size of the given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s=\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi_0)\\)</span>, this instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi)=K(s)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert=c\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0064\">Furthermore, this instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> satisfies the following:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0065\">If the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)=0\\)</span> (that is, it is a Yes instance of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>), then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0066\">If the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)&gt;0\\)</span> (that is, it is a No instance of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>), then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\ge\\alpha\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-315\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0067\">Remark 3.1.5</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0068\">In general, a CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> consisting of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\)</span> constraints is a No instance if and only if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)\\ge\\frac{1}{m}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0069\">In Theorem 3.1.4, however, the important point is that the size of the CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> obtained by the transformation becomes a constant <em>independent of</em> the size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\)</span> of the original CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0070\">We prove that constructing a PCP verifier is equivalent to transforming a CSP.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-316-equivalence-of-pcp-verifiers-and-csp-transformations\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0071\">Lemma 3.1.6 (Equivalence of PCP Verifiers and CSP Transformations)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0072\">Theorem 3.1.4 and Theorem 1.2.14 are equivalent.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-11\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0073\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0074\">We prove the two directions separately.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0075\"><em>Proof that Theorem 3.1.4 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)</span> Theorem 1.2.14.</em></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0076\">For some <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=O(\\log n)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;=O(1)\\)</span>, construct a PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> with seed length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\)</span> and query complexity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0077\">The verifier first receives an instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> and uses the algorithm of Theorem 3.1.4 to compute a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0078\">It also interprets the witness <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> as an assignment to this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>, and the PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0)\\)</span> repeats the following operation a sufficiently large constant number of times: choose a constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> uniformly at random, read the values of the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> for the variables contained in that constraint, and output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> and halt if this constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> is <em>not satisfied</em> by the assignment read.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0079\">If it has not halted after all the repetitions, it outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> and halts.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0080\">The number of repetitions made by this verifier is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(1)\\)</span>, and each repetition reads the values of at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> variables, so its query complexity is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;=O(q)=O(1)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0081\">Note that because the alphabet size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c\\)</span> is constant, the total number of proof bits read is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q&#39;\\cdot\\lceil\\log_2 c\\rceil=O(1)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0082\">Also, because the random seed is used to choose a random constraint, its length is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log K(\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi_0)))=O(\\log\\operatorname{size}(\\varphi_0))\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0083\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> is a Yes instance, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> is also a Yes instance, so by taking <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> to be a satisfying assignment, all constraints <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> are satisfied and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0)=1\\)</span> with probability <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> (over the randomness used to choose constraints).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0084\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> is a No instance, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\ge\\alpha\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0085\">Therefore, for every assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span>, the probability that a uniformly random constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_i\\)</span> is satisfied is at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1-\\alpha\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0086\">Thus, if this operation is repeated <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lceil10/\\alpha\\rceil=O(1)\\)</span> times, with probability at least <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1/3\\)</span> an unsatisfied constraint is chosen at least once, and the verifier outputs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0087\"><em>Proof that Theorem 1.2.14 <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Rightarrow\\)</span> Theorem 3.1.4.</em></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0088\">By assumption, there exists a PCP verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> with random seed length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r=O(\\log n)\\)</span> and query complexity <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0089\">On input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span>, algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> enumerates all random seeds <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in\\{0,1\\}^r\\)</span> and outputs the CSP obtained by adding, for each one, the constraint \"<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0;s)=1\\)</span>.”</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0090\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi\\)</span> is regarded as the variables, each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi(\\varphi_0;s)\\)</span> reads the values of at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> variables, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\((c_s)_{s\\in\\{0,1\\}^r}\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP consisting of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^r=n^{O(1)}\\)</span> constraints.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0091\">Moreover, because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^\\pi\\)</span> is a polynomial-time algorithm, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> is also a polynomial-time algorithm.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0092\">Accordingly, from this point onward we focus on proving Theorem 3.1.4.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-312-constraint-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0093\">3.1.2 Constraint Graphs</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0094\">The proof of the PCP theorem, that is, the proof of Theorem 3.1.4, is obtained by first transforming <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>, a special case of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSP, into a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSP and then repeatedly applying various transformations to this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSP.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0095\">Because each constraint in a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSP depends on assignments to at most one variable, its constraints can be described as a graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0096\">Such a graph representation of a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSP is called a <em>constraint graph</em>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-317-constraint-graph\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0097\">Definition 3.1.7 (Constraint Graph)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0098\">For a 2-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span>, the tuple <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> determined below is called a <em>constraint graph</em><a href=\"#en-fn9\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref9\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>9</sup></a>:</span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0100\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> is a graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0101\">Its vertex set is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V=X\\)</span>, and its edge set is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E=\\mathcal{I}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0102\">The graphs considered here may have parallel edges and self-loops<a href=\"#en-fn10\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref10\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>10</sup></a>, and in particular, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I_i\\rvert=1\\)</span>, the corresponding edge is a self-loop.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0104\">See Definition 3.2.1 for details.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0105\">The alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0106\">For the sequence of constraints <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}&#39;=(c&#39;_e)_{e\\in E}\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e=c_e\\)</span> if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert e\\rvert=2\\)</span>, and set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e=\\{(u,u)\\in\\Sigma^2\\colon u\\in e\\}\\)</span> if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert e\\rvert=1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0107\">Thus every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\)</span> is a subset of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^2\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0108\">For a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> and an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, define its <em>unsatisfiability value</em> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)=\\Pr_{e\\sim E}[a(e)\\notin c_e],\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0109\">where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(e)=(a(u),a(v))\\in\\Sigma^2\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u&lt;v\\)</span>, and define the unsatisfiability value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=\\min_{a\\in\\Sigma^V}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0110\">This gives the definition.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0111\">For a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>, define its <em>size</em> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{size}(G)=\\lvert V\\rvert+\\lvert E\\rvert.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0112\">This gives the definition.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-318-relationship-between-input-length-and-size\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0113\">Remark 3.1.8 (Relationship between Input Length and Size)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0114\">We assume that the alphabet size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span> of every constraint graph considered in these lectures is always a constant, that is, a value independent of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0115\">Under this assumption, consider the number of bits required to specify a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0116\">The graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> can be represented by an adjacency matrix using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert^2\\)</span> bits.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0117\">A constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\subseteq\\Sigma^2\\)</span> associated with each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span> can be represented by listing, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a,b)\\in\\Sigma^2\\)</span>, a bit indicating whether <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a,b)\\in c&#39;_e\\)</span>, so all constraints can be represented with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\cdot\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert^2\\)</span> bits.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0118\">Therefore, the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> can be represented using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\rvert^2+\\lvert E\\rvert\\cdot\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert^2=O(\\operatorname{size}(G)^2)\\)</span> bits.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0119\">Consequently, when discussing whether an algorithm that receives a constraint graph as input runs in polynomial time, it suffices to discuss whether its running time is polynomial in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0120\">Constraint graphs are intended to represent 2-CSPs, but a general <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP can also be represented as a constraint graph.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-319-constraint-graph-representation-of-a-k-csp\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0121\">Lemma 3.1.9 (Constraint-Graph Representation of a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0122\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, there exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> such that the following holds:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0123\">It receives a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\Sigma,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C})\\)</span> and outputs a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma^k,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> satisfying the following properties.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0124\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\le ck\\cdot(\\lvert X\\rvert+\\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0125\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0126\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\ge\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-12\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0127\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0128\">Given the variable set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=\\{\\mathrm{x}_1,\\dots,\\mathrm{x}_n\\}\\)</span> and constraint set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}=(c_I)_{I\\in\\mathcal{I}}\\)</span> of the input <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP, the newly constructed constraint graph has the following structure:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0129\">For each variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_i\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>, introduce a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span>, and for each constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span> (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\in\\mathcal{I}\\)</span>), introduce a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0130\">If the variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_i\\)</span> occurs in the constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span>, that is, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_i\\in I\\)</span>, draw an edge between the two vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span>; let <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> be the resulting bipartite graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0131\">For simplicity, assume below that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I\\rvert=k\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(I\\in\\mathcal{I}\\)</span> (if constraints not having this property exist, allow each such constraint to read the same variable more than once and make the corresponding edges parallel edges, so that the number of reads is exactly <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0132\">In this bipartite graph, every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_i\\)</span> has degree exactly <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>, and its size is bounded by a constant factor depending only on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/k-to-two.png\" alt=\"Example construction of a constraint graph. For example, the constraint associated with variable y_1 depends on variables x_1, x_2, and x_3.\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0133\">Figure 3.1: Example construction of a constraint graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0134\">For example, the constraint corresponding to the variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{y}_1\\)</span> depends on the variables <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{x}_1,\\mathrm{x}_2,\\mathrm{x}_3\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0135\">Set the alphabet of the new constraint graph to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^k\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0136\">The constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\)</span> corresponding to each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{x_i,y_I\\}\\)</span> is satisfied if and only if the assignments <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\leftarrow\\vec{\\sigma}=(\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_k)\\in\\Sigma^k\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\leftarrow\\vec{\\tau}=(\\tau_1,\\dots,\\tau_k)\\in\\Sigma^k\\)</span> satisfy the following conditions, where the neighbors of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span> are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_{i_1},\\dots,x_{i_k}\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i_1&lt;\\dots&lt;i_k\\)</span>:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0137\">The partial assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}\\leftarrow\\tau_1,\\dots,\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}\\leftarrow\\tau_k)\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0138\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\tau_i=\\sigma_1\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0139\">Note that among the components of the assignment to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x_i\\)</span>, the values <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_2,\\dots,\\sigma_k\\)</span> are ignored here.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/k-to-two2.png\" alt=\"Example assignment. Here, if (tau_1,tau_2,tau_3) satisfies the constraint for y_1 and sigma_j=tau_j, then the constraint-graph constraint corresponding to edge {y_1,x_j} is satisfied.\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0140\">Figure 3.2: Example assignment.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0141\">Here, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\tau_1,\\tau_2,\\tau_3)\\)</span> satisfies the constraint for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{y}_1\\)</span> and moreover <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_j=\\tau_j\\)</span> (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j=1,2,3\\)</span>), then the constraint-graph constraint corresponding to the edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{y_1,x_j\\}\\)</span> is satisfied.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0142\">If the original <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> is satisfiable, that is, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>, then for its satisfying assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, define an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to\\Sigma^k\\)</span> to the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;a&#39;(x_i)=(a(\\mathrm{x}_i),\\underbrace{\\ast,\\dots,\\ast}_{k-1}) \\quad (i\\in[n]),\\\\\n&amp;a&#39;(y_I)=\\left(a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_1}),\\dots,a(\\mathrm{x}_{i_k}),\\sigma_1\\right) \\quad (I=\\{i_1,\\dots,i_k\\}\\in\\mathcal{I}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0143\">Here, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ast\\)</span> may be any element of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0144\">This assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> satisfies every constraint of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0145\">Conversely, for an arbitrary assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to\\Sigma^k\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, define an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon X\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\na(\\mathrm{x}_i)=a&#39;(x_i)_1 \\quad (i\\in[n]).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0146\">This defines the assignment.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0147\">If the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> satisfies the constraints of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> corresponding to all edges <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{x_i,y_I\\}\\)</span> (<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\forall i\\in I\\)</span>) incident to the vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y_I\\)</span>, then the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> satisfies the constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0148\">Taking the contrapositive, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> does not satisfy <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_I\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> fails to satisfy the constraint corresponding to at least one edge in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{\\{x_i,y_I\\}\\}_{i\\in I}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0149\">That is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G)\\ge\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0150\">Therefore, choosing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> so that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span> gives</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G)\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;\\varphi)}{k}\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)}{k},\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0151\">which proves the claim.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-32-introduction-of-multigraphs-and-expander-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0001\">3.2 Introduction of Multigraphs and Expander Graphs</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0002\">For the various operations on constraint graphs that follow, we first give a formal definition of a multigraph.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-321-multigraph\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0003\">Definition 3.2.1 (Multigraph)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0004\">A pair <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> consisting of a finite set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> and a multiset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span> is called a <strong>multigraph</strong>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0005\">Here, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span> is a multiset consisting of elements of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\cup \\binom{V}{2}\\)</span>, and an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert e\\rvert=1\\)</span> is called a <strong>self-loop</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0006\">Define the <strong>weight</strong> between two vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nw(u,v) = \\lvert \\{e\\in E \\colon e = \\{u,v\\}\\}\\rvert\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0007\">and define the <strong>degree</strong> of a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\deg(u) = \\sum_{v\\in V} w(u,v).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0008\">This defines the degree.<a href=\"#en-fn11\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref11\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>11</sup></a></span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0009\">Also, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W=(w(u,v))_{u,v\\in V}\\)</span> is called the <strong>weight matrix</strong>, and the matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span> defined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P(u,v):=\\frac{w(u,v)}{\\deg(u)}\\)</span> is called the <strong>transition probability matrix</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0011\">The vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d=(\\deg(v))_{v\\in V}\\in \\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span> listing the degrees of the graph is called the <strong>degree vector</strong>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0012\">The degree vector is given by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nd = W\\mathbf{1}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0013\">This gives the degree vector.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0014\">If the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> contains no self-loops, the handshake lemma gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sum_{u\\in V}\\deg(u) = 2\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>; otherwise, since the sum of the degrees counts each self-loop only once, if the number of self-loops is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>, then</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-hand-shake-multigraph\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\sum_{u\\in V} \\deg(u) = 2\\lvert E\\rvert - \\ell \\le 2\\lvert E\\rvert \\tag{3.1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0015\">holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0016\">We state the definitions of regular graphs and expander graphs below.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-322-regularity-and-expansion\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0017\">Definition 3.2.2 (Regularity and Expansion)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0018\">When every vertex of an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>-vertex multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span> has degree <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is said to be <strong><span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0019\">Also, when the eigenvalues <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1=\\lambda_1\\ge \\lambda_2\\ge \\cdots \\ge \\lambda_n \\ge -1\\)</span> of the transition probability matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span> satisfy</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\lambda(P):=\\max\\{\\lvert\\lambda_2\\rvert, \\lvert\\lambda_n\\rvert\\} \\le \\lambda,\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0020\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is said to be a <strong><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander</strong>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0021\">If a multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is regular, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span> is a symmetric matrix and hence immediately has real eigenvalues, but it can be shown to have real eigenvalues even in the general case.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0022\">Furthermore, Gershgorin's theorem implies that the absolute values of those eigenvalues are at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0023\">Also, since the all-ones vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}\\)</span> is an eigenvector of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span> with eigenvalue <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_1=1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-7\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0024\">Exercise 7</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0025\">Show that the transition probability matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span> of any multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> has real eigenvalues.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0026\">It is known that the multiplicity of the first eigenvalue <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> equals the number of connected components of the graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0027\">Here we use the following special case of this fact.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proposition-323\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0028\">Proposition 3.2.3</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0029\">If a multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is connected, then the multiplicity of the eigenvalue <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> of the transition probability matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>; that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_2&lt;1\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-321-properties-of-regular-expanders\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0030\">3.2.1 Properties of Regular Expanders</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0031\">In this section, we verify that properties of simple graphs that are regular and have expansion also hold for multigraphs.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0032\">In particular, note that regularity makes the transition probability matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\)</span> symmetric.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-324-expander-mixing-lemma\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0033\">Lemma 3.2.4 (Expander Mixing Lemma)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0034\">Suppose that a connected <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>-vertex multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0035\">For two vertex subsets <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S,T\\subseteq V\\)</span>, let</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW(S,T) = \\sum_{u \\in S, v\\in T} w(u,v).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0036\">Then, for any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S,T\\subseteq V\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\left\\lvert W(S,T) - \\frac{d}{n}\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert \\right\\rvert \\le \\frac{\\lambda d}{n} \\sqrt{\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus S\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus T\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0037\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0038\">In particular, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq V\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S\\rvert\\le n/2\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW(S,V\\setminus S) \\ge (1-\\lambda)\\frac{d\\lvert S\\rvert}{2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0039\">holds.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-325-intuitive-meaning\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0040\">Remark 3.2.5 (Intuitive Meaning)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0041\">For simplicity, consider a graph without self-loops.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0042\">If this graph is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> vertices, it has <span class=\"math inline\">\\(nd/2\\)</span> edges in total.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0043\">Since there are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\binom{n}{2}\\approx n^2/2\\)</span> vertex pairs in total, the edge density is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d/n\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0044\">Now, if we assume that the graph's edges are distributed \"uniformly\" throughout <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\binom{V}{2}\\)</span>, then for any fixed vertex subsets <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S,T\\subseteq V\\)</span>, the number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W(S,T)\\)</span> of edges crossing between them is expected to be approximately <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d/n)\\cdot \\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0045\">When the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> has expansion, the expander mixing lemma gives an upper bound on the deviation from this expected value.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-13\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0046\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0047\">The \"in particular\" part follows by substituting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T=V\\setminus S\\)</span> into the first assertion and using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V\\setminus S\\rvert\\ge n/2\\)</span>, so we prove the first assertion.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0048\">For the all-ones matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(J \\in \\mathbb{R}^{V\\times V}\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(M:=P - \\frac{1}{n}J\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0049\">Also, for a vertex subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq V\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S\\in \\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathbf{1}_S(u) = \\begin{cases}\n1 &amp; \\text{if } u\\in S \\\\\n0 &amp; \\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0050\">This defines <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0051\">Let the two vectors <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span> be</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;x = \\mathbf{1}_S - \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert}{n}\\mathbf{1},\\\\\n&amp;y = \\mathbf{1}_T - \\frac{\\lvert T\\rvert}{n}\\mathbf{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0052\">These definitions specify <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(y\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0053\">Since the vectors <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x,y\\)</span> are orthogonal to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S = x + \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert}{n}\\mathbf{1}\\)</span> is the orthogonal decomposition of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{1}_S\\)</span>; also using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W\\mathbf{1}=d\\mathbf{1}\\)</span>, we have</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(S,T) &amp;= \\mathbf{1}_S^T W \\mathbf{1}_T \\\\\n&amp;= x^T W y + \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert}{n^2}\\mathbf{1}^T W \\mathbf{1} \\\\\n&amp;= x^\\top W y + \\frac{d}{n}\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0054\">Thus the displayed equality holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0055\">Therefore,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\lvert W(S,T) - \\frac{d}{n}\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert \\right\\rvert\n&amp;= \\lvert x^\\top W y\\rvert \\le \\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\lVert W y\\rVert_2 &amp;&amp; \\text{by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality} \\\\\n&amp;\\le d \\lambda \\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\lVert y\\rVert_2 &amp;&amp; \\text{by the relation between Rayleigh quotients and eigenvalues} \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{\\lambda d}{n} \\sqrt{\\lvert S\\rvert\\lvert T\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus S\\rvert\\lvert V\\setminus T\\rvert} &amp;&amp; \\text{since $\\lVert x\\rVert_2^2 = \\frac{\\lvert S\\rvert(n-\\lvert S\\rvert)}{n}$}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0056\">This gives the desired bound.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0057\">Next, we define the <strong>powering</strong> operation on graphs.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-326-powering-a-graph\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0058\">Definition 3.2.6 (Powering a Graph)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0059\">For a multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=(V,E)\\)</span> with weight matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\ge 0\\)</span>, define the multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k=(V,E^k)\\)</span> to be the multigraph whose weight matrix is</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW&#39; = W^k.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0060\">That is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> is the multigraph having <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W^k\\)</span> as its weight matrix.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0061\">For each pair of vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V\\)</span> of the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, one obtains <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> by adding as many edges between <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span> as there are length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> paths between them.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-327-powering-regular-expanders\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0062\">Lemma 3.2.7 (Powering Regular Expanders)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0063\">Suppose that an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>-vertex multigraph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0064\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d^k\\)</span>-regular and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda^k\\)</span>-expander.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-14\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0065\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0066\">Since the weight matrix of the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> obtained by powering is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W&#39;=W^k\\)</span>, its degree vector is</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW&#39;\\mathbf{1} = W^k\\mathbf{1} = d^k\\mathbf{1},\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0067\">and therefore <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d^k\\)</span>-regular.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0068\">Furthermore, since the transition probability matrix of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> is given by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P^k\\)</span>, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1=\\lambda_1 \\ge \\dots \\ge \\lambda_n \\ge -1\\)</span>, then the eigenvalues of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P^k\\)</span> are <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1=\\lambda_1^k \\ge \\dots \\ge \\lambda_n^k \\ge -1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0069\">Now, since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\max\\{\\lvert\\lambda_2\\rvert, \\lvert\\lambda_n\\rvert\\} \\le \\lambda\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\max\\{\\lvert\\lambda_2^k\\rvert, \\lvert\\lambda_n^k\\rvert\\} \\le \\lambda^k\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0070\">Hence, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^k\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda^k\\)</span>-expander.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-322-construction-of-expander-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0071\">3.2.2 Construction of Expander Graphs</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0072\">Expander graphs are known to have various applications arising from their structure.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0073\">In particular, it is known that, for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\ge 2\\)</span>, an expander graph on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> vertices can be constructed in time <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n^{O(1)}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-328-construction-of-expander-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0074\">Theorem 3.2.8 (Construction of Expander Graphs)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0075\">There exist constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\ge 3\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0&lt;1\\)</span>, and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0076\">Given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n=(\\underbrace{1,\\dots,1}_n)\\)</span> as input, algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> outputs the adjacency matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W\\)</span> of a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-regular, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-expander graph on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> vertices.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0077\">The proof of Theorem 3.2.8 is obtained by making a minor modification to the breakthrough result [RVW02] on the construction of expander graphs.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-329-construction-of-a-family-of-expander-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0078\">Theorem 3.2.9 (Construction of a Family of Expander Graphs)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0079\">There exist constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d&#39;_0\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&#39;_0&lt;1\\)</span>, and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0080\">For each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in \\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^{2^k}\\)</span> as input, it outputs the adjacency matrix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W&#39;\\)</span> of a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d&#39;_0\\)</span>-regular, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&#39;_0\\)</span>-expander graph on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^k\\)</span> vertices.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0081\">The proof of Theorem 3.2.9 can be obtained using a graph product technique called the zig-zag product, but we omit it because it falls outside the scope of this lecture.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0082\">To prove Theorem 3.2.8, that is, to obtain an expander graph with an arbitrary number of vertices, first apply Theorem 3.2.9 for a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{k-1}&lt; n \\le 2^k\\)</span>, then suitably contract pairs of vertices until the graph has <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span> vertices, and make it regular by adding self-loops or parallel edges to vertices as necessary.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0083\">Moreover, by Lemma 3.2.7, increasing the degree makes it possible to bring the eigenvalue <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span> arbitrarily close to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0084\">In the gap amplification lemma, we transform a given constraint graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0085\">To simplify notation, we will apply terminology expressing graph properties directly to constraint graphs as well.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0086\">For example, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> is connected, we say that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> is connected.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0087\">Also, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> is regular, we say that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> is regular.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-33-constant-degree-expanderization-of-constraint-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0001\">3.3 Constant-Degree Expanderization of Constraint Graphs</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0002\">Here we show that a given constraint graph can be transformed so that it has constant-degree regularity and the expander property without greatly decreasing its unsatisfiability value.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-331-constant-degree-expanderization\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0003\">Lemma 3.3.1 (Constant-Degree Expanderization)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0004\">There exist constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&lt;1\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta&gt;0\\)</span>, and a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> that, given any constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> as input, outputs another constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> satisfying the following properties:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0005\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander with self-loops.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0006\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c\\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0007\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0008\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\beta\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0009\">The proof of this lemma consists of two steps: degree reduction and expanderization.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-331-degree-reduction\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0010\">3.3.1 Degree Reduction</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0011\">First, we give a lemma that transforms a given constraint graph into a constant-degree regular graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0012\">Under this transformation, the value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> changes by only a constant factor.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-332-degree-reduction-lemma\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0013\">Lemma 3.3.2 (Degree Reduction Lemma)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0014\">There exist constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span>, and a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm that, given any constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> as input, outputs another constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> satisfying the following properties:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0015\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0016\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V&#39;\\rvert \\le 2\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0017\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0018\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge c&#39;\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-15\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0019\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0020\">Let the given constraint graph be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>, and let the constraint graph obtained by the transformation be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0021\">Before giving the formal construction, we first show an illustrative example.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/degree_reduction.png\" alt=\"Example of the degree-reduction transformation\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0022\">Figure 3.3: Example of the degree-reduction transformation.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0023\">The vertex sets inside the orange regions are clouds, and the edges within each cloud are constructed using Theorem 3.2.8, but here, for simplicity of the figure, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_d\\)</span> is taken to be a cycle of length <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0024\">The constraints on the black edges in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> are the same as those on the corresponding edges of the original graph, while the constraints on the red edges within the clouds are equality constraints.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0025\">For each vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> of the original graph,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n[u]=\\left\\{(u,e)\\in\\{u\\}\\times E \\colon e=\\{u,v\\}\\text{ for some }v\\in V\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0026\">will be called the <em><span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>-cloud</em> (as local terminology for this proof).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0027\">The new vertex set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V&#39;\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V&#39;=\\bigcup_{u\\in V}[u]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0028\">That is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\([u]\\)</span> is the set obtained by making as many copies of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> as there are edges incident to it (counting a self-loop as one).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0029\">Next, construct the edge set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E&#39;\\)</span> as follows.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0030\">First, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\((X_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb{N}}\\)</span> be the family of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(n\\)</span>-vertex, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-regular <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expanders constructed by Theorem 3.2.8.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0031\">For each vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> of the original graph, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d=\\lvert[u]\\rvert\\)</span>, construct on the vertex set <span class=\"math inline\">\\([u]\\)</span> a graph isomorphic to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_d\\)</span>, and denote it by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(([u],E_u)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0032\">Then define the edge set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\)</span> within the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>-clouds by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nE_{\\mathrm{inner}}=\\bigcup_{u\\in V}E_u\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0033\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0034\">Next, define the edge set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\\)</span> connecting distinct clouds by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nE_{\\mathrm{outer}}=\\left\\{\\left\\{(u,e),(v,e)\\right\\}\\colon e\\notin[u]\\cap[v]\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0035\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0036\">Then the edge set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E&#39;\\)</span> of the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nE&#39;=E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\cup E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0037\">.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0038\">Next, define the constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e&#39;}\\)</span> of each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in E&#39;\\)</span> as follows:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0039\">If an edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\\)</span> connects two clouds, its constraint is the same as the constraint on the corresponding original edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0040\">That is, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;=\\{(u,e),(v,e)\\}\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e&#39;}=c_e\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0041\">If an edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\)</span> lies within a cloud, set its constraint to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e&#39;}=\\{(\\sigma,\\sigma)\\colon\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\}\\)</span>, namely, the equality constraint.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0042\">We verify that the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> obtained in this way satisfies all the claims of the lemma.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0043\">First, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d_0+1)\\)</span>-regular.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0044\">Indeed, each vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\in V&#39;\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is incident to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span> edges within its cloud and one edge between clouds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0045\">Moreover, the only new edges added when constructing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> are those in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\)</span>, and there are at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sum_{u\\in V}\\frac{d_0\\deg_G(u)}{2}\\le d_0\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span> of them (where we used equation (3.1)).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0046\">Therefore,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-bound_of_E-prime\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert=\\lvert E_{\\mathrm{inner}}\\rvert+\\lvert E_{\\mathrm{outer}}\\rvert\\le(d_0+1)\\lvert E\\rvert \\tag{3.2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0047\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0048\">Also, because the cardinality of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V&#39;\\)</span> equals the sum of the degrees, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert V&#39;\\rvert\\le2\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0049\">Next, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0050\">Indeed, for an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> satisfying all constraints of the original constraint graph, define an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V&#39;\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\na&#39;(u,e)=a(u)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0051\">; then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> satisfies the constraints of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> (because every vertex in the same cloud is assigned the same value, all constraints on edges within clouds are satisfied, and the choice of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> ensures that all constraints corresponding to edges between clouds are satisfied).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0052\">We show the final claim, namely, that there exists a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)\\ge c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0053\">Define the constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-c_of_degree_reduction\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\nc&#39;=\\min\\left\\{\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8(d_0+1)},\\frac{1}{2(d_0+1)}\\right\\} \\tag{3.3}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0054\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0055\">Take any assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V&#39;\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0056\">For this assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>, define an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> of the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> by</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\na(u)=\\mathrm{Maj}((a&#39;(u,e))_{(u,e)\\in[u]})\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0057\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0058\">Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(\\cdot)\\)</span> is the majority function, with ties broken arbitrarily.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0059\">For example, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,2)=2\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,3,3)=3\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,2,3,3)=2\\)</span> (we could also set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathrm{Maj}(1,2,2,3,3)=3\\)</span>, but for convenience we choose the smaller number here).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0060\">Henceforth, for an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V&#39;\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, we call the value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u,e)\\)</span> assigned to a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\in V&#39;\\)</span> the <em>opinion</em> of the vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0061\">Similarly, for an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, we call the value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\)</span> assigned to a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> the opinion of the vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0062\">Intuitively, the opinion of vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> is the majority opinion among the vertices in the corresponding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>-cloud.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0063\">Define the edge set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)</span> to be the set of edges not satisfied by the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>, namely,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nF=\\left\\{e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\colon(a(u),a(v))\\notin c_e\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0064\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0065\">Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}=(c_e)_{e\\in E}\\)</span> is the collection of constraints of the original constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0066\">Similarly, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F&#39;\\subseteq E&#39;\\)</span> be the set of edges not satisfied by the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0067\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)=\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\frac{\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0068\">Define the vertex subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\subseteq V&#39;\\)</span> to be the set of vertices whose opinions were not selected by the majority vote of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>, namely,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nS=\\left\\{(u,e)\\in V&#39;\\colon a(u)\\ne a&#39;(u,e)\\right\\}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0069\">; for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in V\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v=S\\cap[v]\\)</span>, and for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma=\\{(u,e)\\in S^v\\colon a&#39;(u,e)=\\sigma\\}\\)</span> (Figure 3.4).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0070\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\)</span> is the majority opinion (that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma=a(v)\\)</span>), set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma=\\emptyset\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/majority_degree_reduction.png\" alt=\"Set of vertices not selected by majority vote\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0071\">Figure 3.4: Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S_v\\subseteq[v]\\)</span> be the set of vertices in the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>-cloud that were not selected by majority vote.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0072\">Consider the following two cases:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-case-1-lvert-srvertgefracoperatornameunsatag2lvert-ervert\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0073\">Case 1. <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S\\rvert\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0074\">For every vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in V\\)</span> and every opinion <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\setminus\\{a(v)\\}\\)</span> not selected by majority vote, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S^v_\\sigma\\rvert\\le\\frac{\\lvert[v]\\rvert}{2}\\)</span> (otherwise, this would contradict the fact that opinion <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)</span> was not selected by majority vote).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0075\">Because the graph inside the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>-cloud is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-regular <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-expander, the expander mixing lemma (Lemma 3.2.4) applied to its vertex subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma\\subseteq[v]\\)</span> gives</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nW(S^v_\\sigma,[v]\\setminus S^v_\\sigma)\\ge(1-\\lambda_0)\\cdot\\frac{d_0\\lvert S^v_\\sigma\\rvert}{2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0076\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0077\">Here <span class=\"math inline\">\\(W(S,T)\\)</span> is the number of edges of the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(([v],E_v)\\)</span> inside the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>-cloud that cross between <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0078\">Since the opinions at the endpoints of every edge crossing between <span class=\"math inline\">\\(S^v_\\sigma\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\([v]\\setminus S^v_\\sigma\\)</span> differ, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> does not satisfy any of these edges.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0079\">Therefore, all these edges belong to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0080\">Consequently,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert &amp;\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{v,\\sigma}W(S^v_\\sigma,[v]\\setminus S^v_\\sigma) \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{4}\\sum_{v,\\sigma}\\lvert S^v_\\sigma\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{4}\\lvert S\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\cdot\\lvert E\\rvert &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{assumption of Case 1} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8(d_0+1)}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\cdot\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert. &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{equation (3.2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0081\">In particular, it follows that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\frac{\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\ge\\frac{(1-\\lambda_0)d_0}{8(d_0+1)}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-case-2-lvert-srvertfracoperatornameunsatag2lvert-ervert\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0082\">Case 2. <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert S\\rvert&lt;\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span>.</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0083\">For any edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in F\\)</span>, consider the corresponding edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;=\\{(u,e),(v,e)\\}\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0084\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)=a&#39;(u,e)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(v)=a&#39;(v,e)\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span> have the same constraint, and hence <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e&#39;\\in F&#39;\\)</span> because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in F\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0085\">Otherwise, that is, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\ne a&#39;(u,e)\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(v)\\ne a&#39;(v,e)\\)</span>, then the opinion of at least one of <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\((v,e)\\)</span> was not selected by majority vote; that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u,e)\\in S\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\((v,e)\\in S\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0086\">The above implies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F\\rvert\\le\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert+\\lvert S\\rvert\\)</span>, so</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert &amp;\\ge\\lvert F\\rvert-\\lvert S\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\lvert E\\rvert\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)-\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{assumption of Case 2} \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2}\\lvert E\\rvert \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)}{2(d_0+1)}\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{equation (3.2)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0087\">, and hence</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)=\\frac{\\lvert F&#39;\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\ge\\frac{1}{2(d_0+1)}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0088\">holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0089\">Cases 1 and 2 imply that, for the constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;&gt;0\\)</span> defined by equation (3.3), <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G&#39;)\\ge c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\ge c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"en-332-expanderization\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0090\">3.3.2 Expanderization</span></h3>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0091\">Next, we give a lemma that transforms a given regular constraint graph into an expander graph.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-333-expanderization-lemma\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0092\">Lemma 3.3.3 (Expanderization Lemma)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0093\">There exist constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda&lt;1,d,d_0\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> satisfying the following:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0094\">Given as input a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>, algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> outputs a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> that is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d+d_0+1)\\)</span>-regular, has a self-loop at every vertex, and is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0095\">Moreover, this constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-16\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0096\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0097\">Let the input constraint graph be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0098\">The transformation is performed as follows:</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0099\">First, use Theorem 3.2.8 to construct a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d_0\\)</span>-regular <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-expander graph on the vertex set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>, and add its edge set to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span> (allowing multiple edges here).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0100\">Next, add a self-loop to each vertex.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0101\">For each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span> added in Steps 1 and 2, set the corresponding constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e\\)</span> to the trivial constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_e=\\Sigma^2\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0102\">Let the constraint graph obtained in this way be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0103\">Because the original constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d+d_0+1)\\)</span>-regular (note that a self-loop contributes <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> to the degree).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0104\">Also, by Step 2, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> has a self-loop at every vertex.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0105\">Next, we show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0106\">For any assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, the constraints corresponding to the edges added in Steps 1 and 2 are always satisfied, so the numbers of constraints <em>not</em> satisfied in the two graphs are equal.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0107\">Thus <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)=\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G&#39;)\\)</span>, and hence</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)&amp;=\\min_a\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G&#39;) \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\lvert E&#39;\\rvert}\\min_a\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G) \\\\\n&amp;=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0108\">holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0109\">Finally, we show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda:=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}+\\frac{\\lambda_0(d_0+1)}{d+d_0+1}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0110\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span> be the transition probability matrix of the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span> be the graph consisting of the edges added in Steps 1 and 2, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P_0\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span> be its transition probability matrix.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0111\">Also, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P&#39;\\in[0,1]^{V\\times V}\\)</span> be the transition probability matrix of the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> ultimately obtained.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0112\">Because the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular and the added graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d_0+1)\\)</span>-regular,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nP&#39;=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}P+\\frac{d_0+1}{d+d_0+1}P_0\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0113\">.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0114\">Furthermore, because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(P_0\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda_0\\)</span>-expander, for any vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\in\\mathbb{R}^V\\)</span> orthogonal to the all-ones vector <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\boldsymbol{1}\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^\\top P&#39;x&amp;=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}x^\\top Px+\\frac{d_0+1}{d+d_0+1}x^\\top P_0x \\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\lVert x\\rVert^2+\\frac{d_0+1}{d+d_0+1}\\lambda_0\\lVert x\\rVert^2 &amp;&amp; \\because\\ \\text{expander property of $P_0$} \\\\\n&amp;\\le\\left(\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}+\\frac{\\lambda_0(d_0+1)}{d+d_0+1}\\right)\\lVert x\\rVert^2\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0115\">and therefore <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda(P&#39;)\\le\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}+\\frac{\\lambda_0(d_0+1)}{d+d_0+1}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0116\">This completes the preparation for proving Lemma 3.3.1.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-lemma-331\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0117\">Proof of Lemma 3.3.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0118\">For the given constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, first run the algorithm of Lemma 3.3.2 with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> as input, and denote its output by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0119\">By the claim of Lemma 3.3.2, this constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0120\">Next, run the algorithm of Lemma 3.3.3 with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span> as input, and take its output to be the final output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0121\">By the claim of Lemma 3.3.3, this constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> has a self-loop at every vertex and is <span class=\"math inline\">\\((d+d_0+1)\\)</span>-regular and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0122\">Moreover, under this sequence of transformations, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;)\\)</span> is only a constant factor larger than <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0123\">Finally, consider the value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0124\">If the original constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> has <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then Lemmas 3.3.2 and 3.3.3 give <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1)=0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0125\">On the other hand,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1)\\ge\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\cdot c&#39;\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p07-0126\">Thus the claim has been proved for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\frac{d}{d+d_0+1}\\cdot c&#39;\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"en-chapter-4-proof-of-the-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0001\">Chapter 4 Proof of the PCP Theorem</span></h1>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0002\">This chapter gives a proof of the PCP theorem.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-41-gap-amplification-lemma\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0003\">4.1 Gap Amplification Lemma</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0004\">Dinur's proof of the PCP theorem is based on an approach that amplifies the unsatisfiability value of a given constraint graph in stages.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0005\">The gap amplification lemma plays the central role in this approach and guarantees that the unsatisfiability value of a constraint graph can be amplified.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0006\">The gap amplification lemma is stated as follows.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-411-gap-amplification-lemma\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0007\">Lemma 4.1.1 (Gap Amplification Lemma)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0008\">For every alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>, there exist two constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0,\\alpha=\\alpha(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)\\in (0,1)\\)</span>, an alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0\\)</span>, and a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> with the following properties:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0009\">Algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> takes as input a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal C\\rangle\\)</span> and outputs another constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,\\mathcal C&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> satisfying the following properties:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0010\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;)\\le c\\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0011\">- If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0012\">- If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)\\ge \\min\\{\\alpha, 2\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0013\">A proof of the PCP theorem (Theorem 3.1.4) is obtained by first using Lemma 3.1.9 to transform the NP-complete problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{ThreeQuadEQ}\\)</span> into a constraint graph and then amplifying its <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> value to a constant by repeatedly applying Lemma 4.1.1 to that constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log s)\\)</span> times.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-42-powering-constraint-graphs\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0014\">4.2 Powering Constraint Graphs</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0015\">By Lemma 3.3.1, any constraint graph can be transformed into a constant-degree expander.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0016\">In this section, we show that the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> value of such a constraint graph with the constant-degree expander property can be amplified by considering an operation called <em>powering</em>, defined below.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-definition-421-powering-a-constraint-graph\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0017\">Definition 4.2.1 (Powering a Constraint Graph)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0018\">For a parameter <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\in\\mathbb N\\)</span> and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal C\\rangle\\)</span> in which every vertex has a self-loop, define the power <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell = \\langle(V,\\mathbf E),\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}},\\mathcal C^\\ell\\rangle\\)</span> as follows:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0019\">- The vertex set is the same set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0020\">Assume that the elements of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> are ordered as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v_1&lt;\\dots &lt; v_n\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0021\">- Let the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,\\mathbf E)\\)</span> be the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)^\\ell\\)</span> obtained by powering (Definition 3.2.6).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0022\">Namely, for every pair of vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V\\)</span>, place as many parallel edges between <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span> as there are length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> <span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>-paths (the numbers of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(uv\\)</span>-paths and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(vu\\)</span>-paths agree, so this can be defined as an undirected graph).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0023\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf E\\)</span> denote the resulting multiset of edges, and write one of its elements as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e = \\{u,v\\} \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0024\">- The alphabet is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0025\">Here, an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}=(\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_{d^{\\ell}}) \\in \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span> to a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> has the following meaning: let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Gamma(u)\\)</span> be the set of vertices at distance at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> from <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> in the original <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, and list its elements in increasing order of vertex number as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Gamma(u)=\\{v_1,\\dots,v_\\ell\\}\\)</span> (the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regularity of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell \\le d^{\\ell}\\)</span>).<a href=\"#en-fn12\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref12\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>12</sup></a></span></p>\n<!-- end footnote -->\n\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0027\">Then regard <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\)</span> as a map assigning <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}_u\\in \\Sigma\\)</span> to each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\in\\Gamma(u)\\)</span>, and call <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}_v\\)</span> <em><span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>'s opinion about <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span></em>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0028\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell &lt; d^{\\ell}\\)</span>, two distinct elements <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma},\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;\\in\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span> may be regarded as the same map (they are not distinguished when determining whether a constraint is satisfied).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0029\">- The constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf c_{\\mathbf e}\\in \\mathcal C^\\ell\\)</span> corresponding to an edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e = \\{u,v\\} \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span> is satisfied by assignments <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\vec{\\sigma},\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;)\\in \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}} \\times \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span> that satisfy the following two conditions:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0030\">For every edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{s,t\\}\\in E\\)</span> of the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> (where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s&lt;t\\)</span>),</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0031\"> - if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in \\Gamma(u), t\\in\\Gamma(v)\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\vec{\\sigma}(s), \\vec{\\sigma}&#39;(t))\\in c_e\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0032\"> - if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(s\\in\\Gamma(v), t\\in\\Gamma(u)\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;(s), \\vec{\\sigma}(t))\\in c_e\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/gap_amplification.png\" alt=\"Illustration of powering a constraint graph\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0033\">Figure 4.1: Illustration of the power <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span> of a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0034\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma},\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;\\in\\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span> are assigned to vertices <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\)</span>, respectively, regard them as the functions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\colon \\Gamma(u)\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}&#39;\\colon \\Gamma(v)\\to\\Sigma\\)</span>, respectively.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0035\">Since the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> has self-loops, opinions are defined for all vertices at distance at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0036\">We always assume that the parameter <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> is a constant independent of the size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span> of the constraint graph.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0037\">The constraint graph obtained by powering has an amplified <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> value relative to the original constraint graph.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-422-properties-of-powering\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0038\">Lemma 4.2.2 (Properties of Powering)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0039\">There exists a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\beta(\\lambda,d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)\\)</span> such that the following holds:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0040\">For a sufficiently large parameter <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell=\\ell(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert,\\lambda) \\le \\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span> and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal C\\rangle\\)</span> in which every vertex has a self-loop, define the power <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell = \\langle(V,\\mathbf E),\\Sigma^{\\ell},\\mathcal C^\\ell\\rangle\\)</span> as in Definition 4.2.1.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0041\">Then</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0042\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G^\\ell) \\le \\operatorname{size}(G)\\cdot d^\\ell\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0043\">- If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)=0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0044\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell) \\ge \\beta\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-remark-423-meaning-of-constant\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0045\">Remark 4.2.3 (Meaning of \"Constant\")</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0046\">Although the word \"constant\" is used frequently in statements and proofs, here we fix <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span> as universal constants and also call values such as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta,\\ell\\)</span> determined by them constants.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0047\">However, constants do not depend on the number of vertices or edges of the graph.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-17\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0048\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0049\">Since the definition gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathbf E\\rvert \\le \\lvert V\\rvert d^{\\ell}\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G^\\ell) \\le \\operatorname{size}(G)\\cdot d^\\ell\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0050\">Also, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then for an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V \\to \\Sigma\\)</span> satisfying every constraint, assign to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u)\\)</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\vec{\\sigma}(u)\\colon w \\mapsto a(w)\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0051\">a value <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\in\\Sigma^{d^\\ell}\\)</span> that can be regarded as the function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\vec{\\sigma}\\colon \\Gamma(w)\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> defined above (if there is more than one candidate, any one may be used).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0052\">The assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to\\Sigma^{\\ell}\\)</span> defined in this way satisfies every constraint, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)=0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0053\">Finally, we prove a lower bound on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0054\">For simplicity, assume that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> is even (if it is odd, the same proof works by replacing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell/2\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lfloor\\ell/2\\rfloor\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0055\">For a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>, let the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span>-valued random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}_j(u)\\)</span> be the final vertex reached by a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(j\\)</span> random walk starting at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> on the graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> (length means the number of edges traversed).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0056\">For an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V \\to \\Sigma^{\\ell}\\)</span> in the powered constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>, define an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> for the original constraint graph by</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-argmax-of-RW\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\na(u) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{\\tau \\in \\Sigma} \\left\\{\\Pr\\left[a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_{\\ell/2}(u))_u = \\tau\\right]\\right\\}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0057\">by this equation.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0058\">Because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}_{\\ell/2}(u)\\in \\Gamma(u)\\)</span> always holds, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}(u))_u\\)</span> always exists.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0059\">In other words, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\)</span> is the opinion that a random walk starting at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> is most likely to \"pick up.\"</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0060\">For an appropriate constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\beta(\\lambda,d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>, the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V \\to \\Sigma\\)</span> defined in this way has the following property:</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-lower-bound-of-UNSAT\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell) \\ge \\beta\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\right\\}. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0061\">We will show later that it satisfies this inequality.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0062\">Applying (4.2) to an <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell)=\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)\\)</span> and then substituting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\le \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span> yields the desired lower bound on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G^\\ell)\\)</span> and completes the proof.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"en-proof-of-42\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0063\">Proof of (4.2).</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0064\">Fix an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon V\\to \\Sigma^{d^{\\ell}}\\)</span> and define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span> by (4.1).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0065\">Let the edge subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_0\\subseteq E\\)</span> be the set of edges not satisfied by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\)</span>, and choose its subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq F_0\\)</span> as follows:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0066\">- If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F_0\\rvert \\le \\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F_0=F\\)</span>;</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0067\">- otherwise, fix any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F\\rvert = \\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\right\\rfloor\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0068\">Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\ge x/2\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(x\\ge 1\\)</span>, using the assumption <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\ge 1\\)</span> gives</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-abs-F\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lvert F\\rvert &amp;\\ge \\min\\left\\{\\lvert F_0\\rvert, \\left\\lfloor\\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\right\\rfloor\\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\min\\left\\{\\lvert F_0\\rvert, \\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{\\ell}\\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{\\lvert E\\rvert}{2}\\min\\left\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\right\\}\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0069\">(the assumption <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert E\\rvert\\ge \\ell\\)</span> is used only here).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0070\">An edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span> of the powered graph corresponds to a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> path in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0071\">We therefore write <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e = (u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span> (where <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in E\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[\\ell]\\)</span>) and, to distinguish these from edges of the original graph, call <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span> a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>-edge and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\in\\mathbf E\\)</span> a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>-edge.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0072\">For the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>th edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e_i:=\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\)</span> of a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^\\ell\\)</span>-edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e=(u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span>, consider the following conditions:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0073\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e_i \\in F\\)</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0074\">- <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u_{0})_{u_{i-1}} = a(u_{i-1})\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;(u_\\ell)_{u_i} = a(u_i)\\)</span></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0075\">When both conditions hold, we call the edge <em>bad</em>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0076\">When <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\)</span> is chosen uniformly at random, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_i\\in\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> if the edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e_i\\)</span> is bad and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> otherwise (these are random variables).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0077\">Furthermore, for</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\nI = \\left[\\frac{\\ell}{2} - \\sqrt{\\ell}, \\frac{\\ell}{2} + \\sqrt{\\ell}\\right] \\cap \\mathbb{Z}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0078\">let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B=\\sum_{i\\in I}B_i\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0079\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B&gt;0\\)</span>, then the constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{\\mathbf e}\\)</span> of the edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\)</span> is not satisfied by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0080\">Thus, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell) \\ge \\Pr_{\\mathbf e\\sim \\mathbf E}[B\\ge 1]\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0081\">By the Paley-Zygmund inequality (Lemma A.1.3), we can obtain a lower bound on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[B\\ge 1]\\)</span> by estimating <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B]\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B^2]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0082\">They can be estimated as follows.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-424-expectation-of-b\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0083\">Claim 4.2.4 (Expectation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span>)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0084\">There exists a sufficiently small constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C=C(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span> such that the expectation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\)</span> satisfies</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathbb E[B] \\ge C\\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-425-expectation-of-b2\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0085\">Claim 4.2.5 (Expectation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B^2\\)</span>)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0086\">There exists a sufficiently large constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C&#39;=C&#39;(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span> such that the expectation of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B^2\\)</span> satisfies</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\mathbb E[B^2] \\le C&#39;\\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0087\">Proofs of these claims will be given later.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0088\">Here we use them to prove (4.2).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0089\">Indeed, by Lemma A.1.3, Claim 4.2.4, and Claim 4.2.5, for the constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta=\\frac{C^2}{2C&#39;}&gt;0\\)</span> we have</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;G^\\ell) &amp;\\ge \\Pr[B\\ge 1] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{\\mathbb E[B]^2}{\\mathbb E[B^2]} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\frac{C^2}{C&#39;} \\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\beta \\sqrt{\\ell} \\cdot \\min\\left\\{\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G), \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\right\\} &amp;&amp; \\text{by (4.3)}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0090\">which proves the claim.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0091\">It remains to prove the final two claims.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0092\">The expander property of the constraint graph is used only in the proof of Claim 4.2.5.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-claim-424\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0093\">Proof of Claim 4.2.4</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0094\">It suffices to lower-bound <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B_i]=\\Pr_{\\mathbf e\\sim \\mathbf E}[e_i\\text{ is bad}]\\)</span> for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0095\">Since a uniformly random <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e=(u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\sim\\mathbf E\\)</span> is a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> random walk on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e\\)</span> is generated by the following process:</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0096\">1. Choose an edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim E\\)</span> uniformly at random.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0097\">2. Perform a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\((i-1)\\)</span> random walk starting at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1}\\)</span>, and label the vertices traversed, in order, as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1},u_{i-2},\\dots,u_0\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0098\">3. Similarly, perform a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\ell-i)\\)</span> random walk starting at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i\\)</span>, and label the vertices traversed, in order, as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i,u_{i+1},\\dots,u_\\ell\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0099\">4. Output the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e \\in \\mathbf E\\)</span> corresponding to the vertex sequence <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/randomwalk_process.png\" alt=\"Illustration of the random walk\" /></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0100\">Figure 4.2: Illustration of the random walk.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0101\">Perform a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\((i-1)\\)</span> random walk starting at vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0\\)</span>, and label the vertices traversed, in order, as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1},u_{i-2},\\dots,u_0\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0102\">Similarly, perform a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\ell-i)\\)</span> random walk starting at vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i\\)</span>, and label the vertices traversed, in order, as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_i,u_{i+1},\\dots,u_\\ell\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0103\">Combine these two random walks to generate a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> random walk.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0104\">We evaluate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[B_i=1]\\)</span> using this process.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0105\">First, the probability that the edge chosen in step 1 satisfies <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0106\">Next, conditioned on the edge chosen in step 1 satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span>, steps 2 and 3 are trials based on independent randomness, so their respective endpoints <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0,u_\\ell\\)</span> are independent random variables.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0107\">Therefore,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-Pr-B_i\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\Pr[B_i=1] \\\\\n&amp;\\quad = \\mathbb E_{\\text{step 1-3}}\\left[\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F \\text{ and }a&#39;(u_0)_{u_{i-1}} = a(u_{i-1})\\text{ and }a&#39;(u_\\ell)_{u_i} = a(u_i)\\right] \\\\\n&amp;\\quad = \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\cdot \\mathbb E_{\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim F}\\left[ \\underbrace{\\Pr_{\\text{step 2}}[a&#39;(u_0)_{u_{i-1}} = a(u_{i-1})]}_{=:p_0} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\Pr_{\\text{step 3}}[a&#39;(u_\\ell)_{u_i} = a(u_i)]}_{=:p_\\ell}\\right]\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.4}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0108\">We obtain this equality.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0109\">In the second equality, note that choosing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim E\\)</span> at random conditioned on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span> is exactly the same as choosing an edge of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\)</span> uniformly at random.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0110\">Next, fix any edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span> and evaluate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_0\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_\\ell\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0111\">Fix the vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1}\\)</span> arbitrarily and consider <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_0\\)</span> (the same argument applies to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_\\ell\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0112\">For a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> and a nonnegative integer <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\ge 0\\)</span>, let the random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,m}\\in\\Sigma\\)</span> be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}_m(u)\\)</span>'s opinion about <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> (under <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>), namely <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,m} = a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_m(u))_u\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0113\">By (4.1), when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m=\\ell/2\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-center_opinion\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X_{u_{i-1},\\ell/2} = a(u_{i-1})] \\ge \\frac{1}{\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert} \\tag{4.5}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0114\">This inequality holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0115\">Since the endpoint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0\\)</span> chosen in step 2 is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_0=\\operatorname{RW}_i(u_{i-1})\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u_{i-1},i}=a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_i(u_{i-1}))_{u_{i-1}} = a&#39;(u_0)_{u_{i-1}}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0116\">Therefore,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-p_0\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\np_0 = \\Pr[X_{u_{i-1},i} = a(u_{i-1})] \\tag{4.6}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0117\">This equality holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0118\">Fix any vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span> and compare the distributions of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,\\ell/2}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X_{u,i}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0119\">Since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert i-\\ell/2\\rvert \\le \\sqrt{\\ell}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0120\">At each vertex of the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, color one of its self-loops red (by assumption, the original graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\((V,E)\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular and every vertex has at least one self-loop).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0121\">Consider a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell/2\\)</span> random walk starting at vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span>, and let the random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_{\\ell/2}\\)</span> be the number of edges traversed by this walk that are <em>not</em> red (define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_i\\)</span> similarly).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0122\">Because each vertex has exactly one red self-loop, the distribution of the random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_{\\ell/2}\\)</span> is independent of the starting vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> and is the binomial distribution <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)\\)</span> (similarly, the distribution of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_i\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(i,1-1/d)\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0123\">Also, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_k(u)\\)</span> be the final vertex reached by a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> random walk starting at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> that does not choose red edges (that is, a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span> random walk on the graph obtained by removing the red edge from every vertex of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0124\">Therefore, for every vertex subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\subseteq V\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-RW_i_distribution\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u)\\in A] &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^{i} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u) \\in A \\mid Y_{i}=k] \\cdot \\Pr[Y_{i}=k] \\\\\n&amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^{i} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_k(u) \\in A] \\cdot \\Pr[Y_{i}=k]\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.7}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0125\">holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0126\">We will prove the following claim later:</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-claim-426\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0127\">Claim 4.2.6</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0128\">There exist a sufficiently large constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1=C_1(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span> and a sufficiently small constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_2=C_2(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span> such that the following holds:</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0129\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K:= \\{k\\in \\mathbb{Z}_{\\ge 0} \\colon \\lvert k-(1-\\frac{1}{d})\\frac{\\ell}{2}\\rvert \\le C_1\\sqrt{\\ell}\\}\\)</span>, then</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0130\">1. <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2} \\notin K] \\le \\frac{1}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0131\">2. For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in K\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Pr[Y_i=k] \\ge C_2\\cdot \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k]\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0132\">Assume Claim 4.2.6 and use its constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1,C_2\\)</span> to prove Claim 4.2.4.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0133\">Take the parameter <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> sufficiently large as a function of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0134\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(K\\subseteq [0,\\ell/2-\\sqrt{\\ell}] \\subseteq [0,i]\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>, so</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-RW_i_distribution_2\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u)\\in A] &amp;= \\sum_{k=0}^{i} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\Pr[Y_{i}=k] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\sum_{k\\in K} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\underbrace{\\Pr[Y_{i}=k]}_{\\ge C_2 \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k]} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge C_2 \\sum_{k \\in K} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge C_2 \\left(\\sum_{0\\le k \\le \\ell/2} \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}&#39;_{k}(u) \\in A] \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k] - \\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}\\notin K]\\right) \\\\\n&amp;\\ge C_2 \\left(\\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{\\ell/2}(u)\\in A] - \\frac{1}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.8}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0135\">We obtain this inequality.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0136\">Applying (4.8) with the vertex subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\subseteq V\\)</span> defined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A = \\{v \\in V \\colon a&#39;(v)_u = a(u)\\}\\)</span> gives</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X_{u,i}=a(u)] = \\Pr[a&#39;(\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u))_u = a(u)] = \\Pr[\\operatorname{RW}_{i}(u)\\in A] \\ge \\frac{C_2}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0137\">and hence, setting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u=u_{i-1}\\)</span>, (4.6) gives</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\np_0 = \\Pr[X_{u_{i-1},i} = a(u_{i-1})]\\ge \\frac{C_2}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0138\">We obtain this bound.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0139\">Similarly, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p_{\\ell}\\ge \\frac{C&#39;_2}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\\)</span> for an appropriate constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C&#39;_2&gt;0\\)</span>, so (4.4) gives, for an appropriate constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_3=C_3(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[B_i=1] \\ge \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\cdot \\mathbb E_{\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\sim F}[p_0 \\cdot p_\\ell] \\ge C_3\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0140\">We obtain this bound.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0141\">Finally, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B=\\sum_{i\\in I}B_i\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B]\\ge C_3\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-claim-426\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0142\">Proof of Claim 4.2.6</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0143\">The first assertion follows from the fact that the distribution of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y_{\\ell/2}\\)</span> is the binomial distribution <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)\\)</span> and from Chebyshev's inequality (Lemma A.1.2) (Exercise 8).</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0144\">We prove the second assertion.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0145\">That is, we compare the two binomial distributions <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(i,1-1/d)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0146\">Fix any constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1&gt;0\\)</span> satisfying the first assertion.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0147\">It suffices to show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_2&gt;0\\)</span> can be chosen appropriately so that the second assertion holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0148\">In general, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(m\\in\\mathbb N\\)</span>, constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(p\\in(0,1)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C&gt;0\\)</span>, and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb N\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert k-mp\\rvert\\le C\\sqrt{m}\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-de-moivre-laplace\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{Bin}(m,p) = k] = \\frac{1 \\pm O_{C,p}(1/m)}{\\sqrt{2\\pi m p(1-p)}} \\exp\\left(-\\frac{(k-mp)^2}{2m p(1-p)}\\right) \\tag{4.9}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0149\">holds (the De Moivre-Laplace theorem).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0150\">In particular, because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert k-mp\\rvert\\le C\\sqrt{m}\\)</span>, the right-hand side can be estimated as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Theta(1/\\sqrt{m})\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0151\">Therefore, there are appropriate constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(D_1,D_2&gt;0\\)</span> such that, when <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> is sufficiently large,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\Pr[Y_i=k]=\\Pr[\\operatorname{Bin}(i,1-1/d)=k] \\ge \\frac{D_1}{\\sqrt{i}}, \\\\\n&amp;\\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2}=k]=\\Pr[\\operatorname{Bin}(\\ell/2,1-1/d)=k] \\le \\frac{D_2}{\\sqrt{\\ell/2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0152\">These inequalities hold.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0153\">The claim follows because <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in I\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-exercise-8-proof-of-the-claim\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0154\">Exercise 8 (Proof of the Claim)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0155\">Prove the first assertion of Claim 4.2.6, namely that there exists a sufficiently large constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_1=C_1(d,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span> such that</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[Y_{\\ell/2} \\notin K] \\le \\frac{1}{2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0156\">holds.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-claim-425\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0157\">Proof of Claim 4.2.5</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0158\">For a random walk <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf e=(u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\sim\\mathbf E\\)</span>, let the random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z_i\\)</span> be <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0\\)</span> otherwise.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0159\">Then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B_i\\le Z_i\\)</span>, so for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Z:=\\sum_{i\\in I} Z_i\\)</span> we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(B\\le Z\\)</span> and, in particular, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[B^2]\\le \\mathbb E[Z^2]\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0160\">We estimate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[Z^2]\\)</span> below.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0161\">First, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[Z_i]=\\Pr[\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F]=\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\)</span> (because the marginal distribution of the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>th edge of a random walk is the uniform distribution on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span>).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0162\">By definition,</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-E-Z2\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[Z^2] &amp;= \\sum_{i,j\\in I} \\mathbb E[Z_i Z_j] = \\mathbb E[Z] + 2 \\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\mathbb E[Z_i Z_j] \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\lvert I\\rvert + 2 \\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\mathbb E[Z_iZ_j].\n\\end{aligned}\\tag{4.10}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0163\">We now upper-bound <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb E[Z_iZ_j]\\)</span> for fixed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i&lt;j\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0164\">That is, for a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> random walk <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_0,\\dots,u_\\ell)\\)</span> on a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander graph, we estimate the probability that both the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>th edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\)</span> and the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(j\\)</span>th edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u_{j-1},u_j\\}\\)</span> belong to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0165\">Letting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V_F:=\\bigcup_{e\\in F} e\\)</span> be the vertex subset incident to the edges in the edge subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)</span>, for the probability concerning the random walk <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_{i-1},\\dots,u_j)\\)</span> we have</span></p>\n<p><a id=\"en-eq-V_F-bound\"></a> <span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[\\{u_{i-1},u_i\\}\\in F \\text{ and } \\{u_{j-1},u_j\\}\\in F] \\le \\Pr[u_{i-1}\\in V_F\\text{ and }u_j\\in V_F] \\tag{4.11}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0166\">This inequality follows.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0167\">Here we consider the probability for a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\((j-i+1)\\)</span> random walk <span class=\"math inline\">\\((u_{i-1},u_i,\\dots,u_{j-1},u_j)\\)</span> that starts from a uniformly random vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u_{i-1}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0168\">This random walk is identical to a length-<span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> random walk on the powered graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^{j-i+1}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0169\">By Lemma 3.2.7, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G^{j-i+1}\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d^{j-i+1}\\)</span>-regular and a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda^{j-i+1}\\)</span>-expander.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0170\">Therefore, by Lemma 3.2.4, the right-hand side of (4.11) is</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr[u_{i-1}\\in V_F\\text{ and }u_j\\in V_F] &amp;= \\frac{W(V_F,V_F)}{nd^{j-i+1}} \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\left(\\frac{\\lvert V_F\\rvert}{n}\\right)^2 + \\lambda^{j-i+1} \\cdot \\frac{\\lvert V_F\\rvert}{n} \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\left(\\frac{2\\lvert F\\rvert}{n}\\right)^2 + \\lambda^{j-i+1} \\cdot \\frac{2\\lvert F\\rvert}{n} \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{d\\lvert F\\rvert^2}{\\lvert E\\rvert^2} + \\frac{\\lambda^{j-i+1}d\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0171\">We obtain this bound.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0172\">Using <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert F\\rvert/\\lvert E\\rvert \\le 1/\\ell\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert I\\rvert \\le 2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\)</span>, there exists a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C=C(d,\\lambda)&gt;0\\)</span> such that</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\mathbb E[Z_iZ_j] &amp;\\le d\\underbrace{\\lvert I\\rvert^2 \\left(\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}\\right)^2}_{\\le 4\\lvert F\\rvert/\\lvert E\\rvert} + \\frac{d\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\sum_{i,j\\in I,i&lt;j} \\lambda^{j-i+1} \\\\\n&amp;\\le 4d \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} + d\\lvert I\\rvert\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\cdot \\sum_{i\\ge 0} \\lambda^i \\\\\n&amp;\\le C\\lvert I\\rvert\\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0173\">Combining this with (4.10) proves the claim.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"en-43-alphabet-reduction\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0001\">4.3 Alphabet Reduction</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0002\">The gap amplification lemma (Lemma 4.1.1) states that the alphabets of the constraint graphs before and after the transformation are the same, but taking a power amplifies the value of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> by a factor of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sqrt{\\ell}\\)</span> while making the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^\\ell\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0003\">We therefore need to transform a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> over the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma^\\ell\\)</span> into a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> over the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span> without losing too much in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-theorem-431-alphabet-reduction\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0004\">Theorem 4.3.1 (Alphabet Reduction)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0005\">There exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma&gt;0\\)</span>, an alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0\\)</span>, and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> such that the following holds: algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> takes a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,C\\rangle\\)</span> as input and outputs another constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,C&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0006\">For some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> satisfies the following.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0007\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c\\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0008\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0009\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\gamma\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0010\">In the proof of Theorem 4.3.1, we first show that there exist an alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_1\\)</span>, a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span>, and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span> that transforms a given constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span> into a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=\\langle(X,\\Sigma_1,\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\rangle\\)</span> over the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_1\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0011\">The proof is then completed by using Lemma 3.1.9 to transform this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(k\\)</span>-CSP into a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span> over <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0:=\\Sigma_1^k\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-432\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0012\">Lemma 4.3.2</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0013\">There exist a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> and a polynomial-time algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span> such that the following holds: for every alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span>, there is a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>, and algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A&#39;\\)</span> takes a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,C\\rangle\\)</span> as input and outputs a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=(X,\\{0,1\\},\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0014\">The instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> satisfies the following.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0015\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert X\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0016\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0017\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi) \\ge 0.99\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-18\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0018\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0019\">Let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b = \\lceil\\log_2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\rceil\\)</span> and identify the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma\\)</span> with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^b\\)</span> (if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span> is not a power of two, add dummy values so that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert=\\lvert\\{0,1\\}^b\\rvert\\)</span> and make every constraint reject whenever it reads a dummy value).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0020\">Identify the constraint <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_e\\subseteq \\Sigma^2\\)</span> corresponding to each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span> of the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> with its indicator function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c_e\\colon\\Sigma^2\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span>, and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e\\colon \\{0,1\\}^b\\times \\{0,1\\}^b\\to \\{0,1\\}\\)</span> be its representation as a two-input Boolean circuit (since any Boolean function <span class=\"math inline\">\\(f\\colon\\{0,1\\}^\\ell\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> can be represented by a Boolean circuit of size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\ell2^\\ell)\\)</span>, this circuit has size at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^{O(b)}\\)</span>, and since <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b=\\log_2\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span> is treated as a constant, its size is also constant).</span></p>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/newCSP.png\" alt=\"The constraint on each edge of the 2-CSP represented as a Boolean circuit\" /></p>\n<p><em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0021\">Figure 4.3: The constraint on each edge of the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2\\)</span>-CSP represented as a Boolean circuit.</span></em></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0022\">For each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>, apply Theorem 2.3.1 to the resulting circuit <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0023\">For the verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e)\\)</span> obtained from that theorem, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span> denote the deterministic verifier obtained by fixing the random seed <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\operatorname{poly}(b)}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0024\">Assume that the running time of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V\\)</span> is at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{poly}(b)\\le T\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0025\">It has the following properties.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0026\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u=\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_v=\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v)\\)</span> for <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)=1\\)</span>, then there exists <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span> accepts for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0027\">For every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\)</span> satisfying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(C_e(\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)=1\\)</span>, suppose that</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u))\\ge0.01 \\text{ or }\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi_v,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v))\\ge0.01.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0028\">Then for every such <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u,\\pi_v\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e \\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr_r\\left[V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\text{ rejects}\\right]\\ge 0.99.\n\\]</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0029\">There is a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q=O(1)\\)</span> such that the verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e)\\)</span> makes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span> oracle accesses.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0030\">The CSP ultimately constructed will be a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"assets/figures/newCSP2.png\" alt=\"The q_0-CSP to be constructed\" /></p>\n<p><em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0031\">Figure 4.4: The <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP to be constructed.</span></em> <em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0032\">Each component of an oracle accessed by the PCPP verifier, viewed as a string, corresponds to a variable.</span></em> <em><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0033\">In fact, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>, we enumerate <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span> and add all of them as constraints.</span></em></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0034\">Construct the new <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> that is ultimately output as follows.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0035\">For each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> and each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in [2^b]\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{u,i}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> be a variable, and set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u=(\\pi_{u,i})_{i\\in[2^b]}\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^b}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0036\">Similarly, for each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E\\)</span> and each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(j\\in [2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}]\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_{e,j}\\in\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span> be a variable, and set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e=(\\pi_{e,j})_{j\\in[2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}]}\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0037\">The full variable set is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X=\\{\\pi_{u,i}\\colon u\\in V,i\\in[2^b]\\} \\cup \\{\\pi_{e,j}\\colon e\\in E,j\\in[2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}]\\}\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0038\">For each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span> and each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^{T}\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e,r}\\)</span> be the constraint “<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)=1\\)</span>,” and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\)</span> collect <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;_{e,r}\\)</span> over all <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in E,r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0039\">This verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span> runs with the variables <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e\\)</span> as oracles.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0040\">Because the verifier makes at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span> oracle accesses, each constraint “<span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)=1\\)</span>” depends on the values of at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span> variables.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0041\">Thus, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0042\">We now verify that this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> has the desired properties.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"en-size-of-varphi\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0043\">Size of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span></span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0044\">The number of vertices (variables) is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^b\\cdot\\lvert V\\rvert + \\lvert E\\rvert\\cdot(2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}) \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>, and the number of constraints is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^T\\cdot\\lvert E\\rvert \\le 2^{\\operatorname{poly}(b)}\\cdot\\lvert E\\rvert\\)</span> (because the verifier's running time is polynomially bounded in <span class=\"math inline\">\\(b\\)</span>, we have <span class=\"math inline\">\\(T\\le\\operatorname{poly}(b)\\)</span>).</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"en-existence-of-a-satisfying-assignment\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0045\">Existence of a Satisfying Assignment</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0046\">Suppose there exists an assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a\\colon V\\to\\Sigma\\)</span> satisfying all constraints of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0047\">For each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>, set</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\pi_u = \\operatorname{Had}_b(a(u)).\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0048\">This defines <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0049\">Then, for each edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a(u),a(v))\\)</span> satisfies the constraint on edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0050\">Therefore, by Theorem 2.3.1, there exists <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_e\\in \\mathbb{F}_2^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}}\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\)</span> accepts for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(r\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0051\">The assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon X\\to \\{0,1\\}\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> determined by the resulting <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\pi_u)_{u\\in V}\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\pi_e)_{e\\in E}\\)</span> satisfies every constraint of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<h5 id=\"en-property-of-the-unsatisfiability-value\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0052\">Property of the Unsatisfiability Value</span></h5>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0053\">We show that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\ge 0.99\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0054\">Fix any assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\colon X\\to\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0055\">For each vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span>, let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi&#39;_u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{b}\\)</span> be the value of the partial assignment to the variables <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u\\)</span> determined by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a&#39;\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0056\">For each vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\in V\\)</span> of the original constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>, define <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\in\\Sigma\\)</span> by “decoding <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi&#39;_u\\)</span>,” as follows.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0057\">If there exists <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\in\\Sigma\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma)) \\le 0.01\\)</span>, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)=\\sigma\\)</span> (Exercise 4 guarantees that such a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\sigma\\)</span>, if it exists, is unique).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0058\">Otherwise, choose <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)\\in\\Sigma\\)</span> arbitrarily.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0059\">Let the edge subset <span class=\"math inline\">\\(F\\subseteq E\\)</span> be the set of edges contributing to <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0060\">That is, it is the set of edges <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span> for which <span class=\"math inline\">\\((a(u),a(v))\\)</span> does not satisfy the constraint on edge <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0061\">If there exists <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)\\in c_e\\)</span> such that, for any <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in E\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u))\\le0.01\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_v,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v))\\le0.01\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(u)=\\sigma_u\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(a(v)=\\sigma_v\\)</span>, so <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\notin F\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0062\">Taking the contrapositive, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e=\\{u,v\\}\\in F\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\((\\sigma_u,\\sigma_v)\\in c_e\\)</span>, either <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_u,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_u))&gt;0.01\\)</span> or <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{dist}(\\pi&#39;_v,\\operatorname{Had}_b(\\sigma_v))&gt;0.01\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0063\">Therefore, by the property of verifier <span class=\"math inline\">\\(V(C_e)\\)</span> (Theorem 2.3.1), for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in F\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\forall \\pi&#39;_e \\in \\{0,1\\}^{2^{2b} + 2^{4b^2}},\\quad \\Pr_{r\\sim\\{0,1\\}^T}\\left[ V^{\\pi&#39;_u,\\pi&#39;_v,\\pi&#39;_e}(C_e;r)\\text{ rejects} \\right] \\ge 0.99.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0064\">This inequality holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0065\">That is, among the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(2^T\\)</span> constraints associated with <span class=\"math inline\">\\(e\\in F\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(0.99\\cdot 2^T\\)</span> are not satisfied by the assignment <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\pi_u\\leftarrow \\pi&#39;_u,\\,\\pi_v\\leftarrow \\pi&#39;_v,\\,\\pi_e\\leftarrow \\pi&#39;_e\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0066\">Therefore,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(a&#39;;\\varphi) &amp;= \\Pr_{e\\sim E,r\\sim\\{0,1\\}^T}\\left[ V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\text{ rejects} \\right] \\\\\n&amp;= \\Pr_{e\\sim E,r\\sim\\{0,1\\}^T}\\left[ V^{\\pi_u,\\pi_v,\\pi_e}(C_e;r)\\text{ rejects} \\mid e\\in F \\right]\\cdot\\Pr[e\\in F] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge 0.99\\cdot \\frac{\\lvert F\\rvert}{\\lvert E\\rvert} \\\\\n&amp;= 0.99\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(a;G)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0067\">holds.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0068\">Finally, use Lemma 3.1.9 to transform the <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP into a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\Sigma_0,\\mathcal{C}&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>, completing the proof.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-theorem-431\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0069\">Proof of Theorem 4.3.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0070\">Given the input constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G=\\langle(V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}\\rangle\\)</span>, use Lemma 4.3.2 to construct an instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi=\\langle(X,\\{0,1\\},\\mathcal{I},\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\rangle\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP over the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}\\)</span> (this can be done in polynomial time).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0071\">Then use Lemma 3.1.9 to transform this <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> into a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;=\\langle(V&#39;,E&#39;),\\{0,1\\}^{q_0},\\mathcal{C}&#39;&#39;\\rangle\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0072\">Algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> outputs the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> obtained in this way.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0073\">We verify below that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> has the desired properties.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0074\">First, by Lemma 4.3.2, there exists a constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> satisfies the following.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0075\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert X\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert \\le c\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0076\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0077\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi) \\ge 0.99\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0078\">Then, by Lemma 3.1.9, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G&#39;\\)</span> is a constraint graph over the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{0,1\\}^{q_0}\\)</span> and, for some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&#39;\\)</span> depending only on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q_0\\)</span>, satisfies the following.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0079\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c&#39;q_0\\cdot (\\lvert X\\rvert + \\lvert\\mathcal{I}\\rvert)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0080\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0081\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\frac{1}{q_0}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0082\">Combining these gives the following.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0083\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G&#39;) \\le c\\cdot c&#39;q_0\\cdot\\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0084\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0 \\Rightarrow \\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi)=0\\Rightarrow \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;)=0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0085\"><span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G&#39;) \\ge \\frac{1}{q_0}\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi) \\ge \\frac{0.99}{q_0}\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"en-44-proof-of-the-gap-amplification-lemma-and-the-pcp-theorem\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0086\">4.4 Proof of the Gap Amplification Lemma and the PCP Theorem</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0087\">The gap amplification lemma (Lemma 4.1.1) is obtained by amplifying <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\)</span> through powering and then using Theorem 4.3.1 to reduce the alphabet enlarged by that amplification.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-lemma-411\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0088\">Proof of Lemma 4.1.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0089\">Apply the following to the input constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0090\">Run the algorithm of Lemma 3.3.1 on the given constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G\\)</span> and denote its output by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1\\)</span> (expanderization).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0091\">For a parameter <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\in\\mathbb{N}\\)</span> to be chosen later, set <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_2 = G_1^\\ell\\)</span> (powering).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0092\">Run the algorithm of Theorem 4.3.1 on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_2\\)</span>, output its result <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_3\\)</span>, and halt (alphabet reduction).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0093\">Let the universal constants <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d,\\lambda\\)</span> be the constants from Lemma 3.3.1.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0094\">Choose the powering parameter <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span>, which depends only on <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert\\)</span>, to be sufficiently large.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0095\">By Lemma 3.3.1, Lemma 4.2.2, and Theorem 4.3.1, the following holds.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0096\">For a suitable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c=c(d,\\lambda,\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert,\\ell)\\)</span>, the final constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_3\\)</span> has size <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G_3) \\le c \\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G)\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0097\">If <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3) = \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_2) = \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1) = 0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0098\">Finally, we show that by choosing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell\\)</span> appropriately, for the constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha:=\\lceil2/\\ell\\rceil\\)</span> we can obtain <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3)\\ge \\min\\{ \\alpha, 2\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G) \\}\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0099\">First, by Lemma 3.3.1, the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_2\\)</span> is a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(d\\)</span>-regular <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lambda\\)</span>-expander, and for some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta_1\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1)\\ge \\beta_1\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G)\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0100\">Therefore, by Lemma 4.2.2, for some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\beta_2=\\beta_2(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_2) &amp;\\ge \\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_1), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\beta_1\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\} \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\beta_1\\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0101\">This inequality holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0102\">Finally, by Theorem 4.3.1, for some constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma=\\gamma(\\lvert\\Sigma\\rvert)&gt;0\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3) &amp;\\ge \\gamma\\cdot \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_2) \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\gamma\\beta_1\\beta_2\\sqrt{\\ell}\\cdot \\min\\left\\{ \\operatorname{UNSAT}(G), \\frac{1}{\\ell} \\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0103\">This inequality holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0104\">At this point, setting <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\ell = \\left\\lceil \\left(\\frac{2}{\\gamma\\beta_1\\beta_2}\\right)^2 \\right\\rceil\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\alpha = \\frac{1}{\\ell}\\)</span> gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_3)\\ge \\min\\{ \\alpha, 2\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G) \\}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0105\">All preparations for proving the PCP theorem are now complete.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-of-theorem-127\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0106\">Proof of Theorem 1.2.7</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0107\">If we prove Theorem 1.2.14, combining it with the NP-completeness of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> yields Theorem 1.2.7 (Exercise 2).</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0108\">Also, by Lemma 3.1.6, Theorem 1.2.14 and Theorem 3.1.4 are equivalent, so from this point onward we prove Theorem 3.1.4.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0109\">That is, for a given instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>, we construct in polynomial time a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(q\\)</span>-CSP instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi\\)</span> satisfying the conditions of Theorem 3.1.4.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0110\">It is constructed by the following algorithm.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-algorithm-441\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0111\">Algorithm 4.4.1</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0112\">Define algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> as follows.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0113\">Its input is an instance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0=(f_k)\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span>.</span></p>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0114\">Regard <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\varphi_0\\)</span> as a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(3\\)</span>-CSP and use Lemma 3.1.9 to transform it into a constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span>.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0115\">For each <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i=1,\\dots,\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil\\)</span>, run the algorithm of Lemma 4.1.1 on the constraint graph <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_i\\)</span> and let <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_{i+1}\\)</span> be the graph it outputs.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0116\">Output <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_{\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil}\\)</span> and halt.</span></li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0117\">Since the original problem <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\text{3-QuadEq}\\)</span> is a 3-CSP over the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2\\)</span>, the alphabet of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_0\\)</span> is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{F}_2^3\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0118\">The alphabet of the final constraint graph is the alphabet <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Sigma_0\\)</span> from Theorem 4.3.1.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0119\">Fix these alphabets as universal alphabets.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0120\">Then there exists a suitable constant <span class=\"math inline\">\\(c&gt;0\\)</span> such that <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{size}(G_i) \\le c \\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G_{i-1})\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i=1,\\dots,\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil\\)</span>, and hence the size of the final constraint graph satisfies</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{size}(G_{\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil}) \\le c^{O(\\log s)} \\cdot \\operatorname{size}(G_0) = s^{O(1)}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0121\">This bound holds.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0122\">Also, by Lemma 3.1.9 and Lemma 4.1.1, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)=0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_i)=0\\)</span> for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>.</span> <span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0123\">Finally, if <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)&gt;0\\)</span>, then <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(\\varphi_0)\\ge \\frac{1}{s}\\)</span>; Lemma 3.1.9 gives <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_0)\\ge \\frac{1}{3s}\\)</span>; and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_i) \\ge \\min\\{ 2\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_{i-1}), \\alpha \\}\\)</span> holds for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span>, so</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\operatorname{UNSAT}\\left(G_{\\lceil\\log_2 (3s)\\rceil}\\right) \\ge \\min\\left\\{ \\alpha, 2^{\\log_2 (3s)}\\cdot\\operatorname{UNSAT}(G_0) \\right\\} \\ge \\alpha.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0124\">This completes the bound.</span></p>\n<h1 id=\"en-bibliography\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0125\">Bibliography</span></h1>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0126\">[AKS04] M. Agrawal, N. Kayal, and N. Saxena. “PRIMES is in P” en. In: <em>Annals of mathematics</em> 160 (2 Sept. 1, 2004), pp. 781-793. doi: 10.4007/annals.2004.160.781 (cit. on p. 13).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0127\">[ALMSS98] S. Arora, C. Lund, R. Motwani, M. Sudan, and M. Szegedy. “Proof verification and the hardness of approximation problems” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 45 (3 May 1, 1998), pp. 501-555. doi: 10.1145/278298.278306 (cit. on p. 7).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0128\">[AS98] S. Arora and S. Safra. “Probabilistic checking of proofs: a new characterization of NP” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 45 (1 Jan. 1, 1998), pp. 70-122. doi: 10.1145/273865.273901 (cit. on p. 7).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0129\">[Bab85] L. Babai. “Trading group theory for randomness” In: <em>Proceedings of the seventeenth annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing - STOC '85</em>. The seventeenth annual ACM symposium (Providence, Rhode Island, United States). New York, New York, USA: ACM Press, 1985, pp. 421-429. doi: 10.1145/22145.22192 (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0130\">[BLR93] M. Blum, M. Luby, and R. Rubinfeld. “Self-testing/correcting with applications to numerical problems” In: <em>Journal of Computer and System Sciences</em> 47 (3 1993), pp. 549-595. doi: 10.1016/0022-0000(93)90044-W (cit. on p. 29).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0131\">[Coo71] S. A. Cook. “The complexity of theorem-proving procedures” en. In: <em>Proceedings of the third annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing - STOC '71</em>. The third annual ACM symposium (Shaker Heights, Ohio, United States). New York, New York, USA: ACM Press, 1971. doi: 10.1145/800157.805047 (cit. on p. 17).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0132\">[Din07] I. Dinur. “The PCP theorem by gap amplification” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 54 (3 June 1, 2007), 12-es. doi: 10.1145/1236457.1236459 (cit. on p. 7).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0133\">[GMR85] S. Goldwasser, S. Micali, and C. Rackoff. “The knowledge complexity of interactive proof-systems” In: <em>Proceedings of the seventeenth annual ACM symposium on Theory of computing - STOC '85</em>. The seventeenth annual ACM symposium (Providence, Rhode Island, United States). New York, New York, USA: ACM Press, 1985, pp. 291-304. doi: 10.1145/22145.22178 (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0134\">[GMW86] O. Goldreich, S. Micali, and A. Wigderson. “Proofs that yield nothing but their validity and a methodology of cryptographic protocol design” en. In: <em>27th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (sfcs 1986)</em>. 27th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (sfcs 1986) (Toronto, ON, Canada). IEEE, Oct. 1986, pp. 174-187. doi: 10.1109/sfcs.1986.47 (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0135\">[Gol10] O. Goldreich. “Short Locally Testable Codes and Proofs: A Survey in Two Parts” In: <em>Property Testing: Current Research and Surveys</em>. Ed. by O. Goldreich. Berlin, Heidelberg: Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 2010, pp. 65-104. doi: 10.1007/978-3-642-16367-8_6 (cit. on p. 30).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0136\">[GS06] O. Goldreich and M. Sudan. “Locally testable codes and PCPs of almost-linear length” In: <em>Journal of the ACM</em> 53 (4 July 1, 2006), pp. 558-655. doi: 10.1145/1162349.1162351 (cit. on p. 29).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0137\">[Kar72] R. M. Karp. “Reducibility among Combinatorial Problems” en. In: <em>Complexity of Computer Computations</em>. Boston, MA: Springer US, 1972, pp. 85-103. doi: 10.1007/978-1-4684-2001-2_9 (cit. on pp. 17, 21).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0138\">[Lev73] L. A. Levin. “Universal Sequential Search Problems” In: <em>Problems of Information Transmission (translated from Problemy Peredachi Informatsii (Russian))</em> 9 (3 1973), pp. 115-116 (cit. on p. 17).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0139\">[LFKN02] C. Lund, L. Fortnow, H. Karloff, and N. Nisan. “Algebraic methods for interactive proof systems” In: <em>Proceedings [1990] 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science</em>. [1990] 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (St. Louis, MO, USA). IEEE Comput. Soc. Press, 2002, 2-10 vol. 1. doi: 10.1109/fscs.1990.89518 (cit. on p. 23).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0140\">[ODo] R. O'Donnell. <em>A history of the PCP Theorem</em>. url: <a href=\"https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse533/05au/pcp-history.pdf\">https://courses.cs.washington.edu/courses/cse533/05au/pcp-history.pdf</a> (visited on 05/28/2025) (cit. on p. 22).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0141\">[RVW02] O. Reingold, S. Vadhan, and A. Wigderson. “Entropy Waves, the Zig-Zag Graph Product, and New Constant-Degree Expanders” In: <em>Annals of mathematics</em> 155 (1 Jan. 2002), p. 157. doi: 10.2307/3062153 (cit. on p. 59).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0142\">[Sha02] A. Shamir. “IP=PSPACE (interactive proof=polynomial space)” In: <em>Proceedings [1990] 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science</em>. 31st Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (St. Louis, MO, USA). IEEE Comput. Soc. Press, 2002, 11-15 vol. 1. doi: 10.1109/fscs.1990.89519 (cit. on p. 23).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h1 id=\"en-appendix-a-appendix\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0143\">Appendix A Appendix</span></h1>\n<h2 id=\"en-a1-basic-probability-inequalities\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0144\">A.1 Basic Probability Inequalities</span></h2>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0145\">This section states and proves the probability inequalities used in the proofs.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-a11-markovs-inequality\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0146\">Lemma A.1.1 (Markov's Inequality)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0147\">For every nonnegative random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span> and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t&gt;0\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X\\ge t] \\le \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[X]}{t}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0148\">This inequality holds.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-19\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0149\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0150\">For simplicity, assume here that the support <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\Omega=\\operatorname{supp}(X)\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}\\)</span> of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span> is finite, that is, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\lvert\\Omega\\rvert&lt;\\infty\\)</span>.<a href=\"#en-fn13\" class=\"footnote-ref\" id=\"en-fnref13\" role=\"doc-noteref\"><sup>13</sup></a></span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0152\">Then,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[X] &amp;= \\sum_{x\\in \\Omega} x\\Pr[X=x] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\sum_{x\\in \\Omega,x\\ge t} x\\Pr[X=x] \\\\\n&amp;\\ge \\sum_{x\\in \\Omega,x\\ge t} t\\Pr[X=x] \\\\\n&amp;= t\\Pr[X\\ge t].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0153\">Rearranging gives the claim.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-a12-chebyshevs-inequality\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0154\">Lemma A.1.2 (Chebyshev's Inequality)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0155\">For every random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span> whose expectation <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbb{E}[X]\\)</span> and variance <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\mathbf{Var}[X]\\)</span> exist, and every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(t&gt;0\\)</span>,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[|X-\\mathbb{E}[X]|\\ge t] \\le \\frac{\\mathbf{Var}[X]}{t^2}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0156\">This inequality holds.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-20\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0157\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0158\">The random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(Y=(X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2\\)</span> is nonnegative, so applying Markov's inequality to it gives</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr\\left[ \\lvert X - \\mathbb{E}[X]\\rvert \\ge t  \\right] &amp;= \\Pr\\left[ (X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2 \\ge t^2 \\right] \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\frac{\\mathbb{E}\\left[ (X-\\mathbb{E}[X])^2 \\right]}{t^2} \\\\\n&amp;= \\frac{\\mathbf{Var}[X]}{t^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0159\">This proves the inequality.</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-lemma-a13-paley-zygmund-inequality\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0160\">Lemma A.1.3 (Paley-Zygmund Inequality)</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0161\">For every random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span> taking nonnegative integer values,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\Pr[X\\ge 1] \\ge \\frac{\\mathbb{E}[X]^2}{\\mathbb{E}[X^2]}.\n\\]</span></p>\n<h4 id=\"en-proof-21\"><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0162\">Proof</span></h4>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0163\">For a random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span> taking nonnegative integer values,</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"math display\">\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[X] &amp;= \\mathbb{E}[X\\cdot \\mathbf{1}_{X\\ge 1}] \\\\\n&amp;\\le \\sqrt{ \\mathbb{E}[X^2]\\cdot \\mathbb{E}[\\mathbf{1}_{X\\ge 1}] } &amp;&amp; \\text{by the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality}\\\\\n&amp;= \\sqrt{ \\mathbb{E}[X^2]\\cdot \\Pr[X\\ge 1] }.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</span></p>\n<p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0164\">Squaring both sides and rearranging gives the claim.</span></p>\n<section id=\"en-footnotes\" class=\"footnotes footnotes-end-of-document\" role=\"doc-endnotes\">\n<hr />\n<ol>\n<li id=\"en-fn1\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0021\">For now, it suffices to think of a program implemented in Python, C, or a similar language.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref1\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn2\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0044\">The definition of an individual computational step depends on the computational model, but one may essentially think of one-bit operations and reads from or writes to memory.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref2\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn3\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0084\">Because specifying a natural number <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\in[\\lvert\\pi\\rvert]\\)</span> requires fixing <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log \\lvert\\pi\\rvert)\\)</span> bits, each query takes <span class=\"math inline\">\\(O(\\log \\lvert\\pi\\rvert)\\)</span> time in the worst case.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref3\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn4\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p01-0137\">Such a <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\gamma\\)</span> is called a primitive root.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref4\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn5\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0005\">Depending on the context, NP-hardness may also be naturally defined for problems that are not decision problems, such as optimization and counting problems, and this lecture sometimes says that \"optimization problem X is NP-hard\" when presenting applications of the PCP theorem.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref5\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn6\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0115\">Two graphs <span class=\"math inline\">\\(G_1=(V_1,E_1),G_2=(V_2,E_2)\\)</span> are isomorphic if there exists a bijection <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\phi\\colon V_1\\to V_2\\)</span> such that, for every <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in V_1\\)</span>, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in E_1\\)</span> and <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u,v\\in E_2\\)</span> are equivalent.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref6\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn7\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p02-0128\">This idea proves only the claim that the socks can be distinguished and does not require conveying information about the color of either sock to the prover.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref7\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn8\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p03-fn-0001\">Since algorithm <span class=\"math inline\">\\(A\\)</span> is given <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1^n\\)</span> as input, being polynomial-time means that its computational complexity is bounded by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\)</span>.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref8\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn9\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0099\">Strictly speaking, the brackets used in the notation for a constraint graph should formally be written as <span class=\"math inline\">\\(((V,E),\\Sigma,\\mathcal{C}&#39;)\\)</span>, but for readability we deliberately use <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\langle\\cdot\\rangle\\)</span> for the outer brackets.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref9\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn10\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p05-0103\">More specifically, <span class=\"math inline\">\\(E\\)</span> is a multiset, and an edge corresponding to a self-loop is written <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{u\\}\\)</span>.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref10\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn11\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p06-0010\">Note that the contribution of a self-loop to the degree is <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span> (in some contexts, this contribution is also <span class=\"math inline\">\\(1\\)</span>).</span><a href=\"#en-fnref11\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn12\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p08-0026\">A vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span> is at distance at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span> from a vertex <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> if there exists a path of length at most <span class=\"math inline\">\\(i\\)</span> that starts at <span class=\"math inline\">\\(u\\)</span> and reaches <span class=\"math inline\">\\(v\\)</span>.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref12\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n<li id=\"en-fn13\"><p><span class=\"segment\" data-seg=\"p09-0151\">The set of possible values of a random variable <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span>, namely <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\{x\\colon \\Pr[X=x]&gt;0\\}\\)</span>, is called the support of <span class=\"math inline\">\\(X\\)</span> and is denoted by <span class=\"math inline\">\\(\\operatorname{supp}(X)\\)</span>.</span><a href=\"#en-fnref13\" class=\"footnote-back\" role=\"doc-backlink\">↩︎</a></p></li>\n</ol>\n</section>","segmentCount":1465,"generatedAt":"2026-08-23T03:50:34.125109+00:00"};
